# whitefoot conformance manifest — one JSON object per case (blank / #-lines ignored).
# id -> cases/<id>.wf, or a module-form case directory cases/<id>/ ; rules[] -> spec rule ids exercised ; expect -> required verdict.
# status: runnable (must match); there is no skipped or expected-failure status.
{"id":"fn8-pos-affine-requirement-images","rules":["FN-8","ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2","MSR-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An admitted non-L0 ordering requirement supplies its captured affine image. The body uses the operands in reversed order and subtracts their sum; the concrete call returns three. The function binder is renamed from room, which FORM-3 now reserves from every declaration role."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-affine-requirement-measures","rules":["FN-8","ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2","MSR-4","REF-4","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two length observations are place forms rather than calls, so the affine requirement constrains their images directly; their sum bounds one write into the caller-provided output and the executable checks the last element. The whole-output write subsumes its descriptor read, while the two input measure reads remain explicit."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-affine-requirement-signed-leaves","rules":["FN-8","ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2","MSR-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Body-entry signed decomposition establishes both negative disjuncts. The affine image of not(sum > limit) supplies sum <= limit; this case exercises body publication, not an expansion of caller Boolean proof routes. The function binder is renamed from room, which FORM-3 now reserves from every declaration role."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-affine-requirement-disjunct","rules":["FN-8","ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A positive disjunction does not establish either child. With both operands at 16 and a zero limit its second disjunct holds while subtraction underflows; no affine image may be invented for the first child."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-affine-requirement-scalar-replaced","rules":["FN-8","ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An affine requirement names entry values. Replacing a cannot retarget its sum bound; old a and b at zero with a zero limit satisfy the contract but the new subtraction underflows."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-affine-requirement-measure-replaced","rules":["FN-8","ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the whole measured binding kills the current length image. A new length read cannot inherit the former length-plus-extra bound, which named the body-entry image; an empty input with zero extra and limit followed by a one-element replacement is a counterexample."}
{"id":"op2-pos-runtime-division-images","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","ENT-6","FN-9","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Unsigned exact division by a runtime divisor publishes quotient <= dividend without a later product. An independently admitted exact product of the captured quotient and divisor, in either operand order and under let or set transfer, publishes product <= dividend. The calls compute floor(17 / 3) as 5 and its checked product as 15."}
{"id":"op2-pos-runtime-division-aliases","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","ENT-5","INV-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Copies retain the captured quotient, divisor and dividend value images after their original bindings are replaced. The product bound names the saved dividend, and the program observes the old values' product as 15."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-runtime-division-divisor-replaced","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-5","INV-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The independently admitted product uses a replacement divisor. It is not the captured division product, so the old theorem cannot prove the invariant; count=17 and original divisor=3 would give 320 > 17."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-runtime-division-quotient-replaced","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-5","INV-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One branch replaces the quotient by the dividend. The joined value cannot inherit the original quotient-product theorem. With the branch taken, count=17 and divisor=3 give 51 > 17."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-runtime-division-dividend-replaced","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-5","INV-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The product uses the captured quotient and divisor, but its bound refers to the original dividend value. Replacing that dividend by zero cannot retarget the bound; an original count of 17 and divisor of 3 give 15 > 0."}
{"id":"op2-neg-runtime-division-product-domain","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A product consequence is available only after its own ordinary domain discharges. With full-width operands that domain is outside the admitted automatic family here; its later quotient-product image cannot circularly authorize the multiplication itself."}
{"id":"run-ex1-value-match","rules":["GRAM-10","GRAM-11","GIVE-1","ERR-1","OP-1","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"EX-1-class program: let-init give value-match, a reference read through deref, named binders, named user-fn call, checked arithmetic; runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"run-invariant-exact-sum","rules":["OP-1","OP-2","INV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A counted loop supplies its index range and a direct affine invariant bounds the accumulated sum. Together they prove every exact addition before execution; explicit result controls observe 0 through 4 as 10 and its checked double as 20."}
{"id":"inv1-pos-automatic-two-premise-backedge","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"After four assignments replace every value named by the counted-loop header invariant, two independent entry requirements restore the combined affine relation. AUTO checks every coefficient-one premise pair, so the arbitrary backedge is proved without a local certificate."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-base-unproved","rules":["INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A zero-trip counted range still reaches its first loop header, so INV-1 must prove the invariant base case without assuming the same invariant. The false relation 1 <= 0 is rejected before lowering."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-backedge-unproved","rules":["INV-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The base case holds, but an arbitrary normal body fallthrough writes sum = 2 before the next header. INV-1 rejects the unproved backedge rather than checking one selected iteration or retaining a runtime test."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-repeated-name","rules":["INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two invariants in one counted-loop header use the same proof-only name. INV-1 rejects the second declaration deterministically in the separate invariant domain; invariant names create neither runtime bindings nor an overloaded lookup."}
{"id":"inv1-pos-ordinary-loop-guarded-cursor","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary loop header bounds a cursor the body increments under a source guard. The writer publishes the guard as a body invariant on the reaching path while its premises are still live; that affine conclusion over the pre-write value images survives the SET-1 target kill and discharges the backedge on its own, alongside the automatic commit-value route the neighbouring plain-set case isolates."}
{"id":"inv1-pos-ordinary-loop-cursor-set-image","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-5","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same ordinary loop header bounds a cursor the body increments with a plain set. The commit evaluates its right-hand side to that occurrence's commit value before the SET-1 target kill, so the pre-kill closure carries the guarded bound onto that value and the post-write equality names it at the backedge; no republished body invariant is needed, and the two spellings of one value agree."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-explicit-affine-proof","rules":["PRF-1","FN-8","FN-9","OP-2","ENT-3","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Three independent component bounds exceed AUTO's two-premise boundary, so a source-written certificate names all three in order. Its sum directly implies the slightly weaker local invariant; that published fact and the aggregate limit prove both exact additions and the callee postcondition, an unrelated assignment does not change its value images, and the caller uses the verified postcondition for one more exact addition."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-explicit-factor","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"AUTO first publishes a two-component bound. A later certificate names that invariant once with the explicit factor three, and PRF-1 checks the scaled sum without repeated uses or multiplier search."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-integer-tightening-midpoint","rules":["PRF-1","INV-1","ENT-6","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A binary-search midpoint. The unsigned literal division publishes the scaled quotient image, and the written certificate sums it with the ordered endpoints into 2*(mid - hi) <= -1. Halving that accumulated sum and taking the mathematical floor of its bound is the one integer consequence PRF-1 admits, and the halved midpoint relation with the verified length requirement discharges the subscript."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-unproved-premise","rules":["PRF-1","FN-8","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The first written use relation is absent from the pre-proof context. PRF-1 rejects that premise in source order; the written conclusion creates neither a fact nor a runtime fallback."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-inexact-combination","rules":["PRF-1","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Both written premises are available, but their coefficient-one sum proves x <= 254 while the target requires x <= 253. The final DIRECT check permits weakening, not strengthening, and PRF-1 never retries with another combination."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-fact-killed-by-write","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-5","OP-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A three-premise certificate proves a relation over the pre-assignment value images. Assigning a new first component does not make that theorem false, but the theorem says nothing about the replacement image, so the later exact addition remains unproved and OP-2 rejects."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-duplicate-use","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A non-AUTO scaled target repeats the same normalized named premise. PRF-1 rejects the second occurrence regardless of factor; one explicit multiplier is the only canonical scaling form."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-explicit-factor-one","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A non-AUTO scaled target writes an explicit proof factor of one. PRF-1 rejects that noncanonical spelling because omission alone denotes coefficient one."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-redundant-use-block","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The local invariant target is already established by AUTO from one requirement. PRF-1 rejects the entire nonempty use block as redundant; removing the block is the deterministic repair."}
{"id":"op4-neg-external-index-without-fact","rules":["OP-4","ENT-6","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary function result is an ordinary symbolic runtime value, not a fact source. Using it directly as a window index leaves the bounds Goal unproved, so OP-4 rejects before lowering. The entry destructuring now uses fresh binders and a final rest marker, as GRAM-10 requires."}
{"id":"op4-pos-external-index-after-branch","rules":["OP-4","ENT-3","ENT-6","PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same ordinary call-produced index becomes safe only on the selected true edge of a real source bounds branch. That ordinary fact discharges OP-4 and the false edge handles the domain outcome."}
{"id":"op4-pos-midpoint-automatic","rules":["OP-4","ENT-6","INV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same midpoint index without a certificate. AUTO proves the written doubled relation from the scaled quotient image and the ordered endpoints, then halves that published fact over the integers while forming the subscript residual, so the bounds obligation is automatic in this specification version."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-external-actual-without-fact","rules":["FN-8","OP-4","ENT-6","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Passing an ordinary call-produced index to a requires-protected callee without a caller fact leaves the instantiated requirement unproved. The callee requirement cannot bootstrap its own caller proof."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-external-actual-after-branch","rules":["FN-8","OP-4","ENT-3","ENT-6","PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A real caller branch proves the instantiated requirement for an ordinary call-produced index. The verified requirement then enters the callee body and discharges its subscript, with no provenance side channel or runtime fallback."}
{"id":"reject-err2-nonexhaustive","rules":["ERR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ERR-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A match missing a variant arm is rejected; exhaustiveness cannot be silenced (W3)."}
{"id":"reject-own10-dangle","rules":["REF-3","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Returning a reference to an own parameter is a dangling reference; with regions retired the subject survives in its simplest form, since a reference never escapes the function that formed it and the repair is to return an index or other owned data. Rejected before codegen."}
{"id":"reject-gram11-unnamed-call","rules":["GRAM-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A user-fn call with positional (unnamed) args must be rejected citing GRAM-11; named-in-declared-order is required."}
{"id":"xfail-own1-bare-affine-use","rules":["OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bare use of an affine value must be rejected (write move); now rejected."}
{"id":"pending-op9-buffer-new","rules":["TYPE-9","OP-9","OP-13","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A boxed runtime-capacity window is taken from the one heap only after its static allocation-size obligation is discharged [OP-9, OP-13]. Allocation is total, so the construction has no failure arm and the accepted formation has no runtime size-overflow proof fallback."}
{"id":"scope1-pos-kernel-no-gated","rules":["SCOPE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A conformant kernel program contains no gated constructs and is accepted [SCOPE-1]."}
{"id":"scope2-pos-accepted","rules":["SCOPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A program that parses, satisfies every rule, and discharges every static obligation is accepted [SCOPE-2]; the reject direction is exercised by every reject case in the suite."}
{"id":"scope3-pos-defined-run","rules":["SCOPE-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An accepted program has no undefined behavior and runs to a normal exit [SCOPE-3]."}
{"id":"form1-pos-canonical","rules":["FORM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A canonical-form program (one spelling per construct, one legal byte formatting) is accepted [FORM-1]."}
{"id":"form1-neg-unknown-construct","rules":["FORM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unknown construct is a hard error (conservative extension) [FORM-1]."}
{"id":"form2-pos-canonical-bytes","rules":["FORM-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A byte-canonical program (two-space indent, one space after ':', one statement per line) is accepted and runs [FORM-2]."}
{"id":"form2-neg-noncanonical-ws","rules":["FORM-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The isolated retained four-space indentation violates the canonical two-spaces-per-level byte format and is a hard error [FORM-2]."}
{"id":"form3-pos-lexical-classes","rules":["FORM-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"IDENT, TYPEID, LABEL (@l) and OPNAME (+checked) lexical classes used canonically; runs. The REGIONID class is deleted with regions, so the case no longer exercises it."}
{"id":"form3-neg-opname-bad-suffix","rules":["FORM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An OPNAME's mode suffix is a closed word set; 'iadd.bogus' is not a legal OPNAME [FORM-3]."}
{"id":"form3-neg-typeid-fn-name","rules":["FORM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fn name must be an IDENT ([a-z][a-z0-9_]*); a TYPEID-spelled name must be rejected [FORM-3]."}
{"id":"form4-pos-doc-not-comment","rules":["FORM-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"There are no comments; documentation rides the doc field of a declaration [FORM-4]."}
{"id":"form4-neg-comment","rules":["FORM-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A source comment must be a hard error; documentation belongs in the doc field [FORM-4]."}
{"id":"form5-pos-suffixed-literal","rules":["FORM-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An integer literal carries its mandatory type suffix ('42_i32') [FORM-5]."}
{"id":"form5-neg-missing-suffix","rules":["FORM-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An integer literal without its mandatory type suffix ('42') is illegal [FORM-5]."}
{"id":"form6-pos-unit-type-and-value","rules":["FORM-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"'unit' names the unit type in type position and the unit value in expression position ('= unit'), resolved by disjoint productions [FORM-6]."}
{"id":"form7-pos-in-range","rules":["FORM-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An in-range integer literal (i32 max, 2147483647_i32) is well-formed [FORM-7]."}
{"id":"form7-neg-out-of-range","rules":["FORM-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An out-of-range integer literal is a hard error at check time; a literal never denotes a wrapped or truncated value [FORM-7]."}
{"id":"form7-neg-leading-zero","rules":["FORM-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A leading-zero integer literal is illegal; the single digit 0 is its own form [FORM-7]."}
{"id":"gram1-pos-lookahead","rules":["GRAM-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"No checkable negative: grammar determinism is a specification and parser property, not a writer-emittable program violation with its own rule-cited verdict.","doc":"GRAM-1: the IDENT-headed place-versus-user-call fork is resolved by two-token lookahead; construction, table-op, statement-match, and value-match positions remain distinct; each source position has one core-tree derivation; runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram2-pos-items","rules":["GRAM-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"No protected negative is assigned to this positive case. Under the active specification, malformed item grammar is attributed deterministically by DIAG-1 and the source EBNF; additive diagnostic cases are separate.","doc":"GRAM-2: a program is item* \u2014 here two fn_decl items and one enum_decl item; compiles and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram3-pos-modes","rules":["GRAM-3","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","reason":"No protected negative is assigned to this positive case. Under the active specification, malformed type or mode syntax receives deterministic source-EBNF attribution through DIAG-1.","doc":"A bare i32 parameter type selects value mode; &i32 and &[i32] select reference and range-reference kinds. No own qualifier is written, and write authority remains in the effect row."}
{"id":"gram4-pos-stmts","rules":["GRAM-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"No protected negative is assigned to this positive case. Under the active specification, ill-formed statement syntax receives deterministic source-EBNF attribution through DIAG-1.","doc":"GRAM-4: exercises let, loop, break, set, statement match, if, give (let-initializer if), and return statements; runs to exit 0. The region statement is retired, so the block structure is the ordinary lexical one."}
{"id":"gram5-pos-exprs-places","rules":["GRAM-5","TYPE-7","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"This positive covers the listed expression and place forms. Other GRAM-5 forms have separate corpus cases; malformed syntax is attributed through the source-EBNF diagnostic path.","doc":"GRAM-5: literal atom, place atom, borrow_expr atom (&a), a deref place reaching a reference's referent, a field step reaching a Box's content, a table-op infix, and a construct; runs to exit 0. deref is the reference step alone and a cell's content is its field inner."}
{"id":"gram6-pos-no-operators","rules":["GRAM-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"No protected negative is assigned to this positive Run case. v0.25 admits a fixed infix set, `if`, and one counted `for`; it still defines no general precedence grammar, `while`, or iterator protocol, whose non-forms are source-grammar diagnostics rather than alternate verdicts for this case.","doc":"GRAM-6: fixed infix operators and Bool `if` drive this ordinary `loop` plus `break`; v0.25 also admits one counted half-open `for`, but no general precedence, `while`, or iterator protocol. The program sums 4..1 to 10 and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram7-pos-two-productions","rules":["GRAM-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"GRAM-7 owns two disjoint productions and their distinct node kinds; give-freedom, double-give, or give in statement position remains a GIVE-1 semantic rejection rather than a GRAM-7 citation.","doc":"GRAM-7: one match arm shape is used by two disjoint productions \u2014 a value_match initializer and a match_stmt statement \u2014 with two distinct core-tree node kinds; runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram8-pos-construct","rules":["GRAM-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-8 positive: named construction writes W's declared field once as inner:atom, and the nullary variant is written N(); accepted and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram8-neg-wrong-field","rules":["GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-8 negative: a construct of W with a misspelled field name (inr for inner) is a hard error citing GRAM-8 and K's declared field list."}
{"id":"gram8-neg-out-of-order","rules":["GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-8 negative: constructing P with fields in reversed order (b, a) is rejected citing GRAM-8 \u2014 declared order is the one legal byte sequence, never a reordering option."}
{"id":"gram9-pos-three-address","rules":["GRAM-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-9 positive: flat three-address computation \u2014 each intermediate (s, then p) is bound by a let stating its explicit mode/type and forwarded by name; no expression nesting. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram9-neg-nested-call","rules":["GRAM-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-9 negative: a call (iadd.wrap) in an atom position \u2014 nested inside imul.wrap's operand \u2014 does not derive under the grammar and must be a hard error citing GRAM-9; the fix is to bind it with a preceding let."}
{"id":"gram10-pos-named-binders","rules":["GRAM-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-10 positive: each variant field is bound as field:binder with a fresh writer-chosen IDENT in declared order \u2014 Ok(value: w), Err(error: e); runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram10-neg-wrong-field","rules":["GRAM-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-10 negative: a match arm that names the Ok field val instead of its declared name value is a hard error citing GRAM-10 and K's declared field list."}
{"id":"gram10-pos-arm-rest-releases-covered-payload","rules":["GRAM-10","WIN-3","STOR-3"],"expect":{"exit":7,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An own-place arm binds one payload field and covers the other with a final `..`; the covered Box takes its compiler-derived release on entry to the arm."}
{"id":"gram11-pos-named-args","rules":["GRAM-11"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-11 positive: a call to user fn add2 names its arguments as x:atom, y:atom in declared parameter order \u2014 the GRAM-8 discipline applied to calls; runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"gram11-neg-misspelled","rules":["GRAM-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-11 negative: a call to add2 naming a parameter z that is not in the callee's parameter list (x, y) is a hard error citing GRAM-11."}
{"id":"gram11-neg-out-of-order","rules":["GRAM-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-11 negative: a call to sub2 writing its named arguments out of declared order (y, x) is rejected citing GRAM-11 \u2014 parameter names are checked facts, never a reordering license."}
{"id":"type1-pos-i32-unit","rules":["TYPE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"i32 and unit primitives [TYPE-1]: an exact add and an ordinary equality branch run to exit 0."}
{"id":"type1-pos-wide-prims","rules":["TYPE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The full primitive set beyond i32 (i8/i64/u32/f64 params) is nameable and type-checks [TYPE-1]."}
{"id":"type2-pos-enum","rules":["TYPE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"enum is a composite type [TYPE-2]: a value is constructed and matched; runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"type3-pos-nameable","rules":["TYPE-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each constructible type, parameter kind and effect has a finite canonical source spelling requiring no compiler execution."}
{"id":"type4-pos-cvt","rules":["TYPE-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Numeric conversion is explicit [TYPE-4]; bare cvt from i32 to i64 has a whole-type total domain."}
{"id":"type5-neg-arg-mismatch","rules":["TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A unit argument passed where an i32 parameter is declared violates exact argument typing [TYPE-5]."}
{"id":"type6-pos-distinct-names","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Distinct value names plus a break to a lexically enclosing loop label [TYPE-6]; explicit wrapping arithmetic keeps the loop total, and the program runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"type6-neg-dup-variant","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One enum declaring the variant name Dup() twice violates that enum's variant-name uniqueness [TYPE-6]."}
{"id":"type6-neg-shadow","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Redeclaring a live name (`let a` twice) must be rejected as shadowing [TYPE-6]."}
{"id":"type7-pos-deref","rules":["TYPE-7","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Subject restated at v0.60: deref is the reference step, so the program reads 42 back through a reference variable [TYPE-7, REF-1] and runs to exit 0. The same case read Box content through deref at v0.59, which the field step inner now owns."}
{"id":"type7-neg-deref-nonref","rules":["TYPE-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"deref applied to a plain i32 value is malformed [TYPE-7]: deref takes a place whose selected kind is a reference, &T or &[T], and an owned scalar is neither; a Box is refused there too, its content being the ordinary field inner [TYPE-9]."}
{"id":"type7-neg-implicit-read","rules":["TYPE-7","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference variable denotes the reference [REF-1] and the storage it names is reached only through deref, so writing the bare reference where a value of the referent type is declared is the missing dereference [TYPE-7], whose mechanical fix is deref(p)."}
{"id":"const1-pos-array-size","rules":["CONST-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A decimal integer literal is a constant-expression usable as a Slots<T, n> capacity."}
{"id":"const1-neg-noninteger","rules":["CONST-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An IDENT resolving to a non-integer (unit-typed) const used in a const-expression position, here a run capacity, is a compile-time rejection."}
{"id":"const2-pos-item","rules":["CONST-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named, immutable, program-lifetime const item of primitive type [CONST-2]."}
{"id":"const2-neg-noneligible","rules":["CONST-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CONST-2 rejects a runtime-capacity owned container as a named constant; the container is reachable only as Box content, and neither Box nor Slots is const-eligible. The original integer initializer remains."}
{"id":"own1-pos-consume-once","rules":["OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OWN-1 positive: an affine own-enum value is consumed by exactly one match-move; no reuse, runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"own1-neg-use-after-move","rules":["OWN-1","OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","reason":"Also the OWN-13 negative: matching an own place moves it (OWN-13), so the second match is a use of a dead binding (OWN-1). The checker cites OWN-1 for use-after-move, so this case carries the OWN-13 violation but asserts OWN-1.","doc":"OWN-1 negative: matching an own value moves it; a second match of the same binding is a use of a moved (dead) binding \u2014 rejected citing OWN-1."}
{"id":"own1-pos-explicit-affine-call","rules":["OWN-1","FN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An affine value passed to an own parameter is explicitly transferred with move; the call consumes it exactly once and runs."}
{"id":"own1-neg-bare-affine-call","rules":["OWN-1","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bare affine argument to an own parameter is not an implicit move; OWN-1 requires the explicit move spelling."}
{"id":"own2-pos-three-modes","rules":["REF-1","SET-1","EFF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"This positive exercises reference reads and effect-authorized writes. Parameter spelling is independently protected by the GRAM-2/3 qualifier negatives and the TYPE-5/7 argument-kind cases.","doc":"A bare parameter type selects value mode and & selects a reference. The case reads through deref and writes through a reference parameter whose declared row carries the write; its existing run verdict is unchanged."}
{"id":"own4-neg-return-local-borrow","rules":["REF-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CITED RULE CHANGED at v0.60 from the retired OWN-4 to REF-3: a reference formed over a local never leaves the function that formed it, so returning it is refused and the return hands back owned data instead."}
{"id":"own5-pos-read-through-holder","rules":["REF-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SUBJECT NARROWED at v0.60 with the verdict kept: reads and writes through a reference are ordinary accesses to the path it names, with no permission marker on the reference; the owner then observes the write. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"own7-pos-distinct-noverlap","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","reason":"OWN-7 defines overlap and is consumed by EFF-5's pairwise call-site comparison. This positive exercises the distinct-root non-overlap case; index and range cases belong to the corresponding families.","doc":"OWN-7 positive: two substituted write paths rooted at distinct bindings do not overlap, so the pairwise call-site comparison admits the pair and each root keeps its own value; runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"own10-neg-dangle-caller","rules":["REF-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CITED RULE CHANGED at v0.60 from the retired OWN-10 to REF-3: the escaping-reference subject survives without region machinery, because a reference is never returned. A function that found something returns an index or other owned data and the caller forms the reference."}
{"id":"own11-pos-loop-inner-region","rules":["OWN-11","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SUBJECT NARROWED at v0.60 with the verdict kept: a body that consumes a binding declared outside it and reinitializes that complete binding before the backedge agrees with the entering edge at the loop head and is admitted. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"own11-neg-borrow-outer-region","rules":["OWN-11","LIV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SUBJECT NARROWED at v0.60 with the verdict kept: regions are gone, and OWN-11's surviving negative is the per-iteration liveness disagreement. A body that consumes a binding declared outside it and never reinitializes it reaches its backedge dead while its entering edge was live, so one iteration would start in a state the previous one did not leave."}
{"id":"own11-neg-move-outer-in-loop","rules":["OWN-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OWN-11 negative (second clause): a binding s declared outside loop @l is moved inside it (via an own-scrutinee match); a second iteration would use a dead binding \u2014 rejected citing OWN-11."}
{"id":"own12-pos-distinct-uniq-args","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two disjoint write paths at one call stay admitted: the substituted paths have different roots, so the pairwise comparison proves them distinct."}
{"id":"own12-neg-alias-uniq-args","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CITED RULE CHANGED at v0.60 from the retired OWN-12 to EFF-5: write authority is now the callee's declared row, and two substituted write paths of one call that resolve to the same place are not proved disjoint."}
{"id":"own12-neg-a-view-argument-beside-a-unique-borrow-of-itself","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CITED RULE CHANGED at v0.60 from the retired OWN-12 to EFF-5: a range-reference parameter is its own binding rather than an unknown overlapping every position, so two arguments of one call resolving to that binding, with the callee's row writing one of them, are the ordinary pairwise call-site overlap."}
{"id":"own13-pos-let-match-give","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OWN-13 positive (let-initializer match): a value-match scrutinee is an owned temporary; each arm delivers the let's declared own value via give, and on the taken arm the own result is moved exactly once (no double-move, T1). Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"own13-pos-borrow-match-live","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OWN-13 positive (reference scrutinee): matching through a reference leaves the scrutinee live (binder modes derived, not written); s is matched a second time after the first match, proving the reference-mode match did not move it."}
{"id":"stor1-pos-frame-resident","rules":["STOR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Storage class is a function of type [STOR-1]: an i32 local is frame-resident, with no per-binding storage annotation and no default clause; runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "stor2-pos-box-new", "rules": ["OP-13", "TYPE-9", "STOR-8"], "expect": {"exit": 0, "kind": "run"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Creation: box_new(value: v) returns own Box<T>, with no store argument and no Result wrapper because allocation is total; content is read as the field inner."}
{"id": "stor3-pos-box-drop-region", "rules": ["STOR-3", "TYPE-9", "OP-13"], "expect": {"exit": 0, "kind": "run"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A cell taken at the heap is released by a compiler-derived walk on every edge that leaves its owner scope, in reverse declaration order; both the early return and the final return carry that release, and it is explicit in the checked program."}
{"id":"op1-pos-table-op","rules":["OP-1","OP-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every computation names one operation from the operation table. A wrapping add and integer comparison run to exit 0, with the comparison supplying the Bool condition required by OP-5."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ineg-unsigned","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ineg has exactly one signed integer type argument [OP-1]; an unsigned instantiation is rejected rather than inferred or reinterpreted."}
{"id":"op2-pos-div-checked","rules":["OP-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"idiv.checked yields Result<T, DivError> [OP-2]: Err(DivideByZero()) for a zero divisor, Err(DivOverflow()) for signed iK::MIN/-1, else Ok."}
{"id":"op2-pos-ineg-modes","rules":["OP-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The exact, wrapping, and checked negation families run through one program [OP-2]: `ineg.wrap` keeps minimum at minimum, dotless exact `ineg` admits a representable negation through its `ineg.defined` obligation, and `ineg.checked` returns Err(Overflow()) for minimum."}
{"id":"op2-pos-guarded-subtraction-l0-bridge","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","ENT-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The true edge of an executed lower <= upper guard contributes one ordinary difference bound. The deterministic affine discharge route consumes that same relation directly, admits upper - lower without an explicit invariant certificate, and the closed call observes 9 - 4 as 5."}
{"id":"op2-neg-div-wrap","rules":["OP-2","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Division and remainder carry no wrap mode [OP-2]; idiv.wrap names no table op and must be rejected."}
{"id":"op3-pos-fadd-strict","rules":["OP-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A float op that ROUNDS carries .strict [OP-3]: fadd.strict is IEEE-754, no reassociation, no contraction \u2014 the family a future fast-math mode would relax."}
{"id":"op3-neg-exact-dotted","rules":["OP-3","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Float ops that are exact or exact-selection are dotless [OP-3]: fneg carries no .strict. OP-3 fixes the dotted/dotless convention but states no rejection of its own; `fneg.strict` is an OPNAME absent from the operation-family inventory, which OP-1 makes a hard error."}
{"id":"op4-neg-index-undischarged","rules":["OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unproven parameter offset is a compile-time rejection under discharge-or-reject. The diagnostic carries the ENT-6 residual i < deref(b).len and points to a dominating branch, function requirement, loop invariant, or checked source proof as the repair."}
{"id":"op4-pos-index-discharged","rules":["OP-4","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The deterministic unsigned-remainder range establishes the exact upper bound needed by the following const-array subscript [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id":"op6-pos-cvt-total","rules":["OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Bare cvt returns Dst after proving its domain [OP-6]; i8->i32 is one of the 39 whole-type total pairs."}
{"id":"op6-pos-cvt-checked","rules":["OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"cvt.checked returns Result<Dst, NarrowError> for every numeric pair [OP-6]: i32->i8 yields Err(NarrowError()) when not exactly representable; the selected value never rounds, truncates, or saturates."}
{"id":"op6-pos-cvt-identity","rules":["OP-6","FN-2","OP-8"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Same-type conversion is whole-type total, including symbolic repeated endpoints. Integer identity, noncanonical f32/f64 NaN payloads and signs, and negative zero are preserved. Identity rejection is deliberately retired because OP-6 now admits all 100 numeric pairs."}
{"id":"op7-pos-name-convention","rules":["OP-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Operation names follow the convention [OP-7]: a truthful domain prefix (i/f/b/e) plus a .mode suffix iff on a mode axis. This existing case exercises iadd.checked and dotless ieq; it does not itself exercise eeq."}
{"id":"op7-neg-missing-prefix","rules":["OP-7","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An arithmetic/logic/bit/compare op carries a domain prefix [OP-7]; unprefixed add.wrap names no table op and must be rejected."}
{"id":"op8-pos-iand","rules":["OP-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"iand/ior/ixor lower to LLVM and/or/xor and are total [OP-8]; the bitwise ops carry no mode suffix."}
{"id":"op8-neg-rotate-checked","rules":["OP-8","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"irotl/irotr take the amount modulo width and are dotless-total [OP-8]; `irotl.checked` is a well-formed operation token but names no table row and is rejected under OP-1."}
{"id":"op8-pos-u64-shift-u32","rules":["OP-1","OP-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Shift amounts are uniformly u32 even when the shifted value is u64; 16 >> 2 equals 4 and runs."}
{"id":"op8-pos-integer-family","rules":["OP-1","OP-2","OP-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A checksum-style scalar mix combines rotate, xor, wrapping addition, population count, and unsigned minimum through the general integer path; it runs without project- or function-specific handling."}
{"id":"op9-pos-buffer-new","rules":["OP-9","FN-8","ENT-6","STOR-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"box_slots_new::<i32>(capacity: n) accepts a runtime count once the source contract establishes a numeric upper bound that closes the allocation-size goal [FN-8, OP-9, ENT-6]. Target qualification then proves that the entire reachable byte range fits the selected allocation and address domains [STOR-6]."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-signature-driven-call","rules":["FN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fn's signature states param modes/types and return mode/type; main calls it relying only on that signature (FN-1). Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-contract-conform","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A formal declares function-kind signatures and a named actual supplies the complete ordered function argument group (D7 FN-3); no implementation is attached to a type."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-two-explicit-actuals","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"D7 removes implicit type/conformance uniqueness. Two differently named actual groups may bind the same formal application; selection is explicit."}
{"id":"fn5-pos-match-dispatch","rules":["FN-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Behavior is selected by match over a closed enum (FN-5's 'closed-set dispatch is match'), not a function value; there is no fn-value construct to violate. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"fn6-pos-recursion","rules":["FN-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A self-recursive fn (sum 0..n) is permitted (FN-6); sumto(4)==10. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"fn6-neg-polymorphic-recursion","rules":["FN-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A generic fn whose recursive call instantiates the callee at a fixed type (i32) instead of the caller's own type parameter T is polymorphic recursion, rejected by FN-6's syntactic rule."}
{"id":"fn6-pos-a-generic-cycle-at-the-callers-own-parameters","rules":["FN-6","FN-2","MSR-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-6 permits recursion and refuses only polymorphic recursion. A call on a cycle that writes exactly the caller's own generic parameters is the caller's own instance and mints no second one, so the cycle is finite and monomorphizes; the case runs one const-parameter cycle over a window reached by reference and one type-parameter cycle."}
{"id":"fn7-pos-single-main","rules":["FN-7","FN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary parameterless main returns ExitStatus. C2 removes the command marker and all entry-only signature or contract restrictions; this selected launcher signature runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"fn7-neg-two-mains","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The later of two fn main declarations is the TYPE-6 duplicate declaration selected by the active specification's exact diagnostic order."}
# obligation discharge: source facts and the entailment fragment (section 18)
{"id":"ent1-pos-instantiation-judged-at-value","rules":["ENT-1","FN-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The generic source-schema judgment receives an executed length guard for its subscript; the n = 4 concrete instance is then independently rechecked and runs with the expected value [ENT-1, FN-2, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent1-neg-instantiation-judged-at-value","rules":["ENT-1","FN-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unguarded subscript in a const-generic source body is rejected by its source-schema ordinary judgment; a later concrete call cannot retroactively admit the body [ENT-1, FN-2, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-array-length-survives-root-replace","rules":["ENT-2","ENT-4","ENT-5","OP-11","OP-4","OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The implicit len = N of an Array<T, N> field holds at every program point. An element read feeding a branch condition puts a join on the path, and the OP-11 swap of explicit nocopy owners then kills every relation supported by their array fields [ENT-5]. The length equality is a function of the field's type, so closure still discharges the later subscripts [ENT-4, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-no-fact-across-call","rules":["ENT-2","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"No fact crosses a call boundary [ENT-2]. A checked comparison bounds the argument, but the value an ordinary call returns is a fresh term carrying no relation to it, so the subscript is undischarged and the residual names the returned binding."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-expired-spelling-inherits-nothing","rules":["ENT-2","ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Facts attach to terms, not spellings. A branch-local checked bound dies when its scope ends, and a later binding reusing that spelling inherits nothing [ENT-2, ENT-5, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s1-branch-fact","rules":["ENT-3","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Source S1: a branch whose condition has comparison origin establishes that relation at the then-block's entry and its exact negation at the else-block's entry [ENT-3]; the guarded branch discharges the subscript with no runtime bounds branch."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s4-requires-fact","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Source S4: each complete static requires-clause goal, with contract definitions expanded and clause locals substituted out, is admitted independently at body entry [ENT-2]. Every ordinary caller proves that goal before the call [FN-8]. The measure operand is now a member place form [OP-15]."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s5-set-commit-image","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-5","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Source S5 publishes a direct-place set commit's literal, term-copy, or admitted integer-conversion equality after the old target facts are killed. Here start = back carries the already proved length-minus-eight bound to the subscript without a runtime check."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s6-allocation-length-fact","rules":["ENT-3","OP-9","OP-4","OP-13"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A construction function's length and capacity facts are the ensures of its PRE-1 record and reach the caller through ENT-3.S12 like any other declared relation; S6 itself carries no construction row in this version. The S5 copy equality carries the literal into the capacity argument, so each placement discharges its len < cap requirement and a constant offset below the window they built discharges. Allocation is total, and its only static obligation is the size one [OP-9]."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-bor-guard-decomposition","rules":["ENT-3","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ENT-3 signed Boolean decomposition: establishing a signed goal also establishes every member of its structural decomposition, and decomp of a negative bor(A, B) is the conjunctive read of both negatives. The else edge of a two-sided bor guard therefore establishes both, whose projections negate to a nonnegative symbol below 4; with the declared window length the guarded subscript discharges and the program runs."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-band-check-decomposition","rules":["ENT-3","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"On the true edge of band(A, B), ENT-3 decomposes the condition into both positive operands. The two projected upper bounds independently discharge the two window subscript obligations."}
{"id":"ent4-pos-transitivity-discharges","rules":["ENT-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary executed control establishes i at most j and j below the length; closure transitivity derives i below the length and discharges the subscript [ENT-4, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent4-pos-disequality-strengthens","rules":["ENT-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary executed control establishes the index at most the length and unequal to it; closure strengthens the relation to the strict bound required by the subscript [ENT-4, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent4-neg-nonstrict-bound-underivable","rules":["ENT-4","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Derivability is exact: a - b <= c is derivable only when the closed state contains a - b <= c' with c' no greater than c [ENT-4]. A checked non-strict bound does not derive the obligation's strict one, correctly, since the index may equal the length."}
{"id":"ent4-pos-contradictory-state-discharges","rules":["ENT-3","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary executed control establishes the real subscript bound. A written proof conclusion has authority only after its premises are checked, so it cannot manufacture an unreachable fact or use explosion to admit a partial operation [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-element-write-preserves-length","rules":["ENT-5","SET-1","OP-4","MSR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The support of a length term is the descriptor storage of the place, not its element storage, so an element write never kills a length fact [ENT-5, MSR-2]: a window is moved only by an operation that moves a boundary. The later subscript still discharges."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-join-keeps-common-bound","rules":["ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The join keeps, for each ordered term pair, the weakest bound held by all joined states [ENT-5]. Both branches establish the same strict bound, so the continuation keeps it and the subscript after the join discharges."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-close-before-scope-kill","rules":["ENT-4","ENT-5","ENT-3","SET-1","OP-4","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The true branch derives out = bumped = i + 1 and i < 3. ENT-4 closure materializes out < 4 before bumped leaves scope; ENT-5 then kills every conclusion still naming bumped, while the survivor-only bound discharges the continuation subscript. The incoming array is a by-value parameter, so the declared row is the empty one."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-project-middle-before-set","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-4","ENT-5","SET-1","OP-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The entry facts x <= middle <= 254 imply the survivor-only bound x <= 254. Before set replaces the middle vertex, complete pre-kill closure materializes that consequence, so the following exact x + 1 is admitted. ent5-neg-kill-on-write separately pins that replacing a conclusion endpoint still removes its old bound."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-close-before-set-strengthening","rules":["ENT-4","ENT-5","SET-1","OP-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The entry facts value <= byte_limit and value != byte_limit first strengthen to value < byte_limit; the implicit u8 ceiling then yields value <= 254. Complete ENT-4 closure materializes that survivor-only consequence before set replaces byte_limit, so the following exact increment remains proved."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-join-takes-weakest-bound","rules":["ENT-5","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The join keeps the weakest (largest-constant) bound held by all joined states [ENT-5]. One branch establishes the strict bound and the other only the non-strict one, so the continuation carries the non-strict bound and the subscript after the join is undischarged."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-kill-on-write","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-3","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A checked branch bounds offset; a later set kills that old fact before S5 establishes only offset = start. Because start itself is unbounded, the new post-write equality cannot resurrect the length bound, and OP-4 still rejects."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-loop-rule-drops-preloop-fact","rules":["ENT-5","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An else-free false edge carries the offset write to the next loop head, so the continuing kill removes the dominating checked-branch bound and the subscript remains undischarged [ENT-5, ENT-6, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-return-does-not-kill-loop-head-fact","rules":["ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A return edge cannot reach a later iteration head, so its scope-exit kills do not remove a pre-loop bound there [ENT-5]. Else-free false edges may continue, but neither kills the bound, so the head subscript discharges [OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent6-neg-join-one-arm-advances-accumulator","rules":["INV-1","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One arm of the body join advances the accumulator and the other leaves it, so the incoming value images share no nonconstant coefficient vector and differ by more than a constant. [ENT-6] gives the binding one fresh full-type atom at the join and [ENT-5]'s all-predecessor intersection keeps no bound that only the advancing arm established, so [INV-1] cannot re-establish the header target over that atom. The rejection is mandatory even though the relation holds on every execution; the join is deliberately not path-sensitive."}
{"id":"ent6-pos-join-value-if-lifted-addend","rules":["ENT-6","INV-1","GIVE-1","OP-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same fold with the choice lifted out of the control join and into the addend. The value_if delivers one owned value [GIVE-1], the body applies one unconditional update, and the single value image leaves the [ENT-6] join nothing to weaken. A local invariant bounds the delivered addend by the byte ceiling, so [INV-1] re-establishes the header target and [OP-2] admits the exact addition; the explicit result control observes the even-index total 6."}
# coverage annotations (non-program rules)
{"reason":"Reject-when-unsure is the checker's global disposition, not a program-checkable constraint: it is never emitted as a standalone 'OWN-8' verdict. Every OWN reject case in this cluster (own1/3/4/5/10/11/12-neg) IS an instance of it \u2014 each names the specific rule and, per the spec, the restructuring. A sound-but-unprovable program is still rejected, but the diagnostic cites the concrete rule, so there is no source->verdict pair that asserts OWN-8 itself.","covered_by":"policy","rule":"OWN-8"}
{"reason":"OWN-9 is explicitly a non-normative optimizer consequence, not a source-to-verdict rule. It states facts licensed by OWN-5 exclusivity; the positive and negative ownership cases exercise the source guarantees from which an optimizer may later derive those facts, without asserting that the current backend emits a particular attribute.","covered_by":"policy","rule":"OWN-9"}
{"reason":"No source program can directly test whether the specification itself contains a context-dependent rule or a default. The active specification is inspected for that property; lexer, grammar, and resolver tests separately exercise the context-free decisions implemented by the compiler.","covered_by":"specification-inspection","rule":"META-2"}
{"id":"eff1-neg-wrong-order-row","rules":["EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An effect row spelling a writes entry before a reads entry violates EFF-1's canonical order, in which every reads entry precedes every writes entry, and is rejected."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-read-subsumed-by-write","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"writes(p) states every access at or below p, so an effect row never lists reads(p) beside it; a row declaring reads(stats.count) beside writes(stats.count) is rejected at the reads entry, carrying the writes entry that covers it, before any call reaches EFF-5."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-read-below-written-path","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"writes(stats) states every access at or below stats, so a row that also lists reads(stats.count) states one access twice and is rejected at that reads entry."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-write-below-written-path","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"writes(pair) states every access at or below pair, so a row that also lists writes(pair.first) states one access twice and is rejected at that writes entry."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-read-below-read-path","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"reads(stats) states every observation at or below stats, so a row that also lists reads(stats.count) states one observation twice and is rejected at that reads entry, carrying reads(stats); the body reads both, so only EFF-1 refuses the row."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-absent-array-capacity","rules":["EFF-1","MSR-1","TYPE-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An Array has no cap measure. A familiar measure spelling alone cannot make reads(values.cap) a well-formed effect path."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-absent-slots-head","rules":["EFF-1","MSR-1","TYPE-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A Slots window has no head measure. Type-directed effect formation rejects reads(values.head) instead of admitting the measure merely by spelling."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-source-generic-is-not-a-window-parameter","rules":["EFF-1","OP-10","TYPE-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A source generic named W is not PRE-1's compiler-owned window parameter. Its effect row cannot select window.next merely from the parameter's spelling; OP-10 fixes that domain by declaration identity."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-undeclared-exhibited","rules":["EFF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A body that appends to the window behind its reference parameter exhibits writes(target.next) and writes(target.len) and declares the empty row instead, which EFF-2 rejects. Allocation no longer contributes an effect entry, so the undeclared-but-exhibited subject is carried by a window operation rather than by a store."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-declared-unexhibited","rules":["EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A function declaring a write of its reference parameter's referent whose body performs none is rejected as declared-but-unexhibited; allocation and release carry no effect entry at all, so no row can be justified by them."}
{"id":"eff3-pos-pure-fn","rules":["EFF-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A genuinely pure function uses only total wrapping arithmetic and performs no reads, writes, or allocation. This is the shape EFF-3 licenses to deduplicate or reorder; it runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"err1-pos-result-value-match","rules":["ERR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A recoverable error is a value: `iadd.checked` yields a prelude `Result`, dispatched by `match` arms (Ok/Err) \u2014 no exceptions/unwinding [ERR-1]. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"err2-pos-exhaustive-match","rules":["ERR-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A `match` covering every declared variant (Red/Green/Blue), with no wildcard arm, is exhaustive and accepted [ERR-2]. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"err2-neg-missing-variant","rules":["ERR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ERR-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A `match` missing the Blue() arm is non-exhaustive; the checker rejects it citing ERR-2 (exhaustiveness cannot be silenced \u2014 there are no wildcard arms)."}
{"id":"err3-pos-propagate","rules":["ERR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`let y = propagate step(x: x);` inside a fn returning `own Result<i32, MyErr>` binds the Ok payload as an own i32 value and returns Err(err) on the Err path, with matching E [ERR-3]; well-formed, accepted."}
{"id":"err3-neg-error-type-mismatch","rules":["ERR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ERR-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`propagate step(x)` where step yields Result<i32, ErrA> but the enclosing fn returns Result<i32, ErrB> violates the same-E requirement of ERR-3 (no conversions); rejected citing ERR-3."}
{"id":"err4-pos-recoverable-value","rules":["ERR-4","ERR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Classification is table-fixed: checked overflow is an expected failure represented as a typed value. iadd.checked on i32::MAX+1 delivers Err, which ordinary control matches and handles [ERR-4]."}
{"id":"err4-checked-domain-violation","rules":["ERR-4","OP-1","SCOPE-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Representability failure is an expected typed value: checked addition returns Err for i32 maximum plus one and ordinary control handles it [ERR-4, OP-1, SCOPE-3]."}
{"id":"prog1-pos-closed-unit","rules":["PROG-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"PROG-1 closed world: `helper` is defined in-unit and `==`/`True` come from the op table + prelude; the surface exposes no import/extern/dynamic-load/reflection construct, so every name resolves within the unit plus the prelude (\u00a715). Compiles and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"pre1-pos-prelude-enums","rules":["PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"PRE-1 positive: the prelude enums are usable with no declaration in the unit -- Bool (True/False) obtained from a compare op, Result (Ok/Err) from checked arithmetic -- proving the prelude supplies exactly these names unimported. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"ex1-pos-worked-example","rules":["EX-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"EX-1 positive: the v0.60 worked-example bytes are accepted and run to exit 0. The counted Slots fill under its header invariant, the reference read bare, and the let-initializer match with give are all exercised, and ordinary result control maps an unexpected arithmetic or value outcome to a nonzero status."}
# coverage annotations (non-program rules)
{"reason":"Every reject expectation names exactly one rule ID, and the runner requires an installed adapter to return that exact ID. Compiler tests additionally check structured rule and source locations for implemented frontend, resolver, and semantic failures. The complete coverage statement becomes executable only when the adapter is installed.","covered_by":"compiler-diagnostics","rule":"DIAG-1"}
{"reason":"Semantic success in the candidate compiler publishes one private checked-program value, and lowering accepts that value rather than reparsing source or replaying a serialized artifact. Compiler tests inspect retained operations, cleanup edges, static obligations, and their dispositions for the implemented semantic families.","covered_by":"compiler-checked-program","rule":"DIAG-2"}
{"reason":"The semantic checker rejects every unproved writer-visible obligation before checked IR exists. Lowering and emitted-module tests establish that accepted proof syntax is erased and introduces no writer abort, exception, unwinding edge, hidden runtime predicate, or proof fallback; ordinary release actions remain on normal control-flow edges.","covered_by":"compiler-source-proof-erasure","rule":"EFF-4"}
{"reason":"Compiler source-bundle tests cover ordered logical records, exact path identity, duplicate and invalid paths, empty-unit rejection, and order-sensitive unit identity. These are invocation-envelope judgments rather than writer-emittable source-language verdicts.","covered_by":"source-envelope-tests","rule":"PROG-2"}
{"reason":"Selected-target representability and target-domain qualification are non-source outcomes. Focused compiler tests cover complete static objects, checked runtime-allocation bounds, element-address obligations, target failures with no source-rule attribution, and the absence of a runtime target-domain guard.","covered_by":"compiler-target-layout-tests","rule":"STOR-6"}
{"reason":"CAP-1 now states that no separate capability category exists: ordinary own, shared and unique loans, place overlap, and exact effects are the complete current concurrency vocabulary. This is a closed language-model property rather than one source verdict; ownership and permission tests exercise its concrete consequences.","covered_by":"compiler-ownership-model","rule":"CAP-1"}
{"id":"x-ownmove-payload-binder-consumed-twice","rules":["OWN-1","OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An affine payload binder pulled out of an own composite by a match is itself affine; matching that binder a second time is a use-after-consume, exercising OWN-1 affinity at the binder (not the scrutinee) level."}
{"id":"x-ownmove-partial-move-kills-binding","rules":["OWN-1","OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Matching an own composite by move consumes the whole binding even when only a payload field is bound (partial move kills the whole binding, OWN-13 move + OWN-1); a second match of the dead binding is rejected citing OWN-1."}
{"id":"x-ownmove-borrow-after-match-move","rules":["OWN-1","OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Matching an own place moves it (OWN-13); forming a reference to the now-dead binding afterward is a use after move, distinct from the usual match-after-move form and rejected citing OWN-1."}
{"id":"x-ownmove-copy-reused-affine-consumed-once","rules":["OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The copy-vs-affine classification of OWN-1 in one program: an i32 binding is reused three times (copy on use) while an enum value is consumed by exactly one match (affine); accepts and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"x-ownmove-borrow-match-live-then-own-move","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Both OWN-13 scrutinee modes in one program: matching through a reference leaves the value live, then a later own-place match moves it exactly once (no double move); accepts and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"x-ownmove-owned-temporary-scrutinee","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OWN-13's owned-temporary clause: a non-place scrutinee (a bare construction) is an owned temporary moved into the match, needing no named binding to consume; accepts and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"x-borrow-write-through-shared-borrow","rules":["SET-1","EFF-1","TYPE-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SET-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Rejecting rule changed at v0.60: write authority is no longer a marker on the reference. A target reached through deref of a reference parameter is writable exactly when that parameter's declared row carries writes of the path, so a row carrying reads alone makes the assignment a SET-1 refusal at the target place. v0.59 cited OWN-5."}
{"id":"x-borrow-two-shared-reads-run","rules":["REF-1","TYPE-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Positive complement to the overlap check: two references naming one place coexist and are both read through deref [REF-1, TYPE-7], read/read overlap never conflicting [EFF-5], so the comparison feeding the ordinary control accepts and runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"x-borrow-uniq-shared-call-args-overlap","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Rejecting rule changed at v0.60: the overlapping-argument subject survives as the pairwise call-site check. Write authority is stated by the callee row rather than by a borrow marker, so a write path and a read path substituted from two arguments naming one run overlap and the call is refused citing EFF-5, carrying both substituted paths. v0.59 cited OWN-12."}
{"id":"x-borrow-return-uniq-local-region","rules":["REF-3","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Rejecting rule changed at v0.60: the escaping-reference subject survives in a simpler form without regions. A reference may be bound to a local and passed as an argument but never returned, so the return expression is refused citing REF-3 with the restructuring to return an index and let the caller form the reference. v0.59 cited OWN-4 outlives."}
{"id":"x-typ-borrow-arg-missing-deref","rules":["TYPE-7","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference formed over a field names that field's path [REF-1], but the reference variable denotes the reference and the field is reached only through deref, so the bare reference at a call argument whose parameter is own i32 is the missing dereference and a hard error citing TYPE-7, with the mechanical fix deref(r)."}
{"id":"x-typ-uniq-deref-write-roundtrip","rules":["TYPE-9","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Writing Box content with set cell.inner and reading the content back exercises the Box content field step [TYPE-9], the content being reached by the ordinary field step and never by deref, together with SET-1's writable-target relation through a live own cell binding; a positive combination that runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"x-typ-value-where-borrow-param","rules":["TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Passing an own i32 value where a reference parameter is declared is a TYPE-5 mismatch: a reference is formed by the & spelling and never by a bare value at that position. The other half of the mode pair, a bare reference at an own parameter, is instead the TYPE-7 missing dereference."}
{"id":"x-typ-bool-cmp-result-as-int","rules":["TYPE-5","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Committing a Bool comparison result to a place whose written type is i32 crosses the arithmetic and enum layers and must be rejected as an exact-type mismatch [TYPE-5]; a subtle negative in the arithmetic subset. The set target carries the written type because a binder's no longer does."}
{"id":"x-typ-match-foreign-variant","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Matching a source enum scrutinee with a prelude Some arm names no variant of the scrutinee's enum; an arm label resolves against the scrutinee's known enum [TYPE-6], so this is a hard error rather than a silently accepted mismatch."}
{"id":"x-typ-let-shadows-param","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A let x inside a function whose parameter is already named x redeclares a live name and must be rejected as shadowing [TYPE-6], covering the parameter-shadow site the existing let/let redeclaration case misses."}
{"id":"x-match-err2-value-match-missing-err","rules":["ERR-2","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ERR-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Non-exhaustiveness in a let-initializer value-match over a prelude Result [PRE-1]: omitting the Err arm is non-exhaustive and is rejected citing ERR-2 (the value-match analog of the statement-match exhaustiveness case, and ERR-2 fires ahead of any GIVE-1 give-completeness objection)."}
{"id":"x-match-give1-give-in-stmt-match","rules":["GIVE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GIVE-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GIVE-1 legality is checker-scoped exactly like break's enclosing-loop rule: a `give` appearing in a statement-position match (not a let-initializer match) is a hard error citing GIVE-1 \u2014 the grammar admits give_stmt, the checker restricts where it is legal."}
{"id":"x-match-gram10-out-of-order-fields","rules":["GRAM-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-10 declared-order discipline on a multi-field variant: an arm for a two-field variant Both(a, b) that binds its fields out of declared order (b before a) is a hard error citing GRAM-10 and the declared field list; declared order is the one legal byte sequence, not a reordering option."}
{"id":"x-match-gram10-binder-not-fresh","rules":["GRAM-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-10 binder freshness: a match binder must be a fresh IDENT distinct from any live name; reusing the live outer name `w` as the Ok payload binder is rejected citing GRAM-10 (the TYPE-6 no-shadowing guarantee GRAM-10's freshness rule preserves)."}
{"id":"x-match-give1-wrong-type","rules":["GIVE-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GIVE-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GIVE-1 type requirement: the binder's mode and type are derived by exact agreement over the closed delivery set, so a delivering give whose type differs from an earlier one is a hard error citing GIVE-1 at the later give_stmt. The scrutinee is a source enum so that GRAM-6 forms no candidate and GIVE-1 genuinely owns the rejection."}
{"id":"x-match-give1-nested-value-match","rules":["GIVE-1","PRE-1","ERR-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Positive combination \u2014 value-match giving from a nested value-match: the outer Ok arm delivers by giving `inner`, itself bound by an inner prelude-Result match [PRE-1]; both matches are exhaustive [ERR-2] and every arm on every path delivers exactly one give [GIVE-1]. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"x-eff-dup-reads-effect","rules":["EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Writing the reads entry twice for one path duplicates that entry rather than being the one canonical reads(x), which EFF-1's canonical row forbids; this subtly differs from a plain wrong-order row."}
{"id":"x-eff-writes-missing-region","rules":["EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bare writes effect with no (REGIONID+) region list violates EFF-1's grammar (writes must name at least one region); covers the region-arity boundary the existing wrong-order case never touches."}
{"id":"x-eff-pure-combined-with-allocation","rules":["GRAM-2","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"pure is the unique spelling of the empty row and is not an entry another entry may follow, so the comma after it fails the enclosing fn_decl continuation under DIAG-1/GRAM-2. The program still rejects and pure still cannot combine with another entry."}
{"id":"x-eff-trailing-comma-row","rules":["EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A trailing comma after an effect entry is rejected because EFF-1 permits commas only between effects."}
