{"id":"op1-pos-tag-enum-equality","rules":["OP-1","OP-7","OP-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Tag-only enum equality is direct, nominal, and complement-correct for zero-, one-, and three-variant enums."}
{"id":"op1-pos-bool-enum-equality","rules":["OP-1","OP-7","OP-8","PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Bool participates in the exact tag-only eeq/ene domain for all four ordered operand pairs."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-distinct-enums","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Equal-width tag-only enum types remain nominally distinct operands of eeq."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-payload-enum","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A payload-carrying enum is outside the exact eeq domain. Its operands are moved, because a payload-carrying enum is affine and bare affine operands would earn the OWN-1 rejection first."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-integer","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"eeq does not accept an integer type argument."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ene-integer","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ene does not accept an integer type argument."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ieq-tag-enum","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`==` remains integer-only and does not admit a user tag-only enum."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ieq-bool","rules":["OP-1","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`==` remains integer-only and does not admit Bool."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ine-tag-enum","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`!=` remains integer-only and does not admit a user tag-only enum."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ine-bool","rules":["OP-1","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`!=` remains integer-only and does not admit Bool."}
{"id":"op1-neg-enum-ordering","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The enum equality family does not introduce enum ordering."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-eeq-implicit-type","rules":["FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A user-generic call stating no instantiation argument violates FN-2; instantiation arguments are always explicit. This row was repurposed in 2026-08-08 because table operations no longer carry that written argument, while the missing user-generic instantiation still needs an executable negative; the stable id is retained."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-wrong-kind-instantiation-argument","rules":["FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An instantiation argument of the wrong kind: a const where the declaration binds a type. The count and the explicitness FN-2 requires are both satisfied, and the type-kind spelling compiles, so the kind is the only thing varied."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-arity","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"eeq has exactly two operands."}
{"id":"op1-neg-written-argument-on-deargumented-row","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A1 deletes the written type argument from every row outside the retained-argument class, so writing one is an invalid operation. The operands are the domain eeq accepts, so the written argument is the sole violation; with operands the row rejects, the domain violation cites OP-1 with the same kind and masks it."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-requires-eeq","rules":["FN-8","OP-1","OP-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Exact-domain enum equality is pure and total, may form the complete requirement goal, and is discharged by structurally identical caller evidence without adding equality to L0."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-requires-eeq-integer","rules":["FN-8","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A requires clause cannot widen eeq to the integer domain."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-requires-move-operand","rules":["FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-8 names move in its own prohibition list. The operation, the domain and the clause shape are all legal here, and the same clause without move is accepted, so the moved operand is the sole violation."}
{"id":"x-nominal-bool-ops-run","rules":["OP-1","OP-8","PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every Boolean table operation executes through the ordinary compiler path, including true and false results."}
{"id":"x-nominal-multifield-payload-run","rules":["TYPE-2","GRAM-8","GRAM-10","OWN-13","OP-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A mixed-width three-field enum payload is constructed, passed across a function boundary, destructured, and validated field by field."}
{"id":"set1-pos-local-and-field-copy","rules":["SET-1","TYPE-5","STOR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SET-1 updates a copy local and nested copy field through statement- and value-match joins; the selected values and untouched sibling are preserved."}
{"id":"win3-pos-affine-set-target-releases-old-value","rules":["WIN-3","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Assigning over an owned affine place releases the old value [WIN-3]; a whole-binding set of a moved struct is an ordinary SET-1 assignment, so the program is accepted. Formerly stor1-neg-affine-set-target under v0.59, whose STOR-1 refusal of replacing a final affine owner left the specification with candidate x1."}
{"id":"win3-neg-arm-rest-covers-linear-payload","rules":["WIN-3","GRAM-10","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"WIN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A final `..` in an own-place arm would cover a linear payload, which has no release, so the arm is refused; the route is to bind the payload and consume it."}
{"id":"own1-neg-set-rhs-kills-root","rules":["OWN-1","SET-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A by-value parameter carries no effect entry, so the helper's row is pure; the commit still writes a root its own right-hand side consumed, which rejects under OWN-1."}
{"id":"const2-neg-set-target","rules":["CONST-2","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named constant is immutable static storage and cannot be selected as a set target."}
{"id":"x-array-const-checksum-run","rules":["CONST-1","CONST-2","TYPE-2","OP-1","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fixed immutable table feeds a loop-carried checksum through statically discharged bounds obligations and ordinary scalar-update paths."}
{"id":"x-array-mutable-checksum-run","rules":["TYPE-2","OP-1","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A mutable inline window is filled and folded through target-before-RHS checked indexed writes and ordinary checked reads."}
{"id":"x-buffer-mutable-checksum-run","rules":["GRAM-4","TYPE-9","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","SET-1","STOR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A heap-resident runtime-capacity run is taken from the one heap, filled, and folded through counted ranges whose binder facts prove every indexed access; normal owner exit releases the cell through the compiler-derived path."}
{"id":"x-struct-of-buffers-checksum-run","rules":["GRAM-4","TYPE-9","OWN-1","SET-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A struct owns two parallel heap-resident runs. Counted ranges prove every left-field access, ordinary branches prove the matching right-field accesses, and normal owner exit releases fields in reverse declaration order."}
{"id":"x-buffer-borrowed-columns-run","rules":["GRAM-4","FN-8","TYPE-7","OWN-7","EFF-2","EFF-5","SET-1","OP-4","OP-9","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Distinct projected run fields are handed to a fill helper and then to a fold helper as range references. Two field selections of different fields never overlap, so EFF-5 admits both calls; ordinary caller branches establish both declared length requirements, and counted-range binder facts combine with those requirements to prove every indexed access before execution."}
{"id":"x-requires-output-capacity-run","rules":["GRAM-4","FN-8","EFF-2","EFF-5","OP-4","SET-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Equal caller allocations discharge the output-capacity requirement before the call. In the callee, a counted range proves each source read, and that binder fact combines with the requirement's body-entry axiom to prove each output write; the two substituted effect paths have different roots, so EFF-5 admits the call. Explicit result controls observe the returned length and copied byte."}
{"id":"x-crc32-standard-vector-run","rules":["OP-6","OP-1","OP-4","SET-1","EFF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The standard CRC32 vector 123456789 computes 0xCBF43926 through checked byte-run access, the general total u8-to-u32 cvt path, and ordinary loop-carried integer operations."}
{"id":"x-option-byte-scanner-run","rules":["PRE-1","ERR-1","GRAM-8","GRAM-10","OWN-13","EFF-2","OP-4","SET-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range-reference byte scanner uses a counted range to prove every read in bounds and returns an owned index rather than a reference. Ordinary controls observe Some(3), the expected None result, and every wrong variant or offset through distinct nonzero statuses."}
{"id":"x-result-buffer-transform-run","rules":["PRE-1","ERR-1","GRAM-8","GRAM-10","OWN-1","OWN-13","OP-4","SET-1","STOR-3","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fallible fixed-size byte transform uses a counted range for matching input and output indices, then uses an observed length branch to prove fixed verification reads. Ordinary controls observe success length and bytes plus every unexpected Result variant, while failure and abandonment preserve active-variant cleanup."}
{"id":"x-borrowed-pool-tree-run","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-7","OWN-1","OWN-7","EFF-2","EFF-5","SET-1","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3","REF-4","ERR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A structure-of-arrays pool builds and recursively validates a complete binary tree through range references over two heap-resident runs plus a reference to a cursor struct. The three write paths have three distinct roots, so EFF-5 admits the builder's call; bounds and arithmetic failures use explicit typed PoolError results, main maps failures to nonzero statuses, and projected access, caller-visible storage, effects, and owner-only cleanup all use the normal compiler path."}
{"id":"x-wc-chunk-summary-run","rules":["TYPE-9","OWN-1","OWN-7","EFF-1","EFF-2","EFF-5","SET-1","OP-1","OP-4","STOR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two owned byte chunks are summarized and associatively combined through one output struct reference and two input struct references; whitespace state, early identity return, call-site disjointness of three distinct roots, caller-visible writes, discharged bounds obligations, and inline-run storage execute through the normal compiler path."}
{"id":"x-base64-rfc-vectors-run","rules":["CONST-1","CONST-2","FN-8","FN-9","ENT-3","OP-6","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","OWN-1","EFF-2","EFF-5","SET-1","STOR-3","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The complete scalar base64 encoder executes the RFC 4648 Man, M, and Ma vectors. Deterministic unsigned-remainder facts publish every table index bound; typed read/write helpers over range references consume those facts directly, out-of-range paths return IndexError, callers establish capacity by ordinary branches, the encoder's two range arguments have different roots so EFF-5 admits every call, and distinct status controls observe every encoded length and byte while preserving ownership cleanup."}
{"id":"x-child-reborrow-run","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","TYPE-7","OWN-7","EFF-2","EFF-5","SET-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference parameter passed onward through an ordinary helper call names the same path at both levels, projects the caller's effect footprint at each call, and remains usable for a later write in the same body; a heap-resident array range and an inline struct field both take that route."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-missing-binding","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An actual must bind every formal member exactly once in source order. FN-3 refuses the complete incomplete actual declaration before publishing a group."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-extra-binding","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An actual has an extra binding beyond its formal's complete member table; FN-3 refuses the extra member."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-out-of-order-binding","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A complete set of actual bindings in a different order from the formal member declarations rejects under FN-3."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-zero-argument-interface-import","rules":["FN-3","FN-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The interface marker imports a zero-header-argument group into a generic declaration. A concrete binding supplies the group at the call without repeating the marker."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-interface-import-forwarding","rules":["FN-3","FN-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nonempty interface import declares its captured binder once. Forwarding keeps the unmarked Identity<T> application, a concrete call keeps the binding name, and the qualified member call keeps its complete application."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-generic-formal","rules":["FN-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"D7 admits flat type/const parameters on a formal header; this replaces the retired source-contract-template refusal."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-contract-arguments","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An actual applies a zero-header-parameter formal to one type argument; FN-3 refuses the arity mismatch."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-source-contract-bound","rules":["FN-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Only Int and Float occupy the numeric-bound TYPEID position. A named formal group occupies its own generic-list position and is refused as a numeric bound under FN-3."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-normalized-region-effects","rules":["FN-1","FN-3","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Formal and actual rows compare by parameter ordinal and path rather than by binder spelling, so renaming both inputs preserves the boundary. The v0.59 case made the same point by reversing the read-set order; EFF-1 now fixes the canonical order as every reads entry before every writes entry in written argument order, so the reversal is spelled as a binder renaming instead."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-empty-marker-conformance","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A zero-member formal admits a matching empty named actual; expansion creates no runtime function or group object."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-returned-slice-inputs-run","rules":["FN-1","REF-3","REF-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference passes through one call and a two-supplier call reads either input. Selection moves inside the callee, because a reference is never handed back; the caller keeps both references live across the calls, which the read-only substituted rows permit."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-returned-slice-const-run","rules":["FN-1","REF-4","CONST-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference over immutable const storage is formed by the reader that uses it rather than returned by a supplier. The caller writes the range step and the callee's own len measure, equal to the written endpoints, discharges the requirement and reads the original bytes."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-returned-slice-arena-origin","rules":["FN-1","REF-3","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference formed over storage the callee owns cannot cross the return boundary. The v0.59 case blamed an arena's cleanup obligation; with one heap and compiler-derived release there is no cleanup obligation left, and the surviving refusal is REF-3's: a reference is never returned."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-borrowed-slice-result","rules":["GRAM-3","REF-3","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A result writes a value type [GRAM-3], so no reference kind derives in result position and a function cannot hand its range reference back [REF-3, FN-1]. The grammar rejects the reference marker without a result-provenance summary."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-contract-borrowed-slice-result","rules":["GRAM-3","REF-3","FN-1","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same reference-result refusal applies to a formal member's signature formation before any actual can bind it [FN-3]; a fn_sig result writes the same value type through rtype [GRAM-3]."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-function-region-bearing-targ","rules":["GRAM-3","TYPE-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference may be a directly written parameter but never a generic type argument: GRAM-3's targ alternatives contain value types and const expressions, with no reference-kind production [TYPE-8]."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-nominal-region-bearing-targ","rules":["GRAM-3","TYPE-2","TYPE-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Even an empty source nominal cannot be instantiated with a reference kind because GRAM-3 gives targ no reference-kind production, independently of stored-field analysis."}
{"id":"stor5-neg-box-new-region-bearing","rules":["STOR-8","OP-13","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Verdict kept, cited rule moved: the cell constructor accepts any ordinary operand type, but the store its content would occupy must exist. A compilation unit declaring no heap cannot call an allocating construction, and the refusal lands at the complete call exactly as the old operand rejection did."}
{"id":"stor5-neg-box-new-arena-content","rules":["STOR-8","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Verdict kept, cited rule moved: cell content must live in a store the program has. A compilation unit declaring no heap cannot name the cell type at all, so the refusal lands at the complete type where the content type is fixed, the companion position to the constructor-call refusal."}
{"id":"stor5-neg-arena-new-region-bearing","rules":["TYPE-9","OP-13","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Verdict kept, cited rule moved: regions are gone, so the surviving placement question is that a window's element position cannot hide a shape with no inline placement. A runtime-capacity window exists only as the content of a cell, and the complete written type is refused before any execution or lowering matters."}
{"id":"stor5-pos-direct-nested-slice-type","rules":["STOR-5","REF-4","TYPE-8","OP-11","OP-15","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference re-sliced from another range reference is type-formable and len may name its one measure in a value or effect path. Indexing a range of arrays selects one complete Array for operand-directed swap; no reference-kinded value is stored."}
{"id":"accept-sysentry-command-all-inputs","rules":["FN-7","EFF-2","PRE-2","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The launcher supplies one ordinary Inputs value and no heap value at all. Whole destructuring exposes ordinary fields; cwd is explicitly consumed through close_directory. There is no input-label order, system declaration domain or implicit native release."}
{"id":"accept-sysentry-command-no-inputs","rules":["FN-7","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary parameterless main calls the standard library's exit_status [PRE-2]. A program names a standard library declaration the same way whichever arguments a build adapter supplies."}
{"id":"fn7-neg-missing-command-marker","rules":["FN-7","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 retires the FN-7 missing-command-marker rejection. An ordinary function named main may return unit and has no entry-only signature restriction; this is an acceptance case, not a launcher execution claim."}
{"id":"reject-type5-system-arg-mismatch","rules":["TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A helper accepts the admitted opaque Args type, while its command caller passes unit. Exact argument conformance rejects the value-type mismatch under TYPE-5."}
{"id":"accept-sysname-near-lookalike","rules":["MOD-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A source function whose name extends a host function's (std::fs::open_read) is an ordinary distinct function and executes normally; the host function enters no inventory of a source that does not name it [MOD-10]."}
{"id":"reject-sysname-collision-in-kind-unit","rules":["TYPE-6","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The source declaration duplicates an ordinary PRE-1 function name. Ordinary TYPE-6 declaration inventory rejects the collision; there is no system domain or qualification rank."}
{"id":"reject-sysexit-return-u8-no-conversion","rules":["FN-7","FN-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary function promises ExitStatus but returns u8 without a declared conversion. FN-1 rejects that result type mismatch at the return statement, independently of the function name or any launcher."}
{"id":"reject-form1-service-leading-construct","rules":["FORM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The v0.33 entry grammar admits only the fixed `command` marker. A leading `service` spelling is an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1 before entry semantics."}
{"id":"reject-form1-embedded-leading-construct","rules":["FORM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The v0.33 entry grammar admits only the fixed `command` marker. A leading `embedded` spelling is an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1 before entry semantics."}
{"id":"reject-form1-daemon-leading-construct","rules":["FORM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The v0.33 grammar no longer admits an arbitrary IDENT as a program kind. A leading `daemon` spelling is therefore an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1."}
{"id":"reject-sysentry-label-out-of-order","rules":["FN-7","FN-1","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 deletes input labels and their FN-7 ordinal-order rejection. The same directory-before-arguments order is an ordinary parameter list; returning the directory satisfies its selected linearity. No launcher support for this arbitrary signature is asserted."}
{"id":"reject-sysentry-label-repeated","rules":["FN-7","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 deletes the repeated input-label rejection. Two distinct ordinary Args parameters are admitted by their modes and types; no input ordinal or implicit value duplication remains."}
{"id":"reject-sysentry-label-unknown","rules":["FN-7","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 removes the command input table and its unknown-label rejection. env is an ordinary parameter binder of the exact declared type; the case asserts source acceptance only."}
{"id":"reject-sysentry-label-outside-entry","rules":["FN-7","FN-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 deletes entry-only input labels. The helper receives an ordinary Args value with no function-name-dependent admission rule."}
{"id":"reject-sysentry-input-type-mismatch","rules":["FN-7","FN-1","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 retires the label-to-type table comparison. An ordinary parameter named args may have DirectoryRead type; returning that linear owner proves normal ownership rather than implicitly dropping it."}
{"id":"reject-sysentry-call-to-kind-entry","rules":["FN-7","FN-6","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 retires the FN-7 ban on source calls to main. The original recursive call is preserved and accepted as an ordinary call; the case is not executed, so no termination claim is made."}
{"id":"accept-sysrelease-return-unit-declared","rules":["PRE-2","PROV-6","EFF-2","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Release is an explicit ordinary consuming call: this function consumes ReadFile through close_read with its reference factory parameter, and the consumed by-value parameter contributes no effect entry of its own."}
{"id":"reject-syseff-return-unit-pure","rules":["PRE-2","PROV-6","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 retires the old missing-release-effect EFF-2 assertion. The original unconsumed ReadFile now rejects PROV-6 because its ordinary PRE-2 declaration is linear; no implicit native drop or writes(file) row supplies consumption."}
{"id":"reject-syseff-declared-unexhibited","rules":["EFF-2","EFF-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"args_count declares reads(args), so a caller that passes its own reference parameter on exhibits that read and a pure row omits it. A by-value parameter would contribute no entry at all, so the case roots the read at a reference parameter."}
{"id":"accept-syseff-conditional-release-union","rules":["PRE-2","EFF-2","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The explicit close occurs only in the Ok arm, but its factory access contributes syntactically to the complete body row even though Err calls nothing. The moved payload is local storage under EFF-2."}
{"id":"reject-syseff-conditional-release-narrow","rules":["PRE-2","EFF-2","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Release carries no effect entry of its own, which is exactly why this EFF-2 exact-row negative is about the explicit close instead: the pure row omits the ordinary factory access that call exhibits on the Ok branch, and every source occurrence contributes whether or not its branch is taken."}
{"id":"accept-syseff-pure-immutable-only","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary args_count signature reads the state its Args parameter supplies. The caller declares precisely that read, rooted at a reference parameter as EFF-1 requires; a by-value parameter would carry no effect entry at all."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-pure-member-binds-release","rules":["FN-4","EFF-2","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The actual explicitly calls close_read, and its declared ordinary effects exceed the pure formal row. No implicit finalizer supplies the difference, and release itself contributes no effect entry."}
{"reason":"A permission rule grants an implementation room to overlap sibling calls without changing source acceptance. Compiler actualization tests run the same emitted program at multiple worker counts and against sequential lowering, checking identical published bytes and typed call outcomes; conformance cases cover the ownership, loans, effects, and exact places from which the permission is derived. A corpus verdict cannot distinguish whether a valid permission was taken.","covered_by":"compiler-permission-judgment","rule":"PAR-1"}
{"reason":"PAR-2 permits optional overlap without changing source acceptance. Programs compare an independently evaluated wrapping fold with ordinary and parallel execution under default, disabled and representative worker settings. Compiler tests additionally observe a real worker callback and exact result for every native combine-table row, capture transport, owned and borrowed maps, and empty/inverted/thin ranges. The separate two-sided identity table covers every admitted signed/unsigned width and Bool; permission or an aggregate grant count alone does not establish that each lowered row executed.","covered_by":"compiler-permission-judgment","rule":"PAR-2"}
