{"id":"run-sysfile-empty","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","656d7074792e747874"],"files":[{"bytes":"","path":"656d7074792e747874"}]},"doc":"The ordinary read_at implementation returns Err(ReadEnd) for a nonempty range on an empty file. Every acquired linear owner is explicitly closed."}
{"id":"run-sysfile-short","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","73686f72742e747874"],"files":[{"bytes":"616263","path":"73686f72742e747874"}]},"doc":"The ordinary read_at implementation returns Ok(next) for a short transfer and Err(ReadEnd) only on a later read observing no byte. The successful endpoint enters proof through ordinary ensures."}
{"id":"run-sysfile-exact","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","65786163742e747874"],"files":[{"bytes":"6162636465666768","path":"65786163742e747874"}]},"doc":"The ordinary read_at implementation returns Ok(end) when the file fills the requested range; a later call observes Err(ReadEnd). Exact byte and endpoint observations are retained."}
{"id":"run-sysfile-multichunk","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","706167652e747874"],"files":[{"bytes":"303132333435363738396162636465666768696a","path":"706167652e747874"}]},"doc":"Source advances its explicit file offset across ordinary read_at calls. Ok payloads are absolute destination endpoints; the final short prefix and later Err(ReadEnd) preserve the original observations."}
{"id":"run-sysout-basic-write","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary write_once signature takes its output and shared invocation factory state by reference and declares the writes in its row. Its linked implementation makes one output attempt and returns the absolute accepted endpoint."}
{"id":"run-sysout-redirect-same-sink-order","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","636f6d62696e6564"],"redirect":{"stderr":"combined","stdout":"combined"}},"doc":"Output calls and the later file read reach the same HandleFactory state through one reference whose writes the rows declare. Ordinary EFF-2 and PAR-1 interference preserves the arranged shared sink order, including independently named aliased output streams; the readback observes the combined bytes."}
{"id":"form3-pos-index-ordinary-ident","rules":["FORM-3","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"index is not a fixed grammar atom, so it is an ordinary IDENT, declarable as a binding and usable as a subscript offset. The spellings FORM-3 now reserves are the four measures and the four window parts, and index is not one of them."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s11-counted-range-run","rules":["GRAM-4","GRAM-6","FN-1","ENT-3","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A normal counted form executes 0..4 as an ascending half-open range, executes 4..4 zero times, and honors a break naming the counted loop after one local iteration [FN-1, GRAM-4, GRAM-6]. On both entered bodies, S11 proves the binder below the captured upper constant and thereby discharges each constant-array subscript before lowering [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-counted-subscript-endpoint","rules":["ENT-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ENT-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each endpoint is a grammar-valid atom and each constant array subscript has its own discharged bounds obligation [OP-4], but ENT-2 admits only a preceding term or constant as a counted endpoint. A subscripted place is a term only when its final step selects a readonly field [ENT-2 clause (b)]; a scalar element is none, so it is rejected at the first complete endpoint atom with the prescribed let-binding restructuring."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-counted-measure-endpoint","rules":["ENT-2","ENT-3","MSR-1","FN-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A measure is an ENT-2 term (a clause (a) tracked place when unsubscripted, a clause (b) place when its measure place carries a subscript), so ENT-2 admits it directly as a counted endpoint with no preceding let: a range reference's len, a local run's len and cap in both endpoint positions, and a len whose measure place carries a discharged subscript. S11 equates each capture with its endpoint term, so the true-edge binder bound discharges every element read [ENT-3, OP-4]; the program observes the exact half-open iteration counts and element sums [FN-1]."}
{"id":"op4-neg-counted-measure-endpoint-shrunk-in-body","rules":["OP-4","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S11 equates the upper capture with run.len only in the preheader. take_back writes that length on a path reaching the next counted header, so ENT-5 removes the capture-to-endpoint equality from the counted head state and the element read has no bound against the current length; the second iteration would otherwise read past it."}
{"id":"set1-neg-counted-binder-write","rules":["SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SET-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The counted binder is immutable compiler-updated own u64 state. A source set targeting it is rejected under SET-1 at the complete binder place; the hidden normal-fallthrough increment grants no source writability."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-plain-direct-result","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified plain-result postcondition is published at a fresh direct ordinary let, and the earlier callee summary discharges the relay's selected return before ordinary result control observes the value."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-ok-selected-receiver","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified Ok payload relation is transferred by a direct call match and its immediate bare payload-to-outer write; the caller's selected return and ordinary result control observe the replaced value."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-no-selected-normal-exit","rules":["FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An Ok selector with only an Err normal return has no selected normal exit; FN-9 rejects at the selector with the residual no selected normal exit."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-unproved-selected-return","rules":["FN-9","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The first source-ordered selected return does not establish the declared output equality, so FN-9 rejects that unproved return even though the later return is discharged."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-entry-image-kill","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An overlapping write permanently invalidates the formal's entry image before the selected return; FN-9 rejects the resulting unproved output relation."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-same-scc-summary","rules":["FN-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A seedless mutually recursive component cannot use a same-SCC postcondition summary to prove either returned call result; an independently proved recursive control in the same unit shows recursion alone is not the rejection."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-stage8b-bit-sources","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unsigned iand is bounded by each of its operands, and a wrapping shift of a one by any amount is nonzero whether the one is a typed literal or an earlier named constant; exact requirements consume all four S7 operation facts."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-local-one-shift","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A local binding equal to one has the closed interval [1, 1], so the wrapping shift's S7 row bounds the result to [1, 2^31] whatever the amount, and the exact nonzero call requirement is proved without a typed-literal or named-constant spelling."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-shift-may-be-zero","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A wrapping shift of a value in [0, 1] has the S7 interval [0, 2^31], which contains zero, so the exact nonzero call requirement stays unproved and FN-8 rejects."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-arithmetic-rows","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The additive, product and saturating S7 rows reach their exact endpoints: sums, non-wrapping wrap forms, differences, products and clamped results are bounded by their operand intervals, and the offset and order relations relate each result to its operand terms."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-division-rows","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Division and remainder rows bound a quotient by the hull over the nonzero divisor parts and by its dividend, an unsigned remainder by its divisor and dividend, and a signed remainder by a runtime divisor in [2, 5] to [-4, 4]."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-bit-rows","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Bit rows bound their results: iand by each nonnegative operand, ior between its operands and the digit ceiling, ixor below that ceiling, inot as M + m - a, and a signed iand with one nonnegative mask converts to u8."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-shift-rows","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Shift rows bound right shifts by the corner hull and their value operand, left shifts that lose no bit, a wrapping shift of a one by any amount, and a wrapping right shift whose amount interval stays below the width."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-order-rows","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"imin and imax are bounded by and related to both operands, iabs and ineg of a guarded signed value reach their hull endpoints, and imulhi of u32 values is bounded by 2^32 - 2 and by each operand."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-count-rows","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ipopcount, iclz and ictz of a nonnegative operand interval are bounded by the operand's binary digits, and iclz of any u64 value by the width."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-reinterpret-and-single-values","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An integer reinterpret keeps an interval both types contain and moves a negative interval into the unsigned upper half, and a rotation of single values has its exact value, which indexes a nine-entry table."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-measure-operand","rules":["ENT-3","OP-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A length read directly as a remainder's divisor is its measure term, so the remainder's strict divisor relation proves the following subscript without binding the length first."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-interval-survives-operand-write","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A result interval names only the result and Z, so writing an operand afterwards leaves it, and a set commit publishes its right-hand side's row facts through the commit value."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-shift-right-one-past","rules":["ENT-3","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A u64 shifted right by 31 has the S7 interval [0, 2^33 - 1], one bit too wide for u32, so the narrowing conversion stays unproved."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-wrap-may-wrap","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A wrapping sum whose operand hull leaves u64 establishes neither an interval nor an offset relation, so the result is not known to be at least its first operand."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-signed-and-without-mask","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A signed iand whose operands may both be negative has no S7 condition met, so its result is not known to be nonnegative."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-shift-into-sign-bit","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Shifting a value in [0, 1] left by 31 at i32 reaches the sign bit, so the corner hull leaves the type and the row establishes nothing; the result is not known to be nonnegative."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-wrap-amount-past-width","rules":["ENT-3","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A wrapping right shift whose amount interval reaches 64 takes every masked amount in [0, 63], so the result may be the whole word and the narrowing conversion stays unproved."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-signed-remainder-one-past","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A signed remainder by a divisor in [2, 5] has the interval [-4, 4], so a requirement that it is at least -3 stays unproved."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-popcount-one-past","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The population count of a value in [0, 31] has the interval [0, 5], so a requirement that it is at most 4 stays unproved."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-reinterpret-mixed-signs","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An i8 interval containing both zero and negative values meets none of reinterpret's conditions, so the u8 result has only its type interval."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-unrepresentable-high-product","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The corner products of two full u64 intervals are not representable in i128, so imulhi establishes nothing and its exact bound 2^64 - 2 stays unproved."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-relation-dies-with-operand","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An order relation names its operand term, so writing that operand kills it; afterwards the iand result is not known to be at most the operand's new value."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s7-callee-in-another-module","rules":["ENT-3","CALL-4","FN-9","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A callee in another module proves its published bound through its own S7 ishr row, and the caller's S7 rows read that bound as the call result's closed interval, so a left shift of the result converts to u16 and a minimum with it converts to u8."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-s7-no-callee-contract-across-modules","rules":["ENT-3","CALL-4","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Without the callee's published bound nothing about its result crosses the module boundary, so the caller's left shift has only its type's interval and the conversion to u16 violates OP-6."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-checked-payload-facts","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each checked integer row's success payload receives the exact row's S7 facts through its conditional context: offsets for +checked and -checked, the product interval for *checked, the dividend order for /checked and %checked, and the hull for iabs.checked and ineg.checked."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-checked-product-one-past","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A checked product of two u8 values has the payload interval [0, 65025], so a requirement one below that endpoint stays unproved."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-direct-set-receiver","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A direct same-binding call result is established only after the target write; because the overwritten receiver formal is absent from the callee relation, the caller's selected return proves the replacement equality."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-named-outcome-publication","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named Result retains its verified conditional payload equality; selecting Ok discharges the ordinary call requirement."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-value-if-delivery-join","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each value_if edge forwards its deterministic signed-remainder range, the continuation joins the unequal bounds at their weakest common bound, and the exact requirement plus both runtime branches observe the delivered value."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-value-match-delivery","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A value-match forwards arm-local scalar bounds through give and joins them at the weakest common bound, discharging the continuation requirement."}
{"id":"set2-pos-affine-field-replace","rules":["OP-11","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The replace form is retired and the atomic exchange is swap: it exchanges the affine values at two writable places and consumes neither root, so both bindings stay live and each root becomes the sole owner of the value the other held. The previous field value is read back after the commit."}
{"id":"set2-neg-rhs-type-mismatch","rules":["OP-11","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The replace form is retired and the atomic exchange is swap, whose two places receive the same exact type judgment TYPE-5 states. An own Replacement place for an own Item place has no mode coercion, conversion, or target-selected overload, so the exchange is a hard error citing TYPE-5."}
{"id":"set2-pos-box-descriptor-replace","rules":["OP-11","STOR-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The positive control for the exchange at an owning descriptor: a Box<u64> descriptor is affine and holds no reference, so swap exchanges the two owning descriptors, leaves both roots live, and makes each root the sole owner of the cell the other held."}
{"id":"op2-pos-overflow-obligation-discharged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bare addition with a constant operand carries an OP-2 overflow obligation. A dominating branch establishes the residual upper bound, so the site returns the exact value with no runtime overflow check and the function's effect row is pure."}
{"id":"op2-neg-overflow-obligation-undischarged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A constant-operand-class call whose fact state does not discharge the obligation is a compile-time rejection citing OP-2 at its infix node, carrying the residual per ENT-6. Repairs include a dominating branch, requirement, invariant, checked source proof, or the explicit wrap, checked, or sat spelling."}
{"id":"op2-pos-wrap-untouched-by-dissolution","rules":["OP-2","OP-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The arithmetic-mode obligation attaches to the bare constant-operand class only. Wrapping addition is total, carries no obligation, needs no discharge, and contributes nothing to the effect row, so an unbounded operand is valid."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind-called","rules":["FN-1","REF-3","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The still-illegal form with a call site present. The callee hands a reference out of its frame, which REF-3 refuses at the returned borrow expression; the caller is retained deliberately, so the case pins that the declaration is still the reported error when a call exists, while fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind pins the same declaration with no caller at all."}
{"id":"const1-pos-forwarded-arithmetic","rules":["CONST-1","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One-operation const arithmetic: a const-expression is at most one operation over two terms. n * 2 is usable as a Slots<T, n> capacity and forwardable as a const targ, and each concrete instantiation evaluates it to one u64 value at monomorphization."}
{"id":"const1-neg-eval-overflow","rules":["CONST-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CONST-1 evaluates constants exactly in the unsigned 64-bit domain. This multiplication leaves that domain and is rejected at the complete const node; an accepted const expression creates no runtime operation."}
{"id":"const1-neg-runtime-arithmetic-mode","rules":["CONST-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The const-expression tail reuses infix_op but its spelling must be one of the five bare operators. Const evaluation has no runtime overflow mode, so a mode-suffixed spelling is a hard error citing CONST-1 at the infix_op node; the grammar admits and the checker restricts."}
{"id":"const2-pos-struct-const","rules":["CONST-2","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CONST-2 struct-typed consts: a source struct whose every field type is const-eligible is const-eligible. The cvalue takes the construction form naming its exact struct and writing every declared field in declared order, the const is laid out as one read-only static aggregate, and it is read via field suffixes exactly as subscript reads."}
{"id":"const2-neg-enum-not-const-eligible","rules":["CONST-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CONST-2 const-eligibility is a closed list of primitives, arrays of const-eligible T, and source non-opaque structs of const-eligible fields. Enums are not const-eligible, so an enum-typed const is rejected."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-obligation-discharged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unsigned bare division carries the two-conjunct OP-2 domain obligation. A dominating branch establishes a nonzero divisor, both conjuncts discharge, and the accepted site keeps no runtime zero-divisor check, so the function is pure."}
{"id":"op2-neg-division-obligation-undischarged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same unsigned division with nothing proving the divisor nonzero rejects at its infix node with residual d != 0. A missing proof never creates a runtime fallback."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-requires-nonzero","rules":["OP-2","FN-8","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nonzero function requirement is proved at the call site and discharges the callee's exact division obligation. The closed program observes 84 / 2 as 42."}
{"id":"op2-pos-unsigned-literal-division-images","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","FN-8","FN-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Unsigned division by the positive literal 2 publishes quotient <= count and 2 * quotient <= count for the exact runtime values. The first image proves one callee requirement; the scaled image admits the exact multiplication, proves the other requirement and the postcondition, and the closed call observes 2 * floor(9 / 2) as 8."}
{"id":"op2-neg-division-constant-zero-divisor","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An integer-typed named const reads as an ENT-2 constant, so the site is in the divisor class, and its zero divisor is a ground false conjunct rejected at every non-contradictory point. The always-invalid bare spelling is never accepted."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-constant-divisor-total","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nonzero constant divisor that is not -1 discharges both conjuncts with no fact source at all, so a signed n / 2_i32 is total and pure and emits one plain instruction."}
{"id":"op2-neg-division-minus-one-divisor-unbounded","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The one expressible signed-overflow shape: n / -1_i32 leaves the second conjunct as the residual `n != -2147483648` and rejects at the infix node."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-minus-one-divisor-bounded","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same -1 divisor with a dominating branch bounding the dividend above i32::MIN discharges both conjuncts and keeps no runtime test."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-remainder-same-obligation","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"% carries the identical obligation as / and discharges from the identical fact: one obligation family covers both spellings."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-checked-untouched","rules":["OP-2","OP-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The dissolution attaches obligations to the bare divisor class only. /checked is untouched: it needs no discharge, stays pure, and returns Err(DivideByZero()) for a zero divisor."}
{"id":"eff2-pos-discharged-division-site-pure-row","rules":["EFF-2","OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The exact division domain is discharged statically [EFF-2, OP-2, ENT-6], so the operation has no runtime fallback and pure is the exact effect row."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind","rules":["FN-1","REF-3","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two indistinguishable reference parameters, and the result still cannot be one of them: REF-3 rejects at the returned borrow expression with no caller anywhere in the program. This is the case fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind-called cannot carry: it proves the declaration alone is the error."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-result-provenance-other-kind","rules":["FN-1","REF-3","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two reference parameters differ only in what the row grants, one written and one only read. Neither difference makes either a candidate to hand back: a function returns owned values only [FN-1] and REF-3 refuses the returned reference itself, with the restructuring 'return an index and let the caller form the reference'. The v0.59 rejection was FN-1's borrow-result provenance judgment, which has no successor; the surviving question is who may leave the frame, and REF-3 owns it."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-result-provenance-distinct-regions","rules":["FN-1","REF-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The accepted repair stated in v0.60 vocabulary: one parameter is the place written and the other the place read, declared by the effect row rather than by a mode marker, and the write through the reference reaches exactly the place the caller named when it formed it."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-result-provenance-zero-candidate","rules":["FN-1","CONST-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The zero-input arm of FN-1's result judgment on the formal-member surface. No parameter supplies the result, which is legal by elimination: named const storage is the only remaining source and it is read by value, so no borrow-provenance question arises at all."}
{"id":"sys14-list-outcome-exhaustive","rules":["ERR-2","PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"bytes":"78","path":"612e747874"},{"directory":true,"path":"737562"}]},"doc":"directory_next returns an ordinary Result<unit,ListStop>, numeric endpoint and entry count. Complete Ok and nested ListEnd/ListFailed matches are exhaustive under ERR-2; the fixture reaches a successful batch. The endpoint contract is unconditional and follows its ordinary numeric result."}
{"id":"reject-sys14-list-end-beyond-buffer","rules":["ERR-4","PRE-2","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The end 4104 exceeds the 4096-element destination range. FN-8 rejects directory_next through its ordinary end <= deref(destination).len requirement, stated over the range reference's single measure."}
{"id":"sys14-list-zero-range","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"bytes":"78","path":"612e747874"},{"directory":true,"path":"737562"}]},"doc":"The ordinary directory_next library operation returns Ok(start) and zero entries for an empty range, leaving enumeration available for a later transfer."}
{"id":"sys14-open-directory-component","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary open_directory implementation rejects a name containing a separator with InvalidPath. The supplied factory remains available on refusal; no permit object or implicit close is involved."}
{"id":"sys14-open-directory-empty-name","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary open_directory implementation rejects an empty component with InvalidPath. The original valid backing bytes and empty selected range distinguish this library outcome from a static range failure."}
{"id":"sys14-open-directory-success","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"directory":true,"path":"737562"}]},"doc":"The ordinary open_directory operation returns a linear DirectoryRead for an existing component named by a range reference over a window; the program uses and explicitly closes the returned owner through its factory."}
{"id":"sys14-list-handle-affine","rules":["OWN-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary DirectorySource is non-copy and linear. The original duplicate use remains an OWN-1 rejection; selected linearity additionally requires explicit consumption on accepted paths."}
{"id":"sys14-list-handle-unique","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Verdict kept, cited rule moved to EFF-5: there is no exclusive borrow mode, and advancing an enumeration is declared as a write in the callee row. The overlapping-argument subject survives as the pairwise call-site check, which refuses one call whose two substituted source write paths are the same place."}
{"id":"sys14-no-path-from-bytes","rules":["TYPE-5","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary relative_path parameter is HostString. Passing a run of u8 is a normal FN-1 argument type mismatch; no path-from-bytes conversion is declared."}
{"id":"sys14-directory-release","rules":["PRE-2","PROV-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"bytes":"78","path":"612e747874"},{"directory":true,"path":"737562"}]},"doc":"DirectoryRead and DirectorySource are ordinary linear opaque values. The program explicitly closes both acquired values through their ordinary factory parameter instead of relying on retired implicit native release."}
{"id":"sys14-entry-kind-closed","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"bytes":"78","path":"612e747874"},{"directory":true,"path":"737562"}]},"doc":"The ordinary enumeration library emits records with one kind byte, a little-endian u16 name length and the name bytes. The program checks the selected record format, kind range and announced entry count without a special language operand class."}
{"id":"accept-sysfile-two-permits-shared-directory","rules":["OWN-1","EFF-5","PAR-1","PRE-2","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two ordinary opens share one DirectoryRead reference and write one HandleFactory in successive statements; read/read overlap is admitted and both linear DirectorySource results are explicitly closed. The former two-permit overlap assertion is retired."}
{"id":"reject-sysfile-permit-used-twice","rules":["OWN-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The witness duplicates consumption of an ordinary linear acquired owner and must still reject OWN-1: after a consuming use the whole binding is dead. Factory state is referenced, not consumed or duplicated."}
{"id":"v033-neg-missing-result-binding","rules":["FORM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every function result is writer-named at the declaration boundary. A bare result type after `->` is not an alternate anonymous form, so parsing rejects this declaration at that type before any body judgment."}
{"id":"v033-pos-shared-contract-define","rules":["FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One erased, pure contract definition is visible to both a requirement and an ensured normal-return relation. It is alpha-expanded independently at the caller and return edge, creates no runtime storage, and is not visible in the function body."}
{"id":"v033-pos-uninhabited-contract","rules":["FN-8","ENT-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Contradictory independent requirements make this concrete function legally uninhabited. The contradictory entry state discharges its deliberately impossible subscript without making the function callable from a reachable state; ordinary IR tests separately pin unreachable lowering."}
{"id":"v033-neg-exact-domain-unproved","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A two-variable exact addition has one IntegerDomain obligation whose canonical predicate is `left +defined right`. With no dominating checked fact or source proof, the operation is rejected statically; exact arithmetic has no implicit runtime-overflow fallback."}
{"id":"s7-pos-signed-remainder-ranges-run","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-6","OP-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Signed exact remainders by fixed nonzero divisors publish their deterministic result intervals. The affine domain checker combines those intervals to prove the following exact addition, which computes 42."}
{"id":"v033-neg-allocation-fit-unproved","rules":["OP-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A runtime window capacity with no established relation leaves OP-9's allocation-size obligation unproved. OP-9 rejects the construction function at its source node before lowering and creates no runtime size check."}
{"id":"v033-run-system-nonzero-next","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","4142"]},"doc":"Ordinary copy function results use absolute endpoints for a nonzero start. Source receives their bounds through ordinary Result.Ok ensures, with the original byte observations retained."}
{"id":"v033-run-open-file-regular","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"bytes":"78","path":"6974656d"}]},"doc":"The ordinary open_file implementation opens the selected regular entry and read_at observes its contents through a range reference; ordinary Result outcomes, explicit factory effects and consuming close replace native special rules."}
{"id":"v033-run-open-file-directory","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"files":[{"directory":true,"path":"6974656d"}]},"doc":"The ordinary open_file implementation reports IsDirectory for the selected directory. The caller matches the normal error outcome and retains its factory after refusal."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-iteration-own-scratch","rules":["PAR-1","EFF-5","EFF-2","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each iteration owns its own scratch run: the run is built inside the scan body, written through a range reference, and released at the end of that iteration."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-loop-with-prologue-break","rules":["GRAM-4","EFF-5","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary loop with a prologue break: the counter is advanced before the body opens anything, so the loop leaves through break with every handle already closed."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-denied-hoisted-scratch","rules":["EFF-5","PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One scratch run is hoisted out of the scan: each iteration writes it through a range reference handed to read_at and then reads it through a second range reference. Ordinary references, requirement discharge and published relations carry the case."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-denied-read-before-write","rules":["EFF-5","PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The scratch run is read through one range reference before it is written through another in the same iteration, so the read precedes the write in source order and both are ordinary call-site effects."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-denied-carried-scratch-byte","rules":["EFF-5","EFF-2","PRE-2","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One scratch byte is carried across the whole scan: the same window is written through one range reference and read through another, and every open is closed on its own arm. The former staged grant or numbered denial is retired, not reproduced; ordinary references, call-site effect comparison, loop ownership and exits still apply."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-denied-opaque-cursor","rules":["EFF-5","PRE-2","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One opaque directory cursor is held across the whole scan while each iteration hands directory_next a fresh listing run through a range reference. Ordinary linear consumption and call-site effect comparison carry the case."}
{"id":"accept-par3-staged-denied-exit-in-remainder","rules":["FN-1","PRE-2","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every arm of the scan leaves by its own exit: the refusing arm returns two distinct codes and the opening arm closes its handle before the next iteration. Ordinary source acceptance and source-order control flow survive the explicit factory and linear-close migration."}
{"id":"gram5-pos-comparison-operators","rules":["GRAM-5","OP-1","OP-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The six integer comparisons are `compare_op` infix expressions over two atoms, each an integer-only table row, and a call writes its type arguments after the `::` delimiter; every comparison holds on the ordered pair and the program exits 0."}
{"id":"gram5-neg-type-application-without-delimiter","rules":["GRAM-5","DIAG-1","FORM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A call's type arguments are written after the `::` delimiter. Spelled bare, `cvt<u8, u32>(narrow)` begins the comparison `cvt < u8` [GRAM-5], and the derivation fails at `u8` where a comparison operand atom must stand; DIAG-1 row 3 attributes that keyword, which has IDENT's raw shape and fails only its fixed-atom exclusion, to FORM-3."}
{"id":"gram4-neg-multiplied-use-relation-bare","rules":["GRAM-4","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A relation premise is delimited. Written bare, `use 3 times first + second <= ...` does not derive: after the multiplicity the grammar admits a named invariant or a delimited relation and nothing else."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-multiplied-relation-use","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A certificate scales a relation-form premise by an explicit factor of three, `use 3 times (first + second <= first_limit + second_limit)`; the parenthesized relation is itself proved by AUTO from the two requirements and the scaled sum closes the target."}
{"id":"run-sysfile-close-returns-permit","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","616761696e2e747874"],"files":[{"bytes":"41","path":"616761696e2e747874"}]},"doc":"Explicit close_read consumes a linear file and restores the ordinary factory credit; the next open through that same factory succeeds and reads the original byte."}
{"id":"run-sysfile-failed-open-returns-permit","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"argv":["63617365","6d697373696e672e747874","616761696e2e747874"],"files":[{"bytes":"41","path":"616761696e2e747874"}]},"doc":"An ordinary failed open preserves the factory credit; a subsequent open of an existing name through the same factory succeeds and reads its first byte. No returned permit type remains."}
{"id":"form8-pos-elided-regions-run","rules":["REF-1","EFF-1","FN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The v0.59 case ran every elided region spelling; there is nothing left to elide, and the surviving question is what a reference is written with. It composes and runs: a bare & borrow expression, a reference-kind local naming that path, a call passing it, and a bare read of the referent, with write authority stated by the row alone and no region block, region name or type application anywhere."}
{"id":"form8-pos-related-pair-written","rules":["FN-1","REF-3","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The one relation the v0.59 rule kept writable was result-borrows-parameter, and it has no form here: a function returns owned values only, so the referent is read out by value and only the read is stated, by the row. The caller forms both references and writes no type application at the call because there is none to write."}
{"id":"own11-pos-loop-body-region","rules":["OWN-11","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A loop body is an ordinary block whose own bindings begin and end with one iteration, so a reference formed directly in it is per-iteration and is written bare with no enclosing construct at all. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"ent6-pos-nested-join-keeps-the-flat-image","rules":["ENT-6","INV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ENT-6 positive: a three-way demux written as nested conditionals passes through two joins in sequence. Each input image is normalized by folding the earlier join's delta atom back into its constant interval, so the second join reaches the image one flat join reaches and every per-iteration header invariant is preserved. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"ent6-pos-join-shape-independence","rules":["ENT-6","INV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ENT-6 positive pair: the same four-lane branch set written as one flat `match` and as three-level nested conditionals reaches the same joined image, so acceptance does not depend on the shape of the control join. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"ent6-neg-nested-join-does-not-invent-a-bound","rules":["ENT-6","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ENT-6 negative: one nested arm advances the accumulator by two, so no per-iteration bound of one is preserved on every path. Folding an earlier join's delta into its interval widens nothing, and the backedge obligation is still refused citing INV-1."}
{"id":"msr5-pos-two-measure-clause","rules":["MSR-5","GRAM-5","FN-8","ENT-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A requires clause names a measure of a place directly on both sides of one comparison and again against a literal, with no contract definition in the block. A measure now reaches the clause as an ordinary place atom and needs no former."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-own-operand-call-datum","rules":["MSR-3","CALL-6","ENT-3","FN-9","MSR-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ensures names the measure of an own parameter the call consumes. The operand denotes that call's call datum, a compiler-owned term with empty support, so the published relation survives the consume the same statement performs and the caller discharges a later requirement from it."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-uniq-state-measure-in-ensures","rules":["MSR-3","FN-9","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"MSR-3 admits a source ensures over the measure of a reference parameter whose row writes that path: the bare measure denotes the exit-state term over the caller's resolved place and entry(parameter) denotes the immutable entry datum. The unmarked reference plus a writes row is what replaced the retired unique borrow mode, and the judgment is unchanged."}
{"id":"call6-neg-contradictory-published-relations","rules":["CALL-6","FN-9","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CALL-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two ensures clauses publish relations that cannot hold together. At a contradictory point every relation and both signs of every goal are derivable, so an inconsistent contract is not one wrong fact at one caller but every fact at every caller; the set is fixed at the declaration and refused there."}
{"id":"call6-pos-routed-relation-over-a-call-datum","rules":["CALL-6","MSR-3","FN-9","ENT-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A variant-routed ensures names a consumed own operand's measure. The relation is instantiated at the call over that call's call datum and established at the selected arm, where the caller reads it to discharge a later requirement."}
{"id":"call4-neg-measured-result-not-admitted","rules":["CALL-4","FN-9","MSR-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ensures names a measure of a result of measured type. MSR-5 makes the clause parse and CALL-4 admits both the measured result and the measure over its bare result place, so what refuses the declaration is the ordinary FN-9 proof: the parameter arrives with no known length and the relation has no premise at the selected return."}
{"id":"s16-pos-result-list-reaches-both-let-binders","rules":["GRAM-2","GRAM-4","FN-1","TYPE-5","CALL-4","ENT-3","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A declaration writes an ordered result list and a caller binds it with a destructuring let. Each binder takes its own result ordinal, and the relation published over that ordinal is established there, so both binders discharge a later requirement the caller could not otherwise prove."}
{"id":"s16-pos-result-list-reaches-both-set-targets","rules":["GRAM-4","SET-1","TYPE-5","CALL-4","ENT-3","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The multi-target set form is retired and one statement is one commit, so the two result ordinals reach two separate set targets through the destructuring let's binders. Each commit establishes its own post-write equality, and each target then carries its own ordinal's published relation to the later requirement."}
{"id":"call4-pos-route-names-a-result-ordinal","rules":["CALL-4","FN-9","GRAM-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A routed ensures names the result ordinal the route applies to, and an unrouted clause names another ordinal of the same declaration. The unrouted relation reaches its binder at the destructuring let while the routed one stays restricted to its own arm."}
{"id":"call4-pos-omitted-route-binder-with-one-enum-ordinal","rules":["CALL-4","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A route omits its ordinal binder where exactly one declared result ordinal has the route's enum type. That is the one case the omission is admitted in, and it is what keeps a single-result declaration writing no ordinal binder."}
{"id":"call4-neg-ambiguous-route-over-two-enum-ordinals","rules":["CALL-4","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CALL-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two declared result ordinals have the route's enum type and the route omits its ordinal binder. The route is one no reader can attribute to one ordinal, and the ordinal set is fixed at the declaration, so the declaration is refused there rather than at a caller."}
{"id":"call4-pos-result-field-measure-reaches-the-caller","rules":["CALL-4","FN-9","MSR-3"],"expect":{"exit":7,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ensures names a measure reached from the result place through a struct field. CALL-4 admits the owned descendant projection, the returned move proves it, and the relation instantiated at the caller's binder discharges the caller's element read."}
{"id":"call4-neg-result-payload-measure-projection","rules":["CALL-4","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ensures names a measure reached from the result place through an enum-payload step. No route selects a variant of an unrouted result, so CALL-4 admits no such member and the clause is refused under FN-9."}
{"id":"call1-pos-a-shared-borrow-keeps-every-fact","rules":["CALL-1","EFF-2","ENT-5","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A shared-reference argument is a kill event for no fact supported by its actual's place. The declared row carries no write of any path rooted at that parameter, so a body can exhibit none and EFF-2's both-ways check admits none; the length the caller established survives two calls and discharges the requirement of each."}
{"id":"call2-pos-an-own-operand-measure-reaches-the-result","rules":["CALL-2","MSR-3","CALL-6","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An own argument of affine class is a consuming use, and the result carries exactly the callee's declared relations. The declared relation names the consumed operand's measure, which at the caller denotes that call's call datum; a datum has empty support, so the consume the same statement performs cannot kill it and the caller reads the length across the transport."}
{"id":"call2-neg-a-result-carries-only-the-contract","rules":["CALL-2","ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The callee declares no relation, so no fact of any argument reaches the result: it is a fresh binding and the caller knows nothing about its length. The subscript into the shorter run the callee actually returned is rejected at OP-4 with the residual 1_u64 < got.len."}
{"id":"call2-pos-a-copy-actual-at-an-own-parameter-is-not-a-consume","rules":["CALL-2","OWN-1","ENT-5","PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CALL-2 makes an own argument a consuming use only where its type is affine or linear. u64 is copy, so handing the saved bound at an own parameter is a duplicate: the caller's place and every fact supported by it survive, and a second call proves its own requirement from the same fact a consume would have killed."}
{"id":"call3-pos-a-fill-through-an-exclusive-view-keeps-both-lengths","rules":["CALL-3","REF-4","SET-1","MSR-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A projected callee write through a range reference reaches the range's element storage and no measure term over the origin place nor over the range reference. The helper fills its &[u8] destination; the caller keeps window.len and table.len across the call and reads back the bytes the callee wrote, and a reference that is itself an argument does not invalidate itself."}
{"id":"call5-neg-a-bound-borrow-actual-kills-the-same","rules":["CALL-5","EFF-5","ENT-5","FN-8","MSR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"No transport reads the actual's spelling. With the reference bound to a name and handed at the argument position, the two spellings differ in nothing a transport may read, so the declared whole-state write kills the caller's length exactly as the directly spelled one does and take_back's requires window.len > 0_u64 is undischarged."}
{"id":"msr1-pos-the-four-measure-readers","rules":["MSR-1","OP-15","TYPE-10","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A measure read is a place form and not a call, and the table gives each measured type exactly the cells it has: a Slots has len and cap, a Ring adds head, and a range reference has len alone. The program reads every measure, computes free slots as cap - len, and observes those values over a constant-capacity window, a boxed runtime-capacity window, a ring and a range reference."}
{"id":"msr1-pos-subscript-obligation-against-len","rules":["MSR-1","MSR-2","OP-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscript offset is a logical offset naming the slot at physical offset (origin + i) mod cap, so its OP-4 obligation is against len and never against cap. A Slots window begins at slot zero, so the map is the identity and the injectivity sentence's conclusion follows immediately from len <= cap, which MSR-2 publishes as a standing fact rather than as an obligation any occurrence submits. The wrapped case belongs to Ring, whose head cell is the one bounded cell of the table."}
{"id":"msr2-pos-sibling-field-write-keeps-the-measure","rules":["MSR-2","ENT-5","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The support of a measure term over P is P's descriptor storage rather than P's root binding, so a SET-1 commit to a sibling field of the same struct overlaps neither that storage nor any holder reached to it and the length fact survives the write."}
{"id":"msr2-pos-element-write-keeps-the-measure","rules":["MSR-2","ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write at an element position of P overlaps the descriptor storage of the written element and none of P's own. An element write of a scalar kills nothing at all because a scalar has no measures: the killed set is empty rather than excepted, and the later subscript still discharges from the length taken before the write."}
{"id":"msr2-neg-descriptor-write-kills-the-measure","rules":["MSR-2","ENT-5","OP-11","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write to P's own descriptor storage kills every measure of P. The swap exchanges the whole ten-slot window for one holding two slots, which overlaps the storage the length fact is supported by, so the fact does not survive it and the later subscript of offset nine has nothing to discharge against. This is the other side of the element-position sentence."}
{"id":"msr4-pos-capacity-requirement-discharges-a-length-obligation","rules":["MSR-4","MSR-2","OP-4","FN-8","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every consumer submits its normalized goal to one ordered disposition. The callee's requirement is stated over run.cap and the subscript's OP-4 obligation over run.len in logical coordinates, and MSR-2's standing identity len + (cap - len) = cap relates them in the automatic affine-premise sequence. The caller discharges both requirements from the counted fill's own invariants."}
{"id":"msr6-pos-const-generic-as-a-value","rules":["MSR-6","TYPE-6","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An in-scope const generic is a value wherever a named const is: one pbase with no suffix, whose exact type is the gparam's written integer type and whose value is fixed at [FN-2] instantiation. Before this rule the same spelling was a [TYPE-5] unresolved use reporting available: [ConstGeneric], so a capacity-parametric function could not name its own bound."}
{"id":"msr6-pos-const-generic-in-a-clause-and-an-endpoint","rules":["MSR-6","ENT-2","MSR-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The pbase admission carries the const generic into the two other positions a named const already reaches: the for_stmt endpoint and the clause operand. The requirement names the parameter, the counted loop's upper endpoint is the same parameter, and the subscript at that offset discharges from the requirement after instantiation folds the parameter to its mathematical constant. The result binder is renamed because last is now reserved."}
{"id":"liv1-neg-branches-disagree-on-liveness","rules":["LIV-1","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"LIV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Liveness is a property of a program point, not of a path: one branch consumes the window and the other does not, so the two predecessors of the join disagree about whether the binding is live there. The rejection names the binding and both predecessors."}
{"id":"liv1-pos-loop-moves-and-restores-an-outer-binding","rules":["LIV-1","OWN-11","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OWN-11's per-iteration judgment is LIV-1's agreement read at a loop head: the body consumes a binding declared outside it and commits a fresh value back into it before the backedge, so the backedge carries the same status as the entering edge. The reinitializing commit is cited to SET-1, which is what took over the declaring-set rule's surviving half."}
{"id":"liv1-neg-loop-leaves-an-outer-binding-dead","rules":["OWN-11","LIV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The other side of the per-iteration judgment: the body consumes the outer binding and commits nothing back, so the backedge leaves it dead while the entering edge has it live and a second iteration would start in a state the first did not leave. OWN-11 owns this instance and cites itself at the loop."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-read-out-at-a-binding-a-field-and-a-deref","rules":["OP-12","SET-1","TYPE-7","STOR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One atomic in-place update at each of the three place shapes a commit reinitializes: a bare binding, a field of a struct, and Box content reached through a reference parameter whose row writes that path. The old value enters the callee by value and the callee's result is committed, so the place owns exactly one valid value at every program point."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-read-out-keeps-the-root-and-its-other-fields","rules":["OP-12","OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":3,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The atomic in-place update at one field is not the partial consume that kills the whole root. The struct's other field is read after the commit and reported in the exit code, which no dead root admits, and no residual cleanup of the unselected content is derived."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-swap-and-rotation","rules":["OP-11","SET-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"exit":5,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"swap exchanges the values at two owned places of one type, consuming neither root, so the exchange the retired multi-target commit spelled is one built-in call. A swap over a copy place is refused with the restructuring that reads the two values and assigns them back, which is what the copy pair does here and what the exit code reports; three affine windows are then rotated by two swaps."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-proved-dynamic-indices-swap-affine-elements","rules":["OP-11","OWN-7","ENT-6","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A helper contract proves left < right, so OWN-7 separates the two captured runtime offsets and EFF-5 admits the call's two substituted write paths. swap exchanges the elements without consuming either root, a write at an element position kills no measure of the window, and the executed result confirms the exchange."}
{"id":"own1-neg-an-unproved-candidate-index-pair-does-not-separate-a-cross-path","rules":["OWN-1","SET-1","OWN-7","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Index separation never keeps a later subscript live: one consuming use kills the whole binding that roots the place, so even a proved distinct candidate index pair leaves the second element read rooted in a dead binding."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-two-fields-of-one-root","rules":["SET-1","OWN-7","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"exit":9,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two distinct fields of one root are non-overlapping places, so each takes its own commit and neither disturbs the other or kills the root. A set target that names a binding in scope is a commit and never a declaration, which is now the only thing a set target can be."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-subscript-targets-commit-their-own-ordinals","rules":["SET-1","OP-4","CALL-4"],"expect":{"exit":2,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One commit writes one place, and a subscript in a target is an ordinary target: its base and offset are evaluated during target evaluation and its OP-4 obligation is judged exactly as in read position. The two ordinals of one call land at the binders of a destructuring let, and each binder is then committed at its own window slot."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-dispose-runs-the-release-walk-early","rules":["PROV-6","STOR-3","LIV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Moving a value into a function that consumes it and ends runs, at that call, exactly the release walk the scope exit would have run. Two windows are reclaimed one after the other rather than both at the entry's own exit edge, which is the difference between a peak of one window and a peak of two."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-dispose-writes-the-operands-storage-origin","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","STOR-8","STOR-3"],"expect":{"exit":9,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Allocation and release carry no effect entry, so the early reclamation and the derived one are equally invisible in a signature: one function hands its cell to a consumer and the other lets it reach the scope exit, and both rows are pure. The exit code is the sum of what they returned, so a reclamation that ran twice or not at all reports."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-dispose-without-the-declared-write","rules":["EFF-2","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same body with the row left pure. EFF-2 checks rows both ways, so the access the body exhibits through its reference parameter must be declared; before that access existed the same function exhibited nothing and pure was correct."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-linear-value-not-consumed","rules":["PROV-6","LIV-1","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A value linear in this scope is live on the edge leaving it, where no compiler-derived release exists to carry it. The `linear` modifier states a logical obligation holding in every scope, so the rejection names the binding and the declaration that created the obligation and offers exactly the two routes that remain."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-linear-value-moved-out-whole","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":3,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The first of the two routes out of a scope for a value linear in it: moved out whole. The lease is moved into a callee that moves it out again, so no edge of either scope carries a derived release of it, and the final destructuring consume is where the obligation is discharged."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-destructuring-consume-discharges-the-obligation","rules":["PROV-6","GRAM-4","TYPE-5","GRAM-10"],"expect":{"exit":7,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"`let N(f1: b1, ..., fk: bk) = move v;` consumes a value of nominal struct type whole and binds every declared field in declaration order to a fresh IDENT at that field's declared type and `own` mode. No residual of the consumed value survives it, so it is the second route out of a scope for a linear value, and its binders are ordinary own bindings of the enclosing block."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-linear-value-partially-consumed","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","WIN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A consume of a proper sub-place of a value one of whose remaining parts is linear abandons the residual the obligation was written for. The refusal is stated over the consume, so it reaches every consuming use of that sub-place, and its restructuring is the destructuring consume."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-partial-consume-cannot-abandon-a-symbolically-linear-member","rules":["PROV-6","WIN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The linearity predicate a partial consume reads includes a symbolic type parameter's written linear bound. Moving a different field consumes the whole owner, and the residual linear member cannot be abandoned in it."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-partial-consume-cannot-abandon-storage-without-its-provider","rules":["PROV-6","WIN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same partial-consume refusal at a concrete member the linear modifier makes linear. Moving a different field cannot make the residual Token releasable; the restructuring is the destructuring consume of the whole value."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-commit-reinitialises-the-consumed-sub-place","rules":["PROV-6","OP-12","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":5,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The form the partial-consume refusal is stated against: the atomic in-place update commits f's result with no program point between the old value's departure and the new value's arrival, so the linear owner has no residual and the refusal does not reach it. Without this form the rule refused the central statement of every library that transforms a component of a linear value in place."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-dispose-through-a-shared-borrow","rules":["OWN-1","REF-1","WIN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A consuming use must be rooted in a live own-mode binding of this function. Content reached through a reference whose row declares only a read may never be moved out, so the rejection is OWN-1's ordinary one at the complete place rather than a rule of its own."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-dispose-of-a-view","rules":["OWN-1","REF-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference owns nothing and contributes no release-graph node, so it has no disposition of its own to run. It is not a place rooted in a live own-mode binding, and the consuming use is the ordinary OWN-1 rejection at the complete place."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-dispose-of-a-modifier-linear-node","rules":["PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"No part of a value, the value's own type included, may be linear by the modifier and still reach a compiler-derived release. The modifier can be written only on a struct or an enum, and a type owning one at any depth is linear too, which is why the refusal is stated over the value rather than over a leaf."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-a-proved-empty-generic-run-disposes-early","rules":["PROV-6","OP-14","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A boxed runtime-capacity window with a statically proved zero length is consumed at the written call rather than at a scope exit, and its cell is freed with it. At a boxed argument the requirement's measure place instantiates as window.inner."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-a-proved-empty-generic-run-releases-at-scope-exit","rules":["PROV-6","STOR-3","LIV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The compiler-derived release is also available to a window, once its element type is affine rather than linear. The walk visits the elements before the backing, so the release needs no emptiness premise of its own, and the written requirement records only that this instance is empty."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-an-empty-run-release-needs-a-proof","rules":["OP-14","PROV-6","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-14"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The empty-window route is proof-directed rather than inferred from the element type or an unused capacity. Without a fact establishing the window's zero length at the call, free_empty's own requirement remains undischarged and the call is the rejection."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-proved-empty-run-still-needs-its-backing-provider","rules":["PROV-6","OP-14"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A zero-length fact says the window holds no element and says nothing else. The window's own type stays linear, so the compiler-derived release still does not carry it and free_empty remains the one route that consumes the storage."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-a-proved-empty-fixed-run-releases-at-scope-exit","rules":["PROV-6","OP-14","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A proved-empty constant-capacity window whose element type is linear is consumed by free_empty, and the caller's exact zero length discharges its requirement. The type stays linear; whole-value consumption and the empty-window proof completely discharge the release."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-generic-run-scope-release-needs-an-empty-proof","rules":["PROV-6","OP-14"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A compiler-derived release cannot carry a window whose element type is linear, because such a window is itself linear. Nothing here establishes the window's zero length and nothing consumes it, so the return edge is the refusal."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-dispose-without-a-capability-leaf","rules":["OP-14","STOR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-14"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The one release operation is defined over windows and their cells alone. A frame-resident struct has no storage of its own to reclaim, so there is nothing for an early release to run and the scope exit owns its whole disposition."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-nodrop-tag-only-enum-consumed","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","OWN-13"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nodrop modifier removes both capabilities from a tag-only enum. Moving the value into a whole-value match consumes it exactly once and discharges the obligation."}
{"id":"own1-pos-nocopy-tag-only-enum-consumed","rules":["OWN-1","OWN-13"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nocopy tag-only enum is affine: the written move into a whole-value match is its one consuming use, while drop remains available on every other leaving edge."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-nodrop-tag-only-enum-reaches-exit","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A tag-only declaration may remove drop, but the resulting linear value must still leave its scope by an explicit whole-value consume. This live ticket reaches the return edge and is refused."}
{"id":"own1-neg-nodrop-tag-only-enum-duplicated","rules":["OWN-1","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Nodrop removes copy together with drop. The first move consumes the ticket binding, so a second move cannot duplicate it and is rejected as a use after move."}
{"id":"own1-pos-structural-copy-aggregates","rules":["OWN-1","PROV-6","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Structs, payload enums, and Arrays derive copy structurally when every owned part is copy and the declaration removes no capability. Each aggregate can be passed and returned twice with bare uses."}
{"id":"own1-pos-generic-struct-copy-substitution","rules":["OWN-1","PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A copy bound supplies copy for a generic field at the symbolic instance, and substituting a copy argument gives the generic struct both capabilities."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-generic-struct-affine-substitution-at-copy-bound","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Generic nominal capabilities are decided after substitution. Wrapped<Box<u8>> owns affine content and therefore cannot satisfy a copy bound even though Wrapped has no declaration modifier."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-region-argument-that-names-no-store","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"With store regions retired, the bound axis that decides an instantiation is the linearity class. Satisfaction is the strict chain copy < affine < linear read left to right, so a copy bound accepts copy arguments only and a linear argument is refused at the instantiation's call, which names the parameter, the bound, and the argument."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-linearity-bound-admits-the-instantiation","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":2,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S32's type axis, admitted: the bound names the linearity class the declaration is written for, and an instantiation whose type argument's class is that bound is accepted. A value the modifier does not mark, and whose reclamation spends no capability this scope lacks, is affine."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-linearity-bound-refuses-the-instantiation","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S32's type axis, refused: an instantiation whose type argument's linearity class is not the written bound is a hard error at the call, naming the parameter, the bound and the argument. The verdict stays one per declaration, which is what refuses per-instantiation checking of the declaration itself."}
{"id":"prov1-pos-a-store-branded-run-in-a-field","rules":["TYPE-9","STOR-1","STOR-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary nominals may contain runtime-capacity runs through their cells. A Box carries no brand and there is one heap, so no global entry-heap brand or program-start selection determines declaration validity."}
{"id":"prov1-pos-a-nominal-declares-a-store-region","rules":["TYPE-9","GRAM-2","STOR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A source nominal may declare its own type and const parameters and hold a constant-capacity window in a field: a constant-capacity shape is frame-resident and is admitted inline in its owner."}
{"id":"prov1-neg-a-provider-in-a-stored-position","rules":["TYPE-9","STOR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The stored-position question survives storage identity: a runtime-capacity shape may appear only as the content of a Box, so it may occupy no field, enum payload, element, or local position directly. The boxed form is admitted in all of them; the two halves are one rule and this case pins the refused half."}
{"id":"prov1-neg-a-source-nominal-collides-with-a-container","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The four compiler-owned storage nominals are entries of the nominal-type TYPEID domain in every unit, so a source declaration of one of their spellings in the same domain is the ordinary DIAG-1 collision and neither declaration resolves. No source construct displaces, overrides, reopens, or shadows one."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-a-source-function-collides-with-a-kernel-row","rules":["TYPE-6","PRE-1","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"PRE-1 window-operation records occupy the ordinary callable name domain. A same-spelled source function is the ordinary TYPE-6 collision."}
{"id":"blk1-neg-a-construct-names-a-run","rules":["TYPE-2","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A storage shape is built by a construction function and by nothing else, so the constructor entry each compiler-owned nominal contributes exists to be refused. The refusal is a judgment over a resolved declaration rather than a comparison of spellings in the checker. The refusal is TYPE-2's since the storage shapes became prelude opaque structs."}
{"id":"blk1-pos-both-runs-are-nameable-types","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-2","TYPE-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One ordinary nominal names both placements of one storage shape: a constant-capacity Slots inline in the struct, and a runtime-capacity Slots reached only as the content of its Box. Type formation depends on no store brand and no heap selector."}
{"id":"blk2-pos-a-formation-row-builds-an-empty-run","rules":["OP-13","PRE-1","CALL-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"slots_new forms an empty frame-resident run and publishes its length and its capacity exactly, which is the completeness a construction record owes. The helper writes no clause of its own; what the case fixes is that the record exists, resolves, hands a run back, and lowers."}
{"id":"blk3-pos-a-boundary-row-moves-the-back-boundary","rules":["OP-10","WIN-2","PRE-1","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"place_back fills the append slot and moves the back boundary by one, and a source helper may declare the same window-part row the built-in record declares. The helper's own len < cap requirement discharges the record's free-slot requirement, which is the one obligation a caller carries."}
{"id":"blk3-pos-a-queue-preserves-its-order","rules":["OP-10","WIN-1","CALL-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"place_back moves the back boundary and take_front moves the front one, so a ring is a queue with no Option anywhere; the exit code observes the order the three elements come back in, and each front removal's replaced window state is reread for the next requirement."}
{"id":"blk1-pos-a-wrapped-window-subscripts-against-len","rules":["WIN-1","MSR-1","OP-4","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A front removal followed by a back placement leaves the ring's head nonzero and the window wrapped, and a subscript is still a logical offset whose OP-4 obligation is against len and never against cap or head. MSR-1's injectivity sentence carries the two logical offsets to the two physical slots the window actually holds."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-a-row-requirement-is-not-discharged","rules":["FN-8","MSR-4","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The per-record requirement discharge under MSR-4 is part of FN-8's own judgment. A third placement into a two-slot run refutes window.len < window.cap, and the diagnostic names the operation, because a PRE-1 declaration has no source clause to cite."}
{"id":"call6-pos-a-row-relation-establishes-at-a-caller","rules":["CALL-6","OP-10","PRE-1","MSR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A PRE-1 record's declared relation is instantiated at the call and established on its normal continuation, exactly as a source contract's is. The first placement's published length and capacity are the only premise the second placement's cap - len requirement has, so the second call is admitted by the first call's publication and by nothing else."}
{"id":"blk3-pos-a-deque-moves-both-boundaries","rules":["OP-10","WIN-1","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The other two boundary operations. place_front moves the front boundary backwards, so the window wraps to the high slots of the ring, and take_back moves the back boundary, so the ring is a deque; with the queue case this exercises all four boundary rows and the wrap in both directions."}
{"id":"inv1-pos-a-measure-former-is-an-affine-factor","rules":["INV-1","MSR-1","MSR-5","GRAM-4","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"INV-1: a measure place is an affine factor of a header invariant, so the relation a contract clause already states is statable at the placement the same rule owns. The checker proves it at the loop base and at every backedge over the same ENT-2 measure term the automatic derivation of a numeric goal already carries an atom for. In v0.60 the measure is the pseudo-field run.len rather than a reader call."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-a-measure-invariant-is-unproved","rules":["INV-1","MSR-1","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"INV-1: a measure factor is a proof obligation like any other affine factor rather than an assumption the spelling grants. The caller's requirement gives four slots and the header states eight, so the base obligation fails at the loop header and the residual names the writer's own measure place."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-an-affine-factor-is-not-a-measure-former","rules":["INV-1","GRAM-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"INV-1: the admitted affine atoms are a measure member on an admitted measure place, a bare IDENT place, and an integer literal. Any call in that position is refused at the call node, so widening the production admits one closed set of factors rather than every runtime expression form the rule lists as inadmissible."}
{"id":"msr5-pos-a-clause-side-is-an-affine-expression","rules":["MSR-5","GRAM-4","GRAM-5","FN-9","CALL-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A contract clause side is GRAM-4's own affine expression, so a relation displaced by a written constant is one clause. The local binder is renamed because next is now reserved from every declaration role."}
{"id":"msr5-pos-a-requirement-side-is-an-affine-expression","rules":["MSR-5","FN-8","OP-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A requirement side carries the whole affine expression rather than the difference-bound fragment a published relation is narrowed to, so at + 2_u64 <= run.len is both the caller's obligation and the premise the callee's own subscript bound discharges from."}
{"id":"msr5-neg-a-clause-side-is-not-a-difference-bound","rules":["FN-9","MSR-5","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-9: a declared relation is one difference bound between two operands displaced by a constant, which is what [ENT-4]'s closure represents and what every kernel-domain row writes. A clause side carrying two datums is outside that fragment and is refused at the declaration rather than published as a fact no caller could hold."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-an-affine-atom-is-not-a-bare-local","rules":["INV-1","GRAM-4","MSR-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"INV-1: an affine factor's atom is one bare IDENT place or one integer literal. The production is shared with a contract clause and carries the wider factor set that clause needs, so a field selection derives and the rule refuses it at the factor; one production serves both placements and each rule states its own admitted factors."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-a-parameter-written-back-keeps-its-entry-measure","rules":["MSR-3","FN-9","SET-1","MSR-1","CALL-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An own parameter's measure in ensures denotes the immutable entry datum, so it keeps meaning the incoming length after newly constructed storage is committed into that local binding, and at the caller it denotes this call's call datum. The result carries the new length through the rebind placement; the contract still relates it to the incoming one."}
{"id":"msr3-neg-a-parameter-value-written-back-loses-its-entry-image","rules":["MSR-3","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"MSR-3: the entry placement mints a datum per measure of a parameter of measured type and for nothing else. A parameter of fragment type is not a measured value and has no measure to carry, so writing it back in the body still makes its entry image unavailable and the clause naming it unproved."}
{"id":"set1-pos-a-run-element-is-a-set-target","rules":["SET-1","TYPE-9","WIN-1","MSR-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscript of a run is an ordinary set target. The target's subscript is the same logical offset a read uses and reaches slot (head + i) mod cap, so at a wrapped ring window the store lands on the window's element and not on the physical slot the offset names."}
{"id":"set2-pos-a-run-element-is-a-replace-target","rules":["OP-11","TYPE-9","OWN-1","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscript of a run is a swap place, which is what carries an affine element out of a slot and its successor in at one commit. At every program point the slot holds exactly one valid owner, so the window stays the run's complete typestate."}
{"id":"set1-neg-a-run-element-target-needs-its-subscript-bound","rules":["SET-1","OP-4","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SET-1: the subscript of an element-position store is judged at the target place exactly as in read position, so an offset the program has not bounded against the window's len measure is refused with the ordinary OP-4 residual rather than admitted because the position is a target."}
{"id":"msr2-pos-an-element-write-kills-no-measure-of-the-run","rules":["MSR-2","SET-1","ENT-5","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write at an element position of a window overlaps the descriptor storage of that element and none of the window's own, so the window's length fact survives the store and discharges a later callee requirement. The granularity is stated over storage."}
{"id":"blk1-pos-a-run-element-type-is-a-type-parameter","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-3","FN-2","OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A shape's element type is any nameable type, an unbounded type parameter and an affine nominal included, and FN-2 resolves the parameter at every concrete instance; neither placement inherits a flat-element restriction."}
{"id":"blk2-pos-a-bump-take-hands-out-a-run","rules":["OP-13","OP-10","MSR-1","PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bump allocator is one storage used with place_back as its allocation step. The case reads both sides of what the records publish: the pool's own capacity, and its cursor after the placements, which advances by one placement and by nothing else."}
{"id":"blk2-pos-a-refused-take-leaves-the-store-unmoved","rules":["OP-13","STOR-8","MSR-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Allocation out of a pool is total in the source, so a request larger than cap - len is refused by the writer's own branch and never by a failure value. The case observes both the arm the program takes and the cursor after it."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-a-proved-take-without-the-room-it-requires","rules":["FN-8","MSR-4","OP-10","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"split_off's second requirement is destination.cap - destination.len >= source.len - index, and a destination this small cannot discharge it. The undischarged requirement of an ordinary PRE-1 record is a static FN-8 call-site rejection with no runtime fallback, naming the clause and rendering the residual under MSR-4."}
{"id":"msr6-pos-a-const-generic-is-an-affine-atom","rules":["MSR-6","INV-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A const generic is a value wherever a named const is, and INV-1's affine atom is the fourth of those positions. The capacity-parametric loop states its own bound as n rather than as a local bound before the loop."}
{"id":"msr6-neg-a-const-generic-affine-atom-is-unproved","rules":["MSR-6","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The admission adds a spelling and no fact source. An invariant naming a const generic is submitted to MSR-4's disposition exactly as one naming a local is, so an overstated bound over the window's own capacity is the ordinary INV-1 unproved target."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-the-bound-chain-admits-every-lower-class","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2","OWN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The three classes form the strict chain copy < affine < linear. An unbounded parameter accepts all three, while a `drop` bound accepts copy and affine arguments; explicit nocopy and nodrop declarations keep the lower-class instances observable after structural copy became the default."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-copy-bound-refuses-an-affine-argument","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S37, the other direction of the chain: a `copy` bound accepts copy arguments only, so an affine argument is a hard error at the instantiation naming the parameter, the bound and the argument."}
{"id":"fn2-pos-the-template-is-the-spelling-authority","rules":["FN-2","OWN-1","PROV-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S37: a generic body is checked once at the symbolic instance under its written bound, and the concrete-instance recheck does not re-judge the spelling FORM-1 keys on a value's class, so one `affine`-bounded body serves a copy instance and an affine one and `move` denotes a copy at the copy instance."}
{"id":"own1-pos-a-copy-bounded-body-duplicates-its-value","rules":["OWN-1","PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S37: under the `copy` bound the body may duplicate its value, use it bare and drop it, and the parameter is copy at the symbolic instance its body is checked at, so no `move` is written anywhere in it."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-linear-bounded-body-drops-its-value","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S37: under the `linear` bound the body must consume its value exactly once and may never drop it, so a body that lets it reach a scope exit is refused, naming the written bound where a marked nominal would otherwise be named."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-bare-type-parameter-without-interface-marker","rules":["FN-3","FN-2","TYPE-6","PROV-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Without the mandatory interface marker, a bare generic-header TYPEID declares an ordinary unbounded type parameter. The marker keeps ordinary <T> declarations unchanged and prevents lookup from reclassifying T as a group import."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-a-region-argument-names-a-bump-extent","rules":["PROV-6","FN-2","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The admitted half of the bound axis: an argument whose class satisfies the written bound instantiates it, and the written arguments are substituted into the parameter's container type at the call, which is what makes a source function generic over a storage shape."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-a-set-target-that-declares-its-binding","rules":["CALL-4","TYPE-6","GRAM-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A call that declares two result ordinals lands them on a parenthesized let binder list, ordinal i at binder i, each binder fresh under TYPE-6 and visible after the complete statement. Since a set target must already be in scope, the binder list is the one spelling that names two results."}
{"id":"liv2-neg-a-projected-set-target-declares-nothing","rules":["SET-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A projected set target selects a component of a binding that must already exist. The absent base fails TYPE-5 name resolution before SET-1 can type or commit the target; no set target declares a binding."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-tag-only-nodrop-through-unbounded-parameter","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A tag-only nodrop enum satisfies an unbounded parameter and remains linear after substitution. The generic body transfers it whole, and the caller consumes the returned value exactly once."}
{"id":"blk1-pos-a-run-element-is-a-run","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-3","OP-10","MSR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"What a slot may hold: a shape's element type is any nameable type, a shape included, so one frame-resident run holds complete inner runs in its own slots. An element that is a run is affine, so it leaves the window through a window operation and comes back with its own window intact, its measures reread at the caller."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-a-run-visits-its-window-before-its-backing","rules":["PROV-6","STOR-3","TYPE-9","WIN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A container's elements are visited before its backing is released, and a cell is freed after its content. Allocation contributes no effect entry, so the rows stay empty while cleanup order remains the tested behavior."}
{"id":"form8-pos-a-run-element-region-is-determined-by-the-actual","rules":["FN-2","PROV-6","TYPE-9","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A type parameter occupying a position at any depth of a written argument is supplied explicitly at the call exactly as a top-level one is. The lift puts the parameter one level down, in a window's element type, which is what lets one helper serve a run of runs; the element type here is itself a boxed runtime-capacity run."}
{"id":"own3-pos-two-nominals-name-one-region","rules":["TYPE-6","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SUBJECT REPLACED at v0.60 with the verdict kept: regions are gone, so the surviving owner-local-parameter question is the type parameter. A type parameter is unique within the declaration that introduces it, so two nominals of one unit may each write T, and the second declaration is neither a repeated parameter nor a redeclaration of the first's."}
{"id":"type2-pos-a-nominal-is-generic-over-its-store","rules":["TYPE-2","TYPE-5","FN-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nominal's type argument is a component of its type name, so an instance is fixed by that argument and a value of it is built by the ordinary construction. The helper is generic over the element type on the terms every other type is: its parameter type names the argument, and the written instantiation determines it at the call."}
{"id":"type5-neg-two-nominal-instances-at-two-regions","rules":["TYPE-5","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-5: two instances of one generic nominal written with different const arguments are two types, decided by the exact identity TYPE-5 already performs. Both runs are well formed and the call relates its two parameters to one instance, so passing a Chunk<8> beside a Chunk<4> is a type-identity mismatch and never a conversion question."}
{"id":"prov1-neg-two-runs-of-two-stores-at-one-formal-region","rules":["TYPE-5","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A generic parameter occupying two container parameter positions is fixed by its written instantiation argument, and every argument position must then match the resulting parameter type exactly. The second window's capacity differs from the one the instantiation fixed, so the argument is the rejection. With store regions retired, the invariance question is exactly this parameter-identity question."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-an-affine-element-read-out-swaps-two-slots","rules":["OP-11","TYPE-9","OP-10","OWN-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"swap exchanges the values at two owned places of one type and consumes neither root, so two elements of one window exchange slots with no program point at which a slot is empty or a stored element has a second owner. It is the one operation whose two reference arguments may name the same place, so the pair needs no distinctness proof; the exit code reports each cell's own capacity after the exchange."}
{"id":"type2-neg-an-affine-element-moved-outside-its-own-commit","rules":["WIN-3","OWN-1","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"WIN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Verdict kept, cited rule moved to WIN-3, which now owns the element move-out: an affine element leaves its slot through take_back, remove_at, or swap and through nothing else. A bare move out of a window slot would mint a second owner of the stored value and leave the slot holding one it no longer owns."}
{"id":"stor1-neg-an-affine-element-target-with-no-read-out","rules":["STOR-1","WIN-3","SET-1","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Verdict changed by v0.60: a live affine element target whose right-hand side does not read the previous value out is now admitted, because assigning over an owned place releases the affine previous value. Only a linear previous value still refuses the store."}
{"id":"liv2-neg-one-element-target-is-read-out-at-most-once","rules":["WIN-3","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"WIN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"There is no take operation and no hole, so a move out of a window slot is refused wherever it is written, including in the right-hand side of a commit whose target is another slot of the same window. The read-out-at-most-once judgment has no subject in v0.60 because no commit reads its own target out; the surviving question is the refusal itself, and its restructuring names take_back, remove_at and swap."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-two-stores-give-two-result-types","rules":["FN-2","TYPE-5","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-2: a call's type arguments are determined by its written argument list and substituted into every position of the callee's signature, results included. Two calls of one declaration at two arguments therefore hand back two types, and setting a Holder<Slots<u16, 4>> into a dead binding of Holder<Slots<u8, 4>> is TYPE-5's exact-identity mismatch rather than an accepted transfer."}
{"id":"fn2-pos-a-result-only-region-is-written-at-the-call","rules":["FN-2","EFF-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A type parameter that occupies no input position of its declaration is still written at the call and substituted into the result, because every instantiation argument is explicit. A specialized consumer then observes that the empty result has exactly the type the caller selected."}
{"id":"fn2-pos-a-const-parameter-beside-a-region-parameter","rules":["FN-2","MSR-6","TYPE-9","OP-13","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One declaration carrying both a const generic parameter and a type parameter. The instance is keyed on both written arguments and the two axes are independent; allocation is total and carries no effect entry, while EFF-2 records the capacity measure read."}
{"id":"op4-neg-a-run-element-read-carries-its-obligation","rules":["OP-4","TYPE-9","MSR-1","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscript carries i < p.len wherever it is written and a read is written no differently from a target. The window is formed empty, so the residual is false and the read is refused before lowering."}
{"id":"op4-pos-a-run-element-read-is-discharged-by-a-published-length","rules":["OP-4","TYPE-9","FN-9","MSR-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-4: the same obligation, discharged at a read. A verified callee relation puts two elements in the window, so the subscript is proved and executes with no runtime check."}
{"id":"msr1-pos-a-measure-over-a-subscripted-place-is-a-term","rules":["MSR-1","OP-4","TYPE-9","MSR-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An admitted measure place is formed with subscripts as well as field selections, so grid[0].len is a term over a window of windows; the subscript inside it is discharged where the place is formed and the element read reaches the inner window's own descriptor."}
{"id":"msr1-neg-a-subscript-inside-a-measure-place-owes-its-obligation","rules":["MSR-1","OP-4","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscript occurring inside a measure place owes the same bounds obligation every other subscript owes. One row was placed, so the residual is not derivable and no measure term over that place exists."}
{"id":"msr2-neg-an-element-store-kills-the-element-s-own-length","rules":["MSR-2","ENT-5","OP-4","OP-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write at an element position of P overlaps the descriptor storage of P[i] and none of P's own. The element exchange kills every measure of grid[0] and no measure of grid, which the residual names exactly: the outer subscript still discharges and the inner one has nothing left."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-two-elements-of-two-inner-runs-are-distinct-targets","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","TYPE-9","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The overlap relation is over the complete path and not over one offset. Two reference arguments name grid[0] and grid[1], whose index steps are literals with different values, so the call's two substituted write paths are proved disjoint and the pair is admitted; both stamped bytes are read back."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-a-row-does-not-discharge-its-caller-s-obligations","rules":["CALL-6","OP-4","ENT-4","ENT-5","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A PRE-1 record's published relations may narrow what its caller derives and may never make everything derivable. The construction publishes the new run's own measures and nothing about the table beside it, so the table read is judged against the table's own length and is refused."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-the-refusing-formation-row-discharges-nothing-either","rules":["CALL-6","OP-4","OP-13","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same judgment over a heap construction record, whose published relations are all about the cell it made. Its relations are instantiated by the one path every record's are, so the table read beside it is judged against the table's own length and is refused."}
{"id":"blk0-pos-a-row-s-published-state-is-what-its-caller-holds","rules":["CALL-6","MSR-2","MSR-4","OP-10","PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A window operation's own relations are what the caller holds afterwards, so the second placement's requirement is discharged from what the first published rather than from a stale extent, and cap - len computes the free capacity the two placements left."}
{"id":"form8-pos-a-construct-elides-the-region-its-field-determines","rules":["FN-2","TYPE-5","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The v0.59 subject was region elision at a construct. Instantiation arguments are now always explicit, so the surviving question is its answer: a construct writes its type argument even where the field's own operand already fixes it, and there is no elision left. The measure is then read through the field as an ordinary place."}
{"id":"form8-pos-a-construct-writes-the-region-no-field-determines","rules":["FN-2","TYPE-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The other half. No field of the nominal mentions its type parameter, so nothing about the operands could have supplied it, and the construct writes it in its own type-argument list exactly as every other instantiation argument is written."}
{"id":"inv1-pos-a-subscript-inside-an-invariant-s-measure-place","rules":["INV-1","MSR-1","MSR-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"INV-1, MSR-1: an affine factor's measure place is formed with subscripts under the enclosing concrete instance, so a header invariant measures one element of a window of windows and MSR-2's standing ordering over that element discharges it."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-a-subscript-inside-an-invariant-owes-its-bound","rules":["INV-1","OP-4","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"INV-1, OP-4: an invariant evaluates nothing, but a measure over a place whose subscripts are not all discharged is no term there either."}
{"id":"blk0-pos-a-routed-row-publishes-its-payload-on-the-some-arm","rules":["CALL-6","CALL-4","FN-9","MSR-3","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A declaration whose result is an enum states part of its relation list per variant, and publication restricts each routed relation to the arm its route names. The Ok arm binds the payload the route's own binder spelling names, so the bound that route publishes is the caller's and the subscript is discharged from it with no branch on the payload at all."}
{"id":"blk0-pos-the-none-arm-knows-the-store-was-not-advanced","rules":["CALL-6","MSR-2","MSR-4","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"On the arm on which no window operation ran, the run the writer holds is exactly the run it held before the branch: nothing was published there and the measures already established stand, so the placement that follows discharges its own len < cap requirement from them."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-the-none-arm-refutes-the-take-that-refused","rules":["FN-8","CALL-6","MSR-4","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A branch that reads the measure back publishes a fact and not an absence: on the arm where the length is known to be zero, take_back's requires window.len > 0_u64 is refuted rather than merely unproved, and the refusal is a static rejection naming the operation."}
{"id":"blk0-pos-the-some-arm-publishes-the-store-s-own-advance","rules":["CALL-6","MSR-2","MSR-3","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A window operation publishes what it did to both windows as well as the elements it moved: append states destination.len == entry(destination).len + entry(source).len and source.len == 0_u64, so the caller reads both new lengths from the record and from nothing else."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-the-some-arm-s-advance-bounds-the-next-take","rules":["FN-8","CALL-6","MSR-4","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same publication read the other way: split_off publishes source.len == index, so after a split at zero the source is known empty and take_back's requirement is undischarged on the one path on which the source's new length is known at all."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-a-set-target-carries-the-value-s-measures","rules":["MSR-3","SET-1","MSR-1","MSR-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The rebind placement at a SET-1 target. A commit whose right-hand side is a bare use of a measured place carries every measure that place had, exactly as the let rebind does: the datum is minted before the statement's own kills and read after them. The target binding is dead at statement entry, so only the placement discharges the later subscript."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-a-construct-field-and-its-destructuring-carry-the-measures","rules":["MSR-3","MSR-1","TYPE-5","GRAM-10","MSR-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The construct and destructuring placements, with no branch on a measure anywhere. The field operand carries its measures into ticket.run and the destructuring consume carries them back out to the binder that names the field, which under GRAM-10 must be fresh and distinct from the field label."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-a-construct-carries-a-measure-two-levels-down","rules":["MSR-3","MSR-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The construct placement is per placement and not per depth. MSR-1 admits a measure place formed with any number of field selections, so a struct operand holding a struct holding a window names a measured place two levels down; the placement carries its measures to the matching path of the constructed value and the subscript there is discharged."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-owned-descendant-measures","rules":["MSR-3","ENT-2","MSR-1","INV-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two boxed descendants retain distinct lengths through struct construction in Box.inner, owner rebinding, a Some payload, and destructuring the boxed content. The outer Box and struct have no measures; exact descendant projections distinguish their placement datums. Unguarded invariants and subscripts require the transported facts, and execution checks the selected bytes."}
{"id":"msr3-neg-replaced-owned-descendant-measure","rules":["MSR-3","ENT-5","MSR-2","INV-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An invariant confirms that a nonempty boxed descendant's length reached its renamed owner. Replacing that descendant with an empty window kills the old fact, and subsequent destructuring cannot revive it, so reading element zero is an OP-4 rejection."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-an-element-position-keeps-the-value-put-into-it","rules":["MSR-3","SET-1","MSR-1","MSR-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The element placement. A measured value committed at an element position keeps its measures as facts about P[i], so a window that passed through a written slot still discharges the window operation into it without re-reading cap - len. The retired replace statement's other direction, the value displaced out of the slot, has no successor because one commit writes one place."}
{"id":"msr3-neg-one-element-position-is-not-another","rules":["MSR-3","OWN-7","MSR-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An element placement writes the element position it names and no other. The commit at blocks[0] carries that window's measures there, the two literal offsets are provably distinct, and nothing was established about blocks[1], so the subscript inside it has nothing to discharge against. The rejection is OP-4's, the retired BLK-0 having owned it before."}
{"id":"msr3-neg-a-rebind-carries-the-run-and-not-its-elements","rules":["MSR-3","ENT-5","MSR-2","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A placement carries a window's own measures but does not descend through its element positions. The rebind carries the window's length and capacity, so the subscript into the renamed window still discharges, while the element measures an earlier event established die with the consume the rebind performs. The undischarged requirement at the ordinary PRE-1 call is FN-8's rejection; BLK-0 is retired."}
{"id":"msr3-neg-a-boundary-row-carries-no-measure-through-the-slot","rules":["MSR-3","OP-10","MSR-1","MSR-4","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The element placement reaches a written element position and no other. place_back stores at the entry length of its referent and take_back takes from the exit length, and a measure term is not an offset a place relation may name, so a window put into a slot by a window operation and taken back out by one arrives with no measures of its own. The undischarged requirement at that ordinary PRE-1 call is FN-8's rejection; BLK-0, which owned it before, is retired."}
{"id":"msr3-pos-a-payload-binder-carries-the-payload-s-measures","rules":["MSR-3","MSR-1","CALL-4","MSR-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The payload placement over an enum exactly one of whose variants carries fields. The construct carries the window's measures into the payload and the datum minted before the match's own consume is read at the arm binder that names it."}
{"id":"msr3-neg-a-two-payload-enum-has-no-payload-place","rules":["MSR-3","ENT-2","ENT-3","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A tracked place's path now admits the enum payload step, so an arm's path names its own variant's storage and an enum more than one of whose variants carries fields needs no separate treatment. The construct carries the window's measures into Result's Ok payload and the arm binder reads them back, which discharges the window operation in the arm. This case was a rejection while no place path could separate Ok(value) from Err(error); v0.60 admits the step, so the verdict is now an acceptance and the identifier keeps the question it was written for."}
{"id":"view1-pos-an-element-write-through-an-exclusive-view","rules":["REF-4","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SET-1 admits a target path reached through a range reference whose root is a live own binding. The program forms a range reference over a heap-resident array, writes two of its elements, reads all three positions back through the cell, and observes the two written values and the one untouched one."}
{"id":"view1-neg-an-element-write-through-a-shared-view","rules":["SET-1","EFF-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SET-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The refusal the rule keeps, restated without borrow markers: write authority through a reference parameter is its declared row [EFF-1], so a target reached through a reference whose row carries reads and no writes of that path is not writable. The diagnostic names the resolved root class and the writable classes SET-1 admits."}
{"id":"view2-neg-an-exclusive-view-takes-a-unique-borrow","rules":["TYPE-5","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Rewritten subject: v0.60 has no borrow mode, so the written parameter kind is what decides the row. fill takes the range reference &[u8], so a whole-array reference is a TYPE-5 mismatch at that operand's atom, naming the kind the written row takes. The rejection is at the operand rather than at the result, because the row is selected before its result type exists."}
{"id":"view2-neg-an-exclusive-view-of-a-named-const","rules":["CONST-2","SET-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named const is immutable, program-lifetime, read-only storage, so a range reference over one is a read route and never a write route. A write through it is therefore a CONST-2 rejection at the target place, and it is that rule rather than any overlap rule because the storage is not writable at all."}
{"id":"view2-pos-an-exclusive-view-over-an-array","rules":["REF-4","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-4 states one range-reference source class for every indexable shape, and this program is the writing half of it over an inline array: the range reference is formed, one element is written through it, and the array itself is read back. The heap sibling view1-pos-an-element-write-through-an-exclusive-view is the same program over cell content."}
{"id":"view1-pos-a-shared-view-is-used-twice-without-move","rules":["REF-1","REF-4","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference is not an owned value, so handing one to a helper is not a consuming use: one range reference is passed to the helper twice with no move, and read/read overlap at the two calls is admitted [EFF-5]. Its own length is read after both calls, so a classification that consumed it at the first call is visible as a rejection rather than as a silent difference."}
{"id":"view1-neg-a-move-of-a-shared-view","rules":["OWN-1","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A move of a copy value is OWN-1's refusal, and a reference variable denotes the reference [REF-1] whose referent is reached through deref, so move deref(window) resolves to the copy u64 the reference names. The rejection is at the use rather than at the formation, and its restructuring is to drop the move spelling."}
{"id":"prov3-neg-an-append-while-a-copy-view-is-still-used","rules":["REF-2","REF-4","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The boundary-moving call while a range reference over the window has a later use. The write of the prefix is now legal and carries no loan; what the checker refuses is the later read through the reference the call invalidated. The pair pins that validity, not a region end, is what ends a reference."}
{"id":"view2-pos-a-view-over-a-run","rules":["REF-4","MSR-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A Slots window is a range-reference source, its initialized storage being one contiguous extent beginning at slot zero; a Ring is refused there instead. Two overlapping range references of the one run are formed, which read/read overlap admits without limit, and the program reads the range length through one and the elements back through both."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-a-view-over-a-wrapped-run","rules":["REF-4","TYPE-9","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A Ring's logical window is two extents and a range reference has one len, so v0.60 refuses the range step over a Ring at the psuffix itself instead of submitting a non-wrap premise for proof. The verdict stays a rejection; the rule that owns it moves from the retired formation-row requirement to REF-4."}
{"id":"view2-pos-an-exclusive-view-over-a-run","rules":["REF-4","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The writing half of the range-reference source class over a run: the range reference is formed over a frame-resident Slots window, one element is written through it, and the run itself is read back."}
{"id":"blk4-pos-a-unique-parameter-reaches-a-run","rules":["EFF-1","TYPE-9","SET-1","TYPE-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference parameter reaches a heap-resident run. Whether the callee may write through it is stated by its declared row alone, there being no permission marker on the reference; the element write is exactly that declared effect, reported at its nearest statically nameable enclosing path."}
{"id":"blk4-pos-a-unique-parameter-reaches-a-type-parameter","rules":["REF-1","FN-2","EFF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference parameter whose referent type is a generic type parameter. Exact projected effects govern caller fact kills, and this declaration's empty row leaves the referent unchanged; there is no permission marker to suggest otherwise."}
{"id":"fn7-pos-the-entry-receives-the-general-store","rules":["FN-7","PROG-3","STOR-8","OP-13","EFF-1","PROV-6"],"expect":{"exit":16,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The selected ordinary main is an ordinary function with an ordinary signature; the heap is ambient and has no source spelling, so the v0.59 Heap provider parameter and its region are gone. Allocation, window mutation and element reads use the same contracts as any helper, release is compiler-derived on the return edge, and the program retains its original exit-16 observation."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-store-backed-run-reaches-an-exit-without-the-capability","rules":["PROV-6","LIV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Linearity is a property of the type and holds in every scope, so a value the linear modifier makes linear may not reach a scope exit by a compiler-derived release. The refusal names the binding, the edge, and the declaration that made the value linear."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-a-scope-holding-the-provider-takes-the-derived-release","rules":["PROV-6","STOR-3","LIV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The other half of the linear refusal: the same shape whose element type is affine is affine, its release is derived on the return edge, and the row stays empty because a release contributes no effect path. The pair is what makes the refusal about the type rather than about the scope."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-an-early-release-of-a-store-backed-run","rules":["PROV-6","STOR-3","OP-13"],"expect":{"exit":5,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The early release with no release operation to write: an affine value is released early by moving it into a function that consumes it and ends, and that return edge runs the same walk the caller's scope exit would have run. The exit code is a byte read out before the value is handed over, so a walk that ran early or twice reports rather than passing quietly."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-a-dispose-with-no-provider-in-scope","rules":["PROV-6","LIV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Release is compiler-derived and there is no release statement to write, so the question the retired statement asked is asked at the edge itself: a binding whose value is linear and which is live on an edge leaving its scope is refused there, with the written bound that made it linear rendered."}
{"id":"blk4-neg-a-field-branded-to-a-heap-the-unit-cannot-reach","rules":["TYPE-9","STOR-8","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A runtime-capacity run reaches a field only as the content of its Box, and that field needs no store brand, region parameter or selected provider input: there is one heap and a Box carries no brand. Ordinary ownership closure still governs constructing and releasing actual values."}
{"id": "s39-pos-a-cell-holds-one-value-at-the-general-store", "rules": ["TYPE-2", "TYPE-9", "PROV-6", "OP-13", "PRE-1"], "expect": {"exit": 14, "kind": "run"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Box<T> is one value of T at the one heap and carries no measure at all, so a read of one needs no proof; a move of its field inner takes the value out and frees the cell in one expression. The exit code adds the read and the taken value, so a cell read or released wrongly reports rather than passing quietly."}
{"id":"s39-pos-a-cell-at-a-bump-extent-reclaims-with-its-region","rules":["TYPE-2","STOR-1","OP-13","PRE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same storage at the other placement: a constant-capacity window is frame-resident, derives no storage reclamation of its own, and its slots are released with the frame. The pair with the heap-cell case is what makes the release class a property of the placement rather than of the type."}
{"id":"s39-neg-a-cell-reaches-an-exit-without-the-capability","rules":["PROV-6","TYPE-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A cell whose content is linear is linear itself, because linearity is closed under ownership, so it may not reach a scope exit by a compiler-derived release; the compiler never releases a linear value."}
{"id":"op9-neg-kernel-acquisition-without-a-fit-proof","rules":["OP-9","OP-13","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every runtime-capacity construction carries the static allocation-size obligation over its own stored type and count. An unconstrained runtime count discharges nothing, so the construction is the ordinary static OP-9 rejection rather than an admitted unproved partial operation."}
{"id":"op9-pos-kernel-acquisition-with-a-fit-proof","rules":["OP-9","OP-13","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same construction is admitted once its own allocation-size obligation is discharged, from a written numeric requirement on the count, or from a literal count that closes the goal on its own [OP-9, MSR-4]."}
{"id":"blk4-pos-a-shared-borrow-of-a-run-parameter","rules":["REF-1","TYPE-7","OP-4","STOR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A &Slots<u32, 4> parameter is an ordinary shared reference: the run's storage is inline in its owner, the reference is a name for that path, the window is read bare with no read-through operation, and the caller's reference lasts through the call."}
{"id":"blk4-pos-a-run-reached-through-a-shared-borrow-of-a-nominal","rules":["REF-1","MSR-1","OP-4","EFF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A run field reached through a shared reference to its owner is one measured place: the measure read and the subscript name the same place through the same reference, so the OP-4 obligation is discharged from the guard written over that measure."}
{"id":"blk4-pos-a-reference-holder-over-a-store-resident-run","rules":["REF-1","TYPE-9","TYPE-7","MSR-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference holder over a heap-resident run: the let binder names the path the Box content occupies, and the measure read and the subscript reach that one run through that one holder rather than through one shape each."}
{"id":"type7-neg-a-run-holder-written-where-the-run-is-required","rules":["TYPE-5","TYPE-9","OP-15"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Rejecting rule changed at v0.60: a Box is the prelude's opaque struct and its content is the field inner [TYPE-9], so a Box holder written where the run itself is required is the ordinary exact-argument mismatch [TYPE-5], whose mechanical fix is move cell.inner. v0.59 cited TYPE-7, which now owns the reference step alone."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-a-measure-reader-in-return-position-over-a-run","rules":["FN-9","CALL-4","MSR-1","OP-15"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A measure read in return position is a place form and not a call: the returned value is that measure of that place, so the declared relation is verified directly over it rather than refused for want of a datum."}
{"id":"const2-neg-a-cell-is-not-const-eligible","rules":["CONST-2","TYPE-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CONST-2 rejects an owning heap cell as a named constant: a const is pure static rodata with no allocation and no drop, and Box is not const-eligible. The type is region-free and the original scalar initializer is retained."}
{"id":"own6-pos-a-helper-re-lends-its-view-destination","rules":["REF-4","CALL-3","EFF-5","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A helper handed a range reference passes that same destination on to a second helper of the same parameter shape. The reference that is itself an argument is the thing being accessed rather than a bystander, the callee's write reaches the range's storage while the origin window keeps its own length, and the outer caller reads back what the innermost helper wrote."}
{"id": "view6-pos-a-helper-publishes-the-child-of-its-destination", "rules": ["REF-4", "REF-1", "REF-3", "TYPE-7", "CALL-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The fill-and-publish helper, restated for v0.60. A reference never escapes its callee, so the helper writes through its &[u8] destination with set deref(destination)[i] = value and returns an owned count related in ensures. The caller forms the child range itself with the re-slice &deref(window)[0_u64..filled] over the boxed array content &table.inner[0_u64..4_u64], and reads back through that child exactly what the helper wrote through its destination."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s12-a-published-relation-reaches-a-set-target","rules":["ENT-3","CALL-6","FN-9","FN-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A set target is a destination of the published relation. The relation is established after the call's own transfer, consumes and effect kills and after the target's commit and kills, over the committed place, so the caller carries the published bound past the assignment and discharges the later callee requirement from it."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-s12-a-published-relation-reaches-a-let-binder","rules":["ENT-3","CALL-6","FN-9","FN-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control beside ent3-pos-s12-a-published-relation-reaches-a-set-target: the same callee publishes the same relation onto the fresh binding of a direct ordinary let, and the same later requirement is discharged from it. The two cases differ in the destination and in nothing else."}
{"id":"const2-pos-a-fixed-vector-const-run","rules":["CONST-2","CONST-1","MSR-1","OP-4","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The const form of the inline run: an Array<T, N> of const-eligible T with exactly N literal entries is const-eligible read-only static rodata. It lowers to element storage only and stores no length, capacity or head, so the three measures its row has read those constants, a subscript discharges from len == N, and the const is the immutable origin of a range reference."}
{"id":"const2-neg-a-fixed-vector-const-short-of-its-capacity","rules":["CONST-2","CONST-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An Array<T, N>-typed const takes exactly N entries, because len == cap == N is a standing fact of the type rather than a stored word. A shorter entry list is rejected at the item."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-a-callee-write-through-a-uniq-extent-kills-the-room","rules":["ENT-5","EFF-2","CALL-5","FN-8","MSR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The callee writes the window behind its reference parameter. EFF-2 projects that write onto the actual place, which supports its len and cap measures, and ENT-5 kills those facts. The opening cap - len relation does not survive the call, so the later window operation's requirement is rejected at FN-8 with the residual submitted to MSR-4. The arena extent of the retiring version is now an ordinary Slots window."}
{"id":"call5-neg-a-one-byte-body-kills-the-measure-the-same","rules":["CALL-5","CALL-3","ENT-5","FN-8","MSR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"No transport reads the callee's body. This callee moves the boundary by one byte and the caller's whole length fact nevertheless dies: a projected write through a parameter that is not a range reference reaches the ordinary descriptor storage. A body that places one byte and a body that places two are the same declaration to every reader of the signature, so both reach the same undischarged requirement."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-callee-uniq-buffer-replace-kills-length","rules":["ENT-5","EFF-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The callee exchanges the whole referent of its Box reference parameter. EFF-2 projects writes(handle) onto the actual place supporting the caller length term, so ENT-5 must kill the old length fact and OP-4 must reject offset 9 after replacement by a two-slot allocation. Explicit unsupported capability is not the expected source rejection; this case retains xfail until the ordinary compiler reaches OP-4."}
{"id": "stor1-neg-whole-buffer-replacement", "rules": ["STOR-1", "SET-1", "WIN-3", "TYPE-7"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Verdict changed by v0.60: storing a whole heap-owned window into the field inner of a cell reached by a reference parameter whose row declares the write is now admitted, because assigning over an owned place releases the affine previous value instead of discarding it. The old rejection required a form that bound the previous owner; that form no longer exists."}
{"id": "stor1-neg-whole-cell-replacement", "rules": ["STOR-1", "SET-1", "WIN-3", "TYPE-7"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Verdict changed by v0.60: storing a live affine-typed final place whole into the field inner of a heap-owned cell is now admitted, the previous affine owner taking its ordinary release at the commit. Only a linear previous value still refuses the store."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-a-run-capacity-survives-a-root-replace","rules":["ENT-2","ENT-4","ENT-5","OP-11","OP-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The implicit cap = n of a Slots<T, n> place holds at every program point. An OP-11 swap of the two run roots kills every relation supported by those places, and the capacity equality still reads back on both sides of the exchange because it is a function of the place's type. The honest difference from an Array is that len is a descriptor word rather than a type constant, so the length does not survive the exchange."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-term-multiplicity","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-6","ENT-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A matrix inner index at a runtime stride. `use n times (p < k);` scales one premise by an unsigned value, which makes the accumulated sum a degree-two polynomial; its `n*p` and `n*k` monomials fold to the value images the two admitted exact multiplications bound, and the residual against the target is then the constant zero."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-signed-multiplicity","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same certificate over signed operands. A named multiplicity must be unsigned, because nonnegativity is what makes scaling a normalized premise sound and a signed value could reverse it; the type is the whole check, so nothing here becomes an obligation."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-unfolded-nonlinear-sum","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A term-scaled premise whose `n*p` monomial no admitted multiplication in scope equals. The accumulated sum never reduces to an affine inequality, so PRF-1 rejects rather than measuring the target against a weaker sum."}
{"id":"gram4-neg-bare-relation-premise","rules":["GRAM-4","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A relation premise is delimited whether or not it carries a multiplicity. Written bare, `use x <= middle;` does not derive: after `use` the grammar admits an invariant name, a delimited relation, or a multiplicity, and an affine relation is none of those."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-term-multiplicity-shared-product","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two bindings hold the same product `n*p`. Both equal the monomial the term-scaled premise passes through, so either folds soundly; the one the target itself names is chosen, because a monomial folded to an equal value under another name would not cancel."}
{"id":"gram4-neg-typed-multiplicity","rules":["GRAM-4","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A multiplicity is a bare decimal, so a suffixed literal does not derive. The citation is the position's own rule: `const` and this position share the grammar's sole pattern predicate, and only the first is CONST-1's."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-binder-multiplicity","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A counted loop binder is a named multiplicity. PRF-1 admits any live own-mode unsigned integer value binding there, not only a let-bound local or a parameter, and a counted binder is own u64 by the language's own rule."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-derived-multiplicity","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A padded-bitmap row offset whose stride is computed rather than passed in, which is what every real stride is. The fold names the declaration the writer wrote rather than the sum that declaration expands to, so the product over `stride` and the certificate scaling by `stride` meet."}
{"id":"run-sysin-read-to-end","rules":["FN-7","PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"stdin":"7768697465666f6f7420737464696e20626f647921"},"doc":"The ordinary read_next operation takes the input and shared invocation factory state by reference and declares the writes in its row. Repeated Ok endpoints advance the source total to the arranged twenty-one bytes; a later Err(ReadEnd) ends the loop."}
{"id":"sysin-read-zero-range","rules":["PRE-2","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","arrange":{"stdin":"41424344"},"doc":"The ordinary read_next implementation returns Ok(start) without transfer for an empty range. The following nonempty read still observes the first arranged byte."}
{"id":"sysin-read-outcome-exhaustive","rules":["ERR-2","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ERR-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ReadStop is an ordinary enum nested inside Result. Omitting ReadFailed from its match remains ERR-2 non-exhaustiveness; the storage and reference changes alter the API shape without weakening the rejected coverage."}
{"id":"systcp-address-pure","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary socket_address_v4 and socket_address_v6 declarations have empty rows and total signatures. Their caller constructs both address values in a pure body."}
{"id":"systcp-connection-not-constructible","rules":["PRE-2","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"C2 removes SYS-2/SYS-18 constructor suppression. The original unit/unit construction is retained and rejects ordinary TYPE-5 field type mismatch; this is not converted to an acceptance case."}
{"id":"systcp-connection-moved-half-kills-binding","rules":["WIN-3","PROV-6","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TcpReceive and TcpSend are linear, so moving one direction out of their ordinary TcpConnection is a partial consume with a residual linear part. PROV-6 owns that refusal and directs the writer to destructure the whole value; no special paired-handle rule exists."}
{"id":"systcp-connection-two-halves","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","PAR-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary TcpConnection receive and send fields are disjoint places. One call substitutes one write path per field, and the pairwise check proves them distinct by their different field steps; no target classification authorizes overlap."}
{"id":"systcp-connection-field-effect-paths","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A helper receiving only through link.receive declares that exact ordinary field effect plus its scratch effects. The untouched send field adds nothing; field row precision does not shorten the whole-connection reference."}
{"id":"systcp-listen-permit-returned","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary tcp_listen takes a factory by reference, declares the write in its row, and returns Result<TcpListener, IoError>. Refusal retains the factory for another attempt; success is explicitly consumed by close_listener. Permit-return and qualification assertions are retired."}
{"id":"systcp-accept-permit-returned","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary tcp_accept takes the listener and the factory by reference and declares both writes in its row. Refusal leaves both ordinary values available; a successful public connection is destructured and both halves explicitly closed. No shared-listener overlap or permit-return rule survives."}
{"id":"systcp-connect-permit-returned","rules":["PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Ordinary tcp_connect takes a factory by reference and returns an ordinary success or failure enum. Refusal preserves that factory for another attempt; success requires explicit close of both linear halves."}
{"id":"par1-pos-a-view-argument-is-a-footprint-on-its-origin","rules":["PAR-1","EFF-5","REF-4","OWN-7"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range-reference argument is a footprint on the storage it was formed over, whether the reference is written at the call or bound by a let and handed on. Two calls hand on bound references of two distinct runs, so their substituted write paths have distinct roots, OWN-7 finds them disjoint, and PAR-1 permits the adjacent pair; the program reads both runs back and exits 0. Permission is not acceptance, so what this case pins in the corpus is the acceptance and the result."}
{"id":"par1-neg-two-exclusive-views-of-one-origin-in-one-window","rules":["EFF-5","REF-4","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two range references over one run are handed to the two write parameters of one call. EFF-5 substitutes the actual argument paths into the callee's row and compares the substituted effects pairwise; the two identical ranges overlap under OWN-7 and neither is proved disjoint, so the call is the rejection. Under the retired view rules the second formation was refused first; with references carrying no loan, the overlapping-argument subject now lands squarely on the pairwise call-site check."}
{"id":"par3-pos-a-per-iteration-run-from-the-store-is-iteration-own","rules":["OP-13","PROV-6","PRE-1","REF-1","PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The per-iteration cell is declared inside the counted body, so it is owned by that iteration and takes its compiler-derived release on the iteration's own leaving edge. The allocation, failed-open loop and exit-zero observation remain an ordinary executable program, and the acquired linear file is consumed on every path that acquires one."}
{"id":"own5-pos-rhs-borrow-is-disjoint-from-captured-target","rules":["SET-1","OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Distinct literal elements keep the right-hand side's reference disjoint from the captured commit target, and that reference ends before the following statement writes its element."}
{"id":"set1-pos-index-is-captured-before-rhs-borrow","rules":["SET-1","REF-1","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The target captures index zero before the right-hand side writes the separate scalar index to one through its reference parameter; the final commit still reaches the captured element."}
{"id":"own6-pos-owned-control-headers-return-temporaries","rules":["GRAM-6","GRAM-7","OWN-13","GIVE-1","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Match and if headers, in statement and value forms, end their argument references before branch work begins, so the same path is written again inside each branch; a helper whose own reference parameter supplies every header argument resumes that path after each header call."}
{"id":"blk3-pos-full-array-owning-round-trip","rules":["TYPE-9","OP-13","OP-10","OP-11","FN-2","PROV-6","WIN-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A full Slots of affine records becomes a complete owning array in logical order. Operand-derived element types and explicit generic source helpers retain the exact type; a shared indexed read, an exchange through swap, the inverse conversion and a complete drain observe every value, and zero extent crosses both conversions without element access. The wrapped-window variant is no longer expressible, because the two conversions admit Slots alone and only a Ring can wrap."}
{"id":"blk0-neg-full-array-freeze-requires-fullness","rules":["FN-8","OP-13","MSR-4","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"slots_into_array requires values.len == n. A capacity-two run with one initialized element does not satisfy it, and full-array formation cannot expose the raw second slot; the undischarged requirement is an FN-8 call-site rejection."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-zero-extent-full-array-keeps-linear-obligation","rules":["PROV-6","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An array of explicitly linear Token remains statically linear at extent zero. Its empty runtime element set does not authorize implicit abandonment of the typed obligation."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-full-array-missing-element-provider","rules":["PROV-6","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The release closure reaches a linear value through a cell's content and then through a full array element, so the array is linear. A helper that lets it reach the return edge is refused, because the compiler-derived release never carries a linear value."}
{"id":"stor5-neg-zero-extent-array-cannot-hide-provider","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A full array's complete element type still participates in the placement closure at zero extent. A stored array field cannot hide a runtime-capacity shape, which exists only as the content of a cell."}
{"id":"form8-pos-single-input-invariant-brand","rules":["FN-2","TYPE-2","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One reader serves two separately constructed values of one written instantiation, without a provider argument. The argument is written at each construction and at the parameter type, and the reader's own temporary reference is formed at each call and carries no name of its own."}
{"id":"form8-pos-multiple-brand-positions","rules":["FN-2","TYPE-2","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every instantiation argument is written at every nesting depth; there is no position at which an operand supplies one silently. A wrapper's field and a reader's parameter each name the complete written type, and the place reaches the field by ordinary field steps."}
{"id":"type5-neg-repeated-brand-position-mismatch","rules":["TYPE-5","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two positions of one parameter type name the same formal type parameter but receive distinct actual types. A nominal's type argument is a component of its name, and no outliving or subtyping relation replaces that invariant identity within one type."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-exclusive-owner-exit-state","rules":["FN-1","EFF-2","OP-11","PROV-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A helper exchanges the contents of two cells through references and the caller observes both values. The v0.59 replace statement is retired: swap is the atomic exchange, it consumes neither root, and no program point holds a place with no owner. Each cell is released by the compiler-derived scope exit, and there is one heap with no store value to name."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-installed-owner-read-omitted","rules":["FN-1","EFF-2","OP-11","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The exchange changes the value at owner without changing that storage place, and the later read names owner. Both parameters of the observer are by-value, so neither contributes an effect path and the empty row is exact; the exchange itself is swap, which consumes neither root [OP-11]."}
{"id":"liv2-pos-entry-dead-owner-initialization","rules":["SET-1","EFF-2","FN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Reinitializing a complete binding already dead at statement entry writes no previous owner, while the same-statement update keeps its read and its commit write. An own parameter contributes no effect entry at all, so both helpers declare pure where the previous version wrote reads and writes rooted at an own parameter."}
{"id":"own6-pos-statement-children-use-a-longer-local-region","rules":["REF-1","SET-1","OWN-11","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A by-value parameter contributes no effect entry and allocation contributes none either, so only the writes of referenced field paths are declared. An argument reference lasts exactly through its call, sequential calls are permitted, and owned results stay alive."}
{"id":"run-exclusive-push-pop-counted","rules":["OP-10","FN-9","MSR-3","CALL-6","INV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Exclusive push and multi-result pop preserve two-state length relations across counted loop edges, with no owning window argument or result."}
{"id":"run-exclusive-nested-field","rules":["WIN-2","FN-9","MSR-3","CALL-6","EFF-2","ENT-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Exact projected field effects kill the changed window's support while preserving its disjoint sibling; entry(pair) and exit field measures instantiate to the same resolved caller field at different states."}
{"id":"run-exclusive-take-multiple-results","rules":["OP-10","FN-9","CALL-6","MSR-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A take returns only its element; a source caller returns that element and a scalar measure beside the exclusive window postcondition. Both scalar and state facts reach the caller."}
{"id":"exclusive-neg-whole-referent-replace-kills","rules":["ENT-5","CALL-5","FN-8","MSR-4","OP-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the whole referent through a written reference parameter invalidates the caller's pre-call nonempty fact. A later take without a new check is refused at FN-8 with the residual submitted to MSR-4, regardless of the actual argument's spelling."}
{"id":"exclusive-neg-exit-is-not-own-increment","rules":["FN-9","CALL-6","MSR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CALL-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two occurrences of the same measure of a written reference parameter, deref(values).len, neither written as an entry term, denote the same exit term. The impossible equation exit = exit + 1 is a contradictory declared relation, not an entry/exit transition, and CALL-6 rejects the set at the declaration that publishes it."}
{"id":"exclusive-neg-no-ensures-no-length-fact","rules":["FN-9","CALL-5","ENT-5","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The exact write effect kills the old run length facts; a written reference parameter alone publishes no replacement fact [CALL-5], so the first stated invariant is unproved."}
{"id":"run-exclusive-no-ensures-reread","rules":["OP-10","CALL-6","ENT-5","MSR-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Without a postcondition the caller rereads length and follows a genuine empty/nonempty branch before taking an element."}
{"id":"exclusive-neg-view-live-during-call","rules":["EFF-5","REF-4","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two range references over the same extent of one window reach one call, one of them at a written parameter. EFF-5 compares the substituted paths pairwise, no admitted family proves the two ranges disjoint, and the overlapping write/read pair is rejected at the call. The retiring version's live-view refusal is now that pairwise call-site check."}
{"id":"run-exclusive-owning-map-put","rules":["OP-10","OP-11","FN-9","CALL-6","MSR-3","PROV-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The owning enum-slot put and its allocation wrapper use reference window parameters. Collision insertion and duplicate replacement preserve the window length through verified equality contracts; the old occupant leaves by swap rather than by a hole, and refusal and ordinary cleanup retain their owning payloads. Allocation is total, so the wrapper has no refusal arm."}
{"id":"run-exclusive-generic-append","rules":["OP-10","FN-2","FN-9","CALL-6","MSR-3","WIN-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A generic exclusive append proves two-state length and capacity relations and is instantiated at both scalar and owning window elements. The head measure is absent on Slots, so the preserved second relation is the capacity."}
{"id":"exclusive-generic-whole-replacement","rules":["FN-2","OP-11","ENT-5","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Whole exchange through two bare reference parameters installs each incoming value and leaves the displaced one at the other place, at a struct and at a run instantiation. swap consumes neither root [OP-11], and the run's length facts do not survive it."}
{"id":"exclusive-neg-generic-element-replacement-kills","rules":["FN-2","EFF-5","ENT-5","OP-4","OP-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A generic helper preserves the outer run length but exchanges its run element. Exact projected writes kill the caller fact about that element's own measures; the subsequent inner subscript is unproved."}
{"id":"gram2-neg-retired-behavior-law","rules":["GRAM-2","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"D7 removes law/law_arg syntax. Even a formerly recognized associative law has no declaration or unchecked trust path."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-function-signature-mismatch","rules":["FN-3","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named actual supplies Bool -> Bool for a u64 -> u64 formal member. Exact signature matching rejects the declaration even when unused."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-weaker-ensures-does-not-refine","rules":["FN-4","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The actual's proved result <= 100 promise is weaker than the interface's result <= 99 promise, so the actual ensures set cannot discharge the formal relation under FN-4's fixed implication check."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-input-owner-for-fresh-result","rules":["FN-1","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The actual returning its incoming window is admitted by ordinary signature, row and structural-contract matching; returning an owner does not duplicate it."}
{"id":"fn5-neg-ambiguous-formal-member","rules":["FN-3","FN-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Bare Reader::read cannot choose between two distinct written applications. The caller must spell Reader<A>::read or Reader<B>::read."}
{"id":"fn6-neg-growing-function-argument","rules":["FN-2","FN-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructing a specialized function argument on a recursive edge changes the complete instantiation vector and is refused before discovery."}
{"id":"run-generic-priority-behavior","rules":["FN-2","FN-3","FN-4","FN-9","OP-10","OP-11","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An owned scalar supplied to a local window contributes no enclosing effect path, while the queue's window and the environment paths retain their actual accesses; both generic instantiations still execute. The element exchange is one swap, which is the operation whose two reference arguments may name the same place."}
{"id":"run-generic-owning-map-behavior","rules":["FN-2","FN-3","FN-4","OP-10","OP-11","PROV-6","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One owning enum-slot map instantiated with stateful hash/equality, an owning key, and empty-environment hostile equality. Repeated identical keys under non-reflexive equality remain safe and all owners are cleaned up. Allocation is total, so the creation and payload wrappers have no failure arms."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-member-region-bound","rules":["FN-4","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The formal group's captured type argument fixes the instantiated member signature, and an actual whose parameter type is a different instantiation is refused at binding, before any call. The v0.59 case tested a region bound on the member; member signatures carry no generics of their own now, so the bound and its argument live on the group."}
{"id":"pre1-pos-affine-opaque-empty-drop","rules":["PRE-2","STOR-3","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An affine host handle HostString is moved to a local and then dropped in a pure function. PRE-2 and STOR-3 give a host handle the empty release; no hidden finalizer, release row or value-history effect is attributed."}
{"id":"pre1-pos-linear-opaque-ordinary-transfer","rules":["PRE-2","FN-1","OWN-1","OP-11","PROV-6","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A linear ordinary opaque ReadFile crosses a local, a function result and a swap of two owned places. Both owners are returned exactly once; ordinary ownership and reference-rooted effect paths suffice without state-routing or target summaries. This is source acceptance and invokes no linked operation."}
{"id":"view2-pos-captured-relative-and-empty-ranges","rules":["REF-4","REF-1","ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Relative endpoints select distinct initialized values, retain their captured meaning after reassignment, and admit an empty child re-slice at the parent end."}
{"id":"view2-neg-range-end-outside-source","rules":["REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference must prove its end no greater than the source length; a written end past the array length is unproved and the formation is refused."}
{"id":"view2-neg-reversed-relative-range","rules":["REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range reference must independently prove start no greater than end before its length is the difference of the endpoints; a reversed pair is refused at the formation."}
{"id":"own5-pos-recursive-adjacent-child-ranges","rules":["REF-4","OWN-7","CALL-3","EFF-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Uneven recursive partitions keep their captured endpoints through ordinary calls. Adjacent range references are proved disjoint by those endpoints and coexist, and a write reaching the range's storage leaves the origin's own length alive."}
{"id":"own10-neg-local-range-result-escape","rules":["REF-3","REF-4","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CITED RULE CHANGED at v0.60 from the retired OWN-10 to REF-3: a range reference over local backing storage is a reference like any other and dies with the binding that formed it, so it cannot be returned."}
{"id":"par2-pos-runtime-stride-range-helper","rules":["PAR-2","REF-4","OWN-7","EFF-5","PRF-1","INV-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary helper fills each runtime-width adjacent row through a range reference passed as an ordinary argument. The retained exact products and the explicit endpoint proof permit the range assignment; zero stride is also a valid empty assignment. This corpus pins acceptance and output, with permission and native actualization checked by compiler tests."}
{"id":"op4-neg-unproved-data-dependent-scatter","rules":["OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A scalar count bound does not prove the data-dependent output index is below the output length; the second scatter pass lacks the necessary relation."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-buffer-release-cycle","rules":["PROV-6","STOR-3","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A heap-owned release graph may close through a cell and a window of recursive payloads. PROV-6 admits its value-dependent release depth; the compiler derives the element-before-backing walk STOR-3 requires. Native compiler tests separately observe actual release order and lifetimes."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-full-use-capacity","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nonredundant finite certificate has exactly 4096 individually derived, distinct normalized use sources. Three entering bounds and their distinct weakenings yield the written coefficient sum and total slack. PRF-1 admits its full structural capacity; no timing or native-execution requirement applies. The separate compiler test checks the first overflowing entry at 4097."}
{"id":"msr5-pos-measures-through-owned-transfers","rules":["MSR-5","FN-9","SET-1","MSR-3","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A captured capacity and a zero length survive a verified whole-value result relation, nominal construction, a destructuring consume and a Some payload; each step is one of MSR-3's placements and the invariants at the end read what the first step published. The checks are source obligations; existing container programs own actual append and read behavior."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-unpublished-element-capacity","rules":["INV-1","FN-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified helper exports changes to the pool but no capacity relation for the runtime-capacity window it returns. Its caller cannot invent a four-slot capacity from the cell originally stored in that slot, so the invariant over the returned Box content is the ordinary unproved target."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-pool-static-and-boxed-capacity","rules":["ENT-2","FN-9","MSR-5","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Slots capacity follows its static extent across a nominal pool helper and a boxed pool helper; the pool's own length is carried by the helpers' declared exit relations over a written reference parameter [FN-9, MSR-3]. Returned initialized length is observed dynamically rather than inferred from the capacity type. This case needs no native execution."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-static-capacity-is-not-initialized-length","rules":["INV-1","ENT-2","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The constant extent of Slots<u8, 4> fixes capacity, not the initialized length of the block handed back. The helper publishes the pool's own measures and nothing about the block, and a window operation carries no measure through the slot it writes, so the caller cannot recover that the four slots are initialized even though it stocked a full window itself."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-aggregate-field-result-selector","rules":["FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-9 admits its specified whole-result forms, the explicit Result route and CALL-4's measures of owned descendants; an ordinary integer field of an aggregate result such as result.count is not a selectable result datum."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-take-does-not-preserve-length","rules":["FN-9","MSR-5","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Taking one element off the back moves the window boundary down by one. A postcondition claiming the length equals the entry length is refuted independently of the capacity or derived cap - len value."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-consume-and-drain-on-every-result-arm","rules":["PROV-6","OP-14","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A prior linear ticket is consumed on both acquisition outcomes. A second function drains every ticket from its window on both outcomes and establishes zero length at each leaving edge, which is what free_empty requires. Historical drained-run rejection labels are not retained as language expectations."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-prior-ticket-leaked-on-refusal","rules":["PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The successful acquisition arm consumes both linear tickets, but the refusal arm leaves the already-held ticket live. Correct cleanup on a different Result arm cannot discharge that leaving edge."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-local-relation-unproved","rules":["INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A body-local relation with no certificate is a proof obligation, not an assumption. Two arbitrary u8 parameters do not establish x <= limit; INV-1 rejects. The old PRF-1 comment was incorrect because no proof block exists."}
{"id":"op4-neg-post-loop-cursor-index","rules":["OP-4","ENT-5","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A general loop increments a cursor under a bound, but no invariant retains its upper bound. The exit lower bound permits subtraction; ENT-5 does not infer the missing upper bound, so OP-4 rejects the post-loop subscript."}
{"id":"op4-neg-post-loop-selected-index","rules":["OP-4","ENT-5","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A counted body conditionally saves its binder. Without a header invariant, ENT-5 kills the selected index bound on continuing writes and does not infer it after the loop; OP-4 rejects the final subscript."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-redundant-counted-bound","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The counted-loop structural fact already proves i <= count. PRF-1 checks AUTO before combining the written premise and rejects this redundant block; the weak written premise is not used to choose the verdict."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-write-range-unproved","rules":["FN-8","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary write_once signature requires ordered endpoints within the source length. This wrapper declares neither relation, so FN-8 rejects before lowering; no native IO process is necessary."}
{"id":"op4-neg-index-invariant-before-replacement","rules":["OP-4","ENT-5","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named local bound refers to the original index value. Committing 255 into the index supplies no bound against an arbitrary window length, so OP-4 rejects; ENT-5 must not reinterpret a published invariant using a new image."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-forward-proof-name","rules":["INV-1","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An invariant name resolves only after its declaration. The later declaration is unavailable at the earlier use; lexical INV-1 failure precedes a redundant-block judgment."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-literal-factor-zero","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The literal proof multiplicity must be in 2..=i128::MAX; written zero is noncanonical even though a runtime unsigned multiplicity may equal zero. The target otherwise requires a nonunit scaling."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-duplicate-scaled-use","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two uses name one normalized premise with factors two and three. PRF-1 rejects duplicate sources regardless of their multiplicities; one factor five is the canonical source."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-redundant-pair-bound","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"AUTO completes its two-distinct-premise coefficient-one family before certificate admission. The two written relations already prove this target automatically, making the entire block redundant."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-write-after-certificate","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A certificate is proved at its source position. A subsequent replacement of one operand does not retroactively invalidate that completed proof; this source makes no claim over the replacement."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-mismatched-coefficient-vector","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The written factors two/three/five do not establish the target two/two/two vector by the specified final DIRECT residual. The target may follow from other combinations, but PRF-1 performs neither subset nor multiplier search."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-certified-image-after-replacement","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A successful certificate publishes an immutable named conclusion. Replacing first then citing that conclusion cannot establish a target over the replacement image; PRF-1 must not reparse the old name against current spellings."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-redundant-mixed-bound","rules":["PRF-1","ENT-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The entering pair bound already makes this weakened mixed target an AUTO consequence. PRF-1 therefore rejects the written named/relation block as redundant. The historical finder expectation of acceptance is not normative."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-three-weighted-premises","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Three independent normalized named bounds with distinct nonunit factors combine exactly to the target; the explicit certificate lies beyond AUTO's fixed family and requires no search."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-chained-weighted-certificates","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An earlier two-factor certificate publishes its named target. A later block cites that conclusion together with a third independently scaled premise, testing lexical identity and publication across certificate steps."}
{"id":"prf1-pos-mixed-named-and-relation","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A three-premise certificate combines an earlier named invariant with two relation-form sources. All sources use the same pre-proof context and their normalized sum exactly proves the target."}
{"id":"prf1-neg-duplicate-named-and-relation","rules":["PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named premise and its relation-form spelling normalize to the same immutable inequality. PRF-1 rejects that duplicate even though the two source forms differ."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-owned-run-return-through-loop","rules":["FN-8","FN-9","INV-1","CALL-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A two-result helper returns a read byte and the window's unchanged length, exporting the length as its own result ordinal. The owner stays with the caller and the helper reaches it through a reference, because the multi-target set is retired; the counted caller names both ordinals through a destructuring let binder list and uses the length ordinal to preserve its invariant and prove the next call requirement."}
