{"id":"prov6-pos-actual-captured-bound-2","rules":["PROV-6","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every actual application must satisfy its group's written linearity bound; matching affine, ownership-closed linear and explicitly declared linear applications are distinguished. This variant matches the affine bound exactly and is accepted."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-actual-captured-bound-3","rules":["PROV-6","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every actual application must satisfy its group's written linearity bound; matching affine, ownership-closed linear and explicitly declared linear applications are distinguished. This variant applies an argument that is linear only by ownership closure and violates PROV-6."}
{"id":"fn4-pos-member-region-bound-2","rules":["FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Parameter mode and exact type agree with the instantiated formal member, so the binding is admitted before any call exists."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-member-region-bound-3","rules":["FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An actual member must match its instantiated formal even before any call; a matching one is admitted and a differing parameter mode is rejected. Here the actual takes its input by value where the member takes a reference, and a by-value parameter carries no effect entry at all."}
{"id":"form8-pos-captured-brand-before-loan","rules":["FN-4","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A formal group's captured type argument is substituted into the member signature before FN-4 compares it with the supplied actual. The reference itself carries no qualifier left to compare."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-qualified-member-specialization-1","rules":["FN-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A qualified function formal cannot discard written specialization arguments; the unspecialized qualified member is the valid control. This variant violates FN-2."}
{"id":"fn2-pos-qualified-member-specialization-2","rules":["FN-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A qualified function formal cannot discard written specialization arguments; the unspecialized qualified member is the valid control. This variant is accepted without native construction."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-formal-generic-header-1","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A formal header admits a type parameter, but a nested function-kind formal is not a permitted group constructor. This variant is accepted without native construction."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-formal-generic-header-2","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A formal header admits a type parameter, but a nested function-kind formal is not a permitted group constructor. This variant violates FN-3."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-distinct-actual-names","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Different actual names may bind the same formal and concrete type without introducing type-class coherence restrictions. This variant is accepted without native construction."}
{"id":"fn4-pos-member-effect-ordinals-1","rules":["FN-4","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Formal and actual effect paths compare parameter and field ordinals, not binder spelling; the two field paths here match ordinal for ordinal."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-member-effect-ordinals-2","rules":["FN-4","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Formal and actual effect paths compare parameter and field ordinals, not binder spelling; selecting another field is a mismatch. This variant violates FN-4."}
{"id":"eff2-pos-borrowed-length-read-effect","rules":["EFF-2","MSR-1","OP-15"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Measuring a window through a reference parameter is a place form that performs no operation and exhibits reads(values.len), a path below the declared reads(values) [OP-15, EFF-2]; the guarded element read beside it exhibits reads(values) itself, and the declared row covers both. Accepted without native construction."}
{"id":"eff2-pos-forwarded-storage-effect","rules":["EFF-2","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Forwarding a written reference parameter preserves its effect identity: EFF-5 substitutes the actual path and the forwarded row is the same one. writes(out) states every access at or below out, so reads(out) is never written beside it."}
{"id":"own13-pos-arm-scoped-payload-borrows-1","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Matching through a reference derives a payload binder naming the scrutinee path extended by its payload step, and reading that copy payload leaves the enum live."}
{"id":"own13-pos-arm-scoped-payload-borrows-2","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Repeated matches through one reference each derive their own arm-scoped payload binder, and the scrutinee stays live between them."}
{"id":"own13-pos-arm-scoped-payload-borrows-3","rules":["OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nested match through a reference extends the payload path one step further, and each arm's refinement fact keeps its own binder valid."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-distinct-provenance-regions","rules":["FN-1","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One reference input supplies the result and the other stays unrelated. The relation is settled by value rather than by a region: the result is owned data read out of the first parameter, and the second is neither read nor written."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-directory-result-relations","rules":["FN-9","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The declared directory result bounds cross an ordinary wrapper and remain available on both Result branches. The destination is now the range-reference parameter kind."}
{"id":"op8-pos-integer-width-edges-run","rules":["OP-1","OP-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"All four unsigned widths check masked wrapping shifts, rotation, zero leading/trailing counts, full-width population, saturating multiplication and the admitted byte-swap widths. One image batches the complete width matrix."}
{"id":"own1-neg-moved-directory-source","rules":["OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Forming a reference to an enumeration owner after moving it violates ordinary liveness."}
{"id":"type5-neg-vector-as-relative-path","rules":["TYPE-5","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"relative_path takes HostString; a program-owned byte run is not that type."}
{"id":"err2-neg-directory-result-arm","rules":["ERR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ERR-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary directory result match must handle its Err as well as Ok alternative."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-write-past-source","rules":["FN-8","PRE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The ordinary write_once requirement refuses a literal end past the source range-reference length before any host transfer."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-constant-call-requirement","rules":["FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A constant argument that contradicts the callee requirement is a static caller-side FN-8 rejection."}
{"id":"op2-neg-quotient-replacement","rules":["OP-2","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing a quotient cannot transfer the old captured division image to its replacement. This variant violates OP-2."}
{"id":"op2-pos-branch-quotient-interval","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each branch's quotient by a literal two carries its S7 interval [0, 2^63 - 1]; the interval names only the bound value, so bounded give delivery carries it through the join and proves the doubled product's domain, although the branch-local division images still do not transfer."}
{"id":"op2-neg-branch-runtime-quotient-images","rules":["OP-2","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"With a runtime divisor, each branch's quotient interval does not bound the product, and the branch-local division images that would do not transfer through the join, so the joined product's domain violates OP-2."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-division-operand-replacement-1","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the captured dividend, divisor or quotient cannot retarget a previously derived quotient-product bound. This variant violates INV-1."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-division-operand-replacement-2","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the captured dividend, divisor or quotient cannot retarget a previously derived quotient-product bound. This variant violates INV-1."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-division-operand-replacement-3","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the captured dividend, divisor or quotient cannot retarget a previously derived quotient-product bound. This variant violates INV-1."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-division-operand-replacement-4","rules":["INV-1","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the captured dividend, divisor or quotient cannot retarget a previously derived quotient-product bound. This variant violates INV-1."}
{"id":"op4-neg-interposed-subscript","rules":["OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscript between ordinary read calls still requires its own proved bound. The reference parameters are unmarked and are read through deref, which is what replaced the retired lifetime-annotated reference."}
{"id":"op4-neg-accumulator-subscript","rules":["OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A counted loop bounds its index, not an unrelated accumulating subscript. The constant table is an Array, which is the const-eligible storage shape."}
{"id":"op4-pos-callee-minimum-subscript","rules":["OP-4","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An integer minimum with a constant operand is bounded by that constant under its S7 row, so the minimum with seven indexes the eight-entry constant table."}
{"id":"op4-neg-callee-minimum-past-end","rules":["OP-4","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The minimum with eight is bounded only by eight, one past the last index of the eight-entry constant table, so the subscript violates OP-4."}
{"id":"const2-pos-constant-nested-field-bound-1","rules":["CONST-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A scalar field of a nested struct constant retains its concrete value at the subscript: one is inside the two-element array, two is outside. This variant is accepted without native execution."}
{"id":"op4-neg-constant-nested-field-bound-2","rules":["OP-4","CONST-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A scalar field of a nested struct constant retains its concrete value at the subscript: one is inside the two-element array, two is outside."}
{"id":"call5-pos-view-write-measure-transport-1","rules":["CALL-5","FN-8","ENT-5","REF-4","CALL-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write through a range reference preserves a saved length image, because the projected write reaches the range's element storage and kills no measure over the origin place. An unproved prefix or a whole-window write through an ordinary reference parameter would not establish the later call requirement. This variant is accepted without native execution."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-view-write-measure-transport-2","rules":["FN-8","CALL-5","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unproved prefix does not establish the later call requirement: nothing relates the position the writing callee returned to the window's current length. This variant violates FN-8."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-view-write-measure-transport-3","rules":["FN-8","CALL-5","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unknown caller window and offset make the guarded path reachable. Its length image is hoisted above a call whose substituted row writes the whole window; that write kills the saved image, so a later branch on that stale image establishes nothing about the current length required by the next call."}
{"id":"type5-neg-function-argument-kind-1","rules":["TYPE-5","FN-2","GRAM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A written function actual cannot stand in for a type argument of a source nominal, a cell content type, or a window or array element type. This variant violates TYPE-5 at a source nominal's type argument."}
{"id":"type5-neg-function-argument-kind-2","rules":["TYPE-5","FN-2","GRAM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A written function actual cannot stand in for a type argument of a source nominal, a cell content type, or a window or array element type. This variant violates TYPE-5 at a cell's content type."}
{"id":"type5-neg-function-argument-kind-3","rules":["TYPE-5","FN-2","GRAM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A written function actual cannot stand in for a type argument of a source nominal, a cell content type, or a window or array element type. This variant violates TYPE-5 at a constant-capacity window's element type."}
{"id":"type5-neg-function-argument-kind-4","rules":["TYPE-5","FN-2","GRAM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A written function actual cannot stand in for a type argument of a source nominal, a cell content type, or a window or array element type. This variant violates TYPE-5 at a constant-capacity array's element type."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-function-argument-kind-5","rules":["FN-2","TYPE-5","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A written function actual occupies its own explicit argument-list position and cannot stand in a nominal's type-argument position. This variant violates FN-2."}
{"id":"op1-neg-function-argument-kind-6","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A table operation carries no written argument and derives its selected type from its operands, so a written function actual there is a hard error citing OP-1 whatever kind the actual is. The retired nominal storage region this case used is replaced by the surviving position where a written argument is refused."}
{"id":"eff1-neg-unused-formal-formation-1","rules":["EFF-1","FN-1","OP-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Unused formal members still obey mode/effect compatibility, unique result provenance and Boolean requirement formation. This variant violates EFF-1."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-unused-formal-formation-2","rules":["FN-1","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Formation is checked with no caller anywhere in the program: the declaration writes one result ordinal and the return writes two expressions, which FN-1 refuses at the return_stmt. The v0.59 variant put this defect in an unused formal member; a fn_sig can no longer carry an FN-1 result defect, because the grammar admits only an owned result and every result count comes from a written return."}
{"id":"op5-neg-unused-formal-formation-3","rules":["OP-5","EFF-1","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Unused formal members still obey mode/effect compatibility, unique result provenance and Boolean requirement formation. This variant violates OP-5."}
{"id":"type5-neg-constructor-call-boundary-1","rules":["TYPE-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates TYPE-5."}
{"id":"type5-neg-constructor-call-boundary-2","rules":["TYPE-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates TYPE-5."}
{"id":"type5-neg-constructor-call-boundary-3","rules":["TYPE-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates TYPE-5."}
{"id":"gram8-neg-constructor-call-boundary-4","rules":["GRAM-8","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates GRAM-8."}
{"id":"gram8-neg-constructor-call-boundary-5","rules":["GRAM-8","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates GRAM-8."}
{"id":"type5-neg-constructor-call-boundary-6","rules":["TYPE-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates TYPE-5."}
{"id":"type5-neg-constructor-call-boundary-7","rules":["TYPE-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constructor type arguments, named payload syntax and result cardinality remain distinct from factored function-call grammar. This variant violates TYPE-5."}
{"id":"fn4-neg-formal-region-position","rules":["FN-4","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Formal and actual effect rows compare the selected parameter ordinal; binder renaming cannot exchange first and second inputs. This variant violates FN-4."}
{"id":"eff2-pos-formal-row-through-instance-1","rules":["EFF-2","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The formal effect row remains exhibited through the containing instance even when its actual reads a narrower field. The wrapper declares the whole parameter the instantiated formal row exhibits, so EFF-2 agrees in both directions."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-formal-row-through-instance-2","rules":["EFF-2","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The formal effect row remains exhibited through the containing instance even when its actual reads a narrower field. The wrapper declares reads(value.left) while the instantiated formal row exhibits reads(value), which EFF-2 rejects in both directions."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-borrowed-column-write-effect","rules":["EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Deleting an exhibited write from a column function's declaration is an effect-row mismatch. This variant violates EFF-2: the row is checked both ways against the complete exhibited set and undeclared-but-exhibited is an error."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-trailing-type-argument","rules":["FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A generic call cannot supply an extra trailing type argument. This variant violates FN-2."}
{"id":"op2-neg-unproved-signed-variable-division","rules":["OP-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Signed exact division with two unconstrained runtime operands has neither its nonzero nor its minimum-overflow domain established."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-bystander-invalidated-by-call","rules":["EFF-5","REF-2","OP-10","WIN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"EFF-5: a live reference outside the call whose path has a proper prefix among the call's substituted write paths becomes invalid after the call, so remove_at, which writes the filled part and moves the boundary, kills a reference into that window."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-overlapping-substituted-write","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"EFF-5: two substituted effects on overlapping paths where at least one is a write must be proved disjoint and are otherwise a hard error at the complete call, carrying both substituted paths and the restructuring prove the two positions distinct, or pass one of them."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-partial-owner-move-overlaps-zero-trip-read","rules":["EFF-5","EFF-2","OWN-1","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An exhibited reference read remains in the row even for a zero-trip body under EFF-2. OWN-1 makes a partial affine move consume its whole root; EFF-5 compares that by-value consumption against the same owner's substituted read and rejects their overlap."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-partial-owner-move-separate-from-zero-trip-read","rules":["EFF-5","EFF-2","OWN-1","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same zero-trip reference read and partial affine consumption are admitted when the reference names a different owner: OWN-7 proves separate roots, so EFF-5 finds no read/consume overlap."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-index-endpoint-substitution","rules":["EFF-5","EFF-1","OWN-7","REF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"EFF-5: each effect path rooted at reference parameter i takes actual argument i's path and each IDENT index takes the value its own argument supplies, evaluated once at the call, so a live reference at a proved-distinct offset is not a bystander of the substituted write."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-substituted-paths-disjoint","rules":["EFF-5","EFF-1","EFF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"EFF-5: at a call the actual argument paths are substituted into the callee's row and the substituted effects are compared in pairs; two effects separated by different roots are admitted and the body is checked against its own declared row."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-one-argument-covering-read","rules":["EFF-5","EFF-1","EFF-2","OWN-7","WIN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A row reading a whole struct and writing one of its fields, and a row reading a whole window and writing its next slot and length, are callable: one argument supplies each pair of entries, whose declared paths overlap at every position, so EFF-5 does not compare them; the calls compute the stated values."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-one-argument-bystander-values","rules":["EFF-5","REF-2","OWN-7","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The bystander helpers of ref2-pos-bystander-preservation, each reading the whole array and writing element j through one argument, run on arrays whose elements hold distinct values: a reference held under a requirement, a dominating guard, a captured index reassigned to j, a join of two targets, a loop-carried alias, or a direct set write returns its element's original value after the write to element j, and the caller then reads 9 in element j."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-one-argument-index-positions","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One argument supplies reads(values[i]) and writes(values[j]), whose overlap depends on the index values, so the call compares them; passing the same index for both makes them the same element."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-covered-field-second-argument","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A row whose one argument supplies reads(stats) and writes(stats.count) still has every entry compared with the other argument's entries; passing the written field as the second argument is rejected."}
{"id":"op10-neg-place-back-full-window","rules":["OP-10","PRE-1","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-10: the window operations' signatures, rows and contracts are the prelude records, so place_back into a full window leaves its requires window.len < window.cap undischarged. OP-10 states the operation; the undischarged requirement at an ordinary call is the contract rule's rejection."}
{"id":"op10-pos-front-operations-on-ring","rules":["OP-10","PRE-1","MSR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-10: place_front and take_front admit Ring alone and shift every logical index of the ring; a ring position is a logical index and the wrap is the storage's business."}
{"id":"op10-pos-insert-remove-append-split","rules":["OP-10","PRE-1","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-10: insert_at and remove_at each shift r.filled by one memmove and move the boundary by one, insert_at filling the append slot on its way, and append and split_off each move a run of elements between two windows and move both boundaries."}
{"id":"op10-pos-place-back-take-back","rules":["OP-10","PRE-1","WIN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-10: place_back fills the append slot r.next and moves the boundary r.len up by one; take_back empties the last filled slot r.last and moves that boundary down by one. The compiler-owned window type parameter is supplied by the operand and never written."}
{"id":"op11-neg-swap-copy-place","rules":["OP-11","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-11: a swap over a copy place is a hard error at the first borrow expression, with the restructuring read the two values and assign them back."}
{"id":"op11-pos-swap-exchanges-values","rules":["OP-11","PRE-1","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-11: swap exchanges the values at two owned places of one type; neither root is consumed, both bindings remain live, and each root is left the sole owner of the value the other held, with no temporary hole or second owner at any program point."}
{"id":"op11-pos-swap-same-place-admission","rules":["OP-11","EFF-5","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-11: swap is the one operation whose two reference arguments may name the same place, so swap(first: &r[i], second: &r[j]) needs no i != j branch; that admission concerns its own two arguments and nothing else, and the ordinary caller's substituted write paths are still judged pairwise."}
{"id":"op12-neg-callee-writes-target-prefix","rules":["OP-12","EFF-5","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-12"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-12: f's declared row must not write, move out of, or free any prefix of the target place, while reading anything and writing disjoint storage is admitted; a row that does is a hard error at the complete call carrying that substituted path."}
{"id":"op12-pos-atomic-in-place-update","rules":["OP-12","SET-1","OWN-1","FN-8","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The atomic update transfers the target to the callee and commits its same-typed result with no intervening program point. Constructor syntax publishes no field-value fact, so an exact +defined branch guard establishes the callee's arithmetic-domain requirement."}
{"id":"op13-neg-runtime-capacity-size-obligation","rules":["OP-13","OP-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-13: each runtime-capacity construction carries the static allocation-size obligation over its own stored type and count, and an unbounded runtime count leaves that goal unproved. OP-13 attaches the obligation; the rejection is the obligation rule's."}
{"id":"op13-pos-construction-functions","rules":["OP-13","PRE-1","TYPE-9","STOR-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-13: the construction set is the prelude records; a window built by slots_new, ring_new, box_slots_new or box_ring_new starts empty, an Array built by array_filled has every slot holding the supplied value, and allocation is total so no construction returns a Result and none has a failure arm."}
{"id":"op13-pos-slots-from-and-into-array","rules":["OP-13","PRE-1","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-13: slots_from_array consumes a full array into a full window and slots_into_array consumes a window whose len equals its cap."}
{"id":"op14-neg-free-empty-nonempty-window","rules":["OP-14","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-14"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-14: an undischarged requires window.len == 0_u64 is a hard error at the complete call, rendering the residual, with the restructuring empty the window and establish its zero length at this point."}
{"id":"op14-pos-free-empty-boxed-window","rules":["OP-14","TYPE-9","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-14: free_empty's compiler-owned shape parameter admits a boxed runtime-capacity window and frees its cell with it; at a boxed argument the row's measure place instantiates as window.inner and the clause reads window.inner.len == 0_u64, exactly as any Box content is reached by its field inner."}
{"id":"op14-pos-free-empty-empty-window","rules":["OP-14","PROV-6","WIN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-14: free_empty(window: move r) consumes a window whose element type is linear, with the contract requires window.len == 0_u64; the type stays linear and the proof is about the runtime length."}
{"id":"op15-neg-measure-type-is-u64","rules":["OP-15","TYPE-5","TYPE-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-15: a measure read's exact type is own u64 and its value mode is exact own, so the ordinary statement-local typing check applies at each use and there are no implicit conversions. OP-15 fixes the type; the rejection is the typing rule's."}
{"id":"op15-pos-measure-read-is-a-place","rules":["OP-15","MSR-1","GRAM-5","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-15: r.len, r.cap and r.head are place-suffix field selections on a measured place and are not calls; free slots is the ordinary expression r.cap - r.len. Reading a measure performs no operation and reads descriptor storage. Through a reference parameter the helper declares those three measure paths; the local-rooted call in main exhibits nothing and stays pure."}
{"id":"ref1-neg-reference-to-reference-variable","rules":["REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-1: a reference variable names a path and is not storage of its own, so &p where p is a reference variable is a hard error at that borrow expression, with the restructuring name the path the reference names."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-index-captured-at-formation","rules":["REF-1","TYPE-7","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-1: an index expression inside a path is evaluated when the reference is formed; the path records that value, and later assignments to the variables the expression used do not change it. The reference is then read through deref."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-join-path-set","rules":["REF-1","GIVE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-1: a let binder whose initializer is a value_if every branch of which delivers a reference takes that reference kind and is itself a reference variable, and at the join its target is the union of the path sets the incoming edges may name."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-use-after-move","rules":["REF-2","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-2: a move never re-roots an existing reference, so after let w = move v the references formed from v are invalid and are not reinterpreted as references into w."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-use-after-prefix-write","rules":["REF-2","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-2: reference validity is a checked fact invalidated when a proper prefix of the reference's path is written, and using the invalid reference afterwards is a hard error carrying the invalidating event."}
{"id":"ref2-pos-content-write-keeps-validity","rules":["REF-2","REF-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-2: writing the storage at a reference's own path, or below it, is a content write and does not invalidate the reference."}
{"id":"ref3-neg-returned-reference","rules":["REF-3","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-3: references never escape, so a reference may not be returned; the violation is a hard error at the offending expression with the restructuring return an index and let the caller form the reference."}
{"id":"ref3-pos-index-result","rules":["REF-3","REF-4","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-3: a reference may be bound to a local and passed as a call argument and used within the function that received it; a function that finds something returns owned data with its bounds relation in its contract, and the caller forms the reference."}
{"id":"ref4-neg-range-over-ring","rules":["REF-4","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-4: a range reference over a Ring is a hard error at the complete place suffix, because a wrapped window is two extents and the range-reference kind has one len; the restructuring is that a ring hands out single slots."}
{"id":"ref4-pos-range-reference-and-reslice","rules":["REF-4","MSR-1","TYPE-8","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-4: ranges and re-slices retain their lengths and original storage for both scalar and affine nominal elements. Runtime checks observe the scalar lengths and replacement of an affine element through a re-sliced range. OP-4 uses the range's bound without restricting its represented element type to scalars."}
{"id":"stor7-pos-relocation-by-copying-bytes","rules":["STOR-7","TYPE-8","STOR-5"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"STOR-7: any value may be relocated by copying its bytes, because no value contains a reference; a shape moved into an aggregate and out again keeps its contents, and no judgment depends on a value's address being stable. This is the observable consequence; the sentence itself has no direct source exhibition."}
{"id":"stor8-neg-no-heap-calls-allocating-row","rules":["STOR-8","OP-13","GRAM-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"STOR-8: a program whose compilation unit carries the no-heap declaration cannot call an allocating prelude row; calling such a row is a hard error at the complete call."}
{"id":"stor8-neg-no-heap-names-box","rules":["STOR-8","GRAM-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"STOR-8: a program whose compilation unit carries the no-heap declaration cannot name Box or the runtime-capacity shapes; naming such a type is a hard error at the complete type, with the restructuring use a constant-capacity shape, or withdraw the no-heap declaration."}
{"id":"stor8-pos-allocation-is-total","rules":["STOR-8","OP-13","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"STOR-8: there is one heap and allocation is total in the source; it never returns a failure, never traps, and no allocating operation carries a Result, so an allocating construction's result is used directly with no failure arm and contributes no effect entry."}
{"id":"stor8-pos-no-heap-program","rules":["STOR-8","GRAM-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"STOR-8: a compilation unit carrying the no-heap declaration as the first item names no Box and no runtime-capacity shape and calls no allocating prelude row; a constant-capacity shape is frame-resident and needs none."}
{"id":"type2-neg-readonly-measure-write-target","rules":["TYPE-2","OP-15"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: len is a readonly field of the prelude's Slots declaration, and a path that ends at a readonly field is never a set target. Formerly type10-neg-measure-write-target; the refusal moved to TYPE-2 when the measures became declared readonly fields."}
{"id":"type10-pos-member-spellings-reserve-nothing","rules":["TYPE-10","TYPE-2","EFF-1","EFF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-10: measure and window-part spellings reserve nothing. Ordinary and nested fields named len, next and filled retain their typed field identities in value reads, exact effect rows and operand-directed window calls. The case preserves its original field/binding observations and checks the nested read and window update. Formerly type10-neg-reserved-field-name, whose FORM-3 reservation the owner deleted on 2026-09-20."}
{"id":"type10-pos-measures-are-read-only","rules":["TYPE-10","OP-15","MSR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-10: len, cap and head are read-only pseudo-fields of a measured place; free slots is the ordinary expression cap - len. A program reads the measures like fields, and only window and construction operations change them."}
{"id":"type8-pos-reference-kinds-in-parameter-position","rules":["TYPE-8","REF-4","STOR-5"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-8: a reference kind is not a value type, so &T and &[T] appear only as parameter kinds and let-bound locals and every aggregate holds only owned values. This is the observable consequence; the refusal itself has no source spelling because the type grammar has no reference production."}
{"id":"type2-neg-storage-shape-constructor-call","rules":["TYPE-2","OP-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: the storage shapes are the prelude's opaque structs, so a constructor call naming Slots is refused like every opaque struct's, with the repair to build it with a construction function. Formerly type9-neg-compiler-owned-constructor-call."}
{"id":"type9-neg-runtime-capacity-outside-box","rules":["TYPE-9","STOR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-9: a runtime-capacity form may appear only as the content of a Box and never inline in another value and never as a local binding; every other position is a hard error at the complete type, with the restructuring wrap it in a Box, or write the constant-capacity form."}
{"id":"type9-pos-constant-and-runtime-capacity-placement","rules":["TYPE-9","STOR-1","TYPE-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-9: three storage shapes, two placements each, and one cell; the constant-capacity form's capacity is the type constant and its storage is inline, while the runtime-capacity form exists only as Box content, reached through the reference with deref(cell).inner."}
{"id":"win1-neg-subscript-above-len","rules":["WIN-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-1: the subscript obligation is stated against len and never against cap or head, so an offset below cap but not below len leaves the subscript's obligation unproved. WIN-1 states the sentence; the rejection is the subscript rule's, which WIN-1 names."}
{"id":"win1-pos-array-len-equals-cap","rules":["WIN-1","MSR-1","OP-15"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-1: an Array has no window; every slot always holds a value, and its one measure len is the type constant. Its prelude declaration has no cap field, so the former len == cap comparison is gone."}
{"id":"win1-pos-window-is-len-slots","rules":["WIN-1","OP-4","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-1: a Slots window is exactly the len slots beginning at slot zero, every other slot holds nothing, and a subscript r[i] carries the obligation i < r.len."}
{"id":"win2-neg-window-part-write-target","rules":["WIN-2","TYPE-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-2: parts are vocabulary for effect rows and the overlap judgment only, so no program forms a reference to one, reads one, or writes one; the rejection of a write to a part in source position is the measures-and-parts rule's, which WIN-2 names."}
{"id":"win2-pos-part-effect-row","rules":["WIN-2","EFF-1","EFF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-2: r.next is the append slot at index r.len and the measure r.len is itself a write target; the part vocabulary is ordinary, so a user function declares the same rows place_back declares and its body is covered by them."}
{"id":"win3-neg-move-out-of-window-slot","rules":["WIN-3","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"WIN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-3: there is no take operation and no hole, so a move out of a window slot is a hard error at that place with the restructuring use take_back, remove_at, or swap."}
{"id":"win3-neg-overwrite-linear-place","rules":["WIN-3","PROV-6","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"WIN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-3: assigning over any owned place is a hard error at the target place when the old value is linear, because no operation releases a linear value."}
{"id":"win3-pos-destructuring-consume","rules":["WIN-3","OWN-1","PROV-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-3: a move out of a field consumes the whole owner; a destructuring consume binds the fields it names and covers the rest with the rest marker, whose other affine parts take their compiler-derived release."}
{"id":"win3-pos-overwrite-releases-affine","rules":["WIN-3","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"WIN-3: assigning over an owned place releases the old value when it is affine, the new value occupies the same place, and the target root is live after the commit."}
{"id":"type2-neg-box-constructor-call","rules":["TYPE-2","TYPE-9","OP-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: Box is the prelude's opaque struct, and an opaque struct's constructor entry exists to be refused; a constructor call whose leading TYPEID names an opaque struct is a hard error citing TYPE-2 at the complete call, with the repair to build it with a construction function. Box values come only from box_new, box_array_filled, box_slots_new and box_ring_new."}
{"id":"type2-neg-box-destructuring-let","rules":["TYPE-2","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: a destructuring let_stmt whose TYPEID names an opaque struct is a hard error citing TYPE-2 at the complete let_stmt; Box is such a struct, and its content is reached by the ordinary field step inner: read as b.inner when it is copy, as here, and otherwise moved out as move b.inner, unless it is a runtime-capacity content, which stays in the cell."}
{"id":"type2-pos-opaque-struct-declarations","rules":["TYPE-2","GRAM-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: a struct declaration may carry the opaque modifier, written before linear when both are present; an opaque struct has fields and no usable constructor. A unit declaring one opaque struct and one opaque linear struct, constructing neither, is otherwise ordinary and is accepted."}
{"id":"type2-neg-opaque-struct-constructor-call","rules":["TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: a source-declared opaque struct carries the same refused constructor entry the prelude's opaque structs do; a constructor call whose leading TYPEID names one is a hard error citing TYPE-2 at the complete call. No value of an opaque struct the program declares is ever formed, so the repair is to remove opaque from its declaration."}
{"id":"gram2-neg-struct-modifier-order","rules":["GRAM-2","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"GRAM-2: struct_decl is opaque? (nocopy | nodrop)? struct TYPEID generics?, so opaque precedes nodrop and nodrop opaque struct is not the grammar's order. No item arm has a matching row at the second modifier, and DIAG-1 attributes the rejection to the owning grammar rule."}
{"id":"type7-neg-deref-box-binding","rules":["TYPE-7","TYPE-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-7: deref(place) where place is not a reference, a Box included, is a hard error citing TYPE-7 at the complete place, with the restructuring a Box's content is its field inner and an owned place is named as itself."}
{"id":"type9-neg-move-runtime-capacity-content","rules":["TYPE-9","WIN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-9: a move of a runtime-capacity content is a hard error citing TYPE-9 at the complete place, with the restructuring let the Box release it at scope exit, or empty it and call free_empty(move b); the unboxing spelling let n = move b.inner is admitted only where the content is not a runtime-capacity shape."}
{"id":"type9-pos-box-content-read-write-unbox","rules":["TYPE-9","TYPE-2","SET-1","WIN-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-9: Box<T> is the prelude's opaque struct whose one field inner is its content, reached by the ordinary field step and never by deref. The case reads b.inner, writes set b.inner = v over the affine old content, and consumes the cell with let n = move b.inner, which yields the content and frees the cell; the observed content drives the exit status and the program runs to exit 0."}
{"id":"op10-pos-boxed-window-grow","rules":["OP-10","TYPE-9","OP-13","OP-15"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"OP-10: a window operation on a boxed window takes a reference to the content, so place_back and take_back are written over &b.inner, while grow is defined on Box<Slots<T>> alone and takes the cell itself, remaking the content whole and carrying the length across. The contract-visible length afterwards is the ordinary field step b.inner.len."}
{"id":"type2-pos-readonly-field-read-and-whole-replace","rules":["TYPE-2","SET-1","MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: a public readonly field is written inside its declaring module like any field; outside it the field is read like any field, an ordinary field beside it stays assignable, and a whole-value assignment replaces it together with its owner."}
{"id":"type2-neg-readonly-field-set-target","rules":["TYPE-2","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: outside its declaring module a path that ends at a readonly field is never a set target, in a source struct exactly as in a prelude one."}
{"id":"type2-neg-readonly-field-written-argument","rules":["TYPE-2","EFF-5","OP-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: outside its declaring module an argument naming a readonly field at a reference parameter whose callee row writes that parameter is refused; swap writes both parameters."}
{"id":"type2-neg-readonly-path-passes-through","rules":["TYPE-2","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"TYPE-2: outside its declaring module a set target whose path passes through a readonly field is refused as well."}
{"id":"ref4-neg-a-requirement-over-a-range-reference-is-the-ranges-length","rules":["REF-4","FN-8","MSR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"REF-4, FN-8: a requirement over deref(part).len is judged against the length of the range the actual names, never against the storage the range was formed over; judged against the owner it would admit an index outside the range and outside the array. This regression retains the range holder in the instantiated goal and refuses the out-of-range index."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-postcondition-sibling-field-write","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ENT-5 gives an established callee postcondition exactly ordinary L0 support. Writing pair.right cannot kill observed == pair.left, whether established by an ordinary read or a verified postcondition. Both functions must accept; the complete resolved holder path preserves the sibling-field distinction."}
{"id":"ent1-pos-generic-transitive-callee-postconditions","rules":["ENT-1","FN-2","FN-9","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An uninstantiated generic body still receives the symbolic judgment. Its lookup bound follows through relay's verified postcondition, which itself depends on clamp's verified postcondition; both transitive callees must contribute before the body is judged. This case requires compilation only, with no native build or execution."}
{"id":"ent1-neg-generic-transitive-callee-postconditions","rules":["ENT-1","FN-2","FN-9","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Weakening clamp's declared result bound to 8 leaves relay's <=7 postcondition unproved and unpublished. The uninstantiated generic body's sole subscript then lacks bounded < len_of(lookup) and must reject. The OP-4 verdict identifies that read without requiring diagnostic wording or native execution."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-enum-payload-contract-targets","rules":["FN-8","FN-9","ENT-2","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Different enum payload fields retain different contract operands: observing left does not prove that right has the observed value. Both fields must remain in the resolved path."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-enum-payload-contract-targets","rules":["FN-8","FN-9","ENT-2","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Observing and checking the same enum payload field preserves its full resolved path and publishes the equality needed by the subsequent call."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-enum-payload-measures-stay-distinct","rules":["FN-8","MSR-3","ENT-2","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One value-if delivers either Short(Slots<u8,4> length one) or Long(Slots<u8,8> length two) to the same match. The variants share a payload field spelling but differ in both variant identity and capacity: the Short arm discharges the exact length-one requirement and the Long arm must reject it rather than inherit Short's measure datum."}
{"id":"op9-neg-known-overflow-in-unused-schema","rules":["OP-9","ENT-1","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The stored aggregate has a fixed mathematical layout exceeding u64, independent of the unused type parameter. Its expressible OP-9 bound is count <= 0, so a contract permitting count one rejects even when the generic function is never instantiated."}
{"id":"op9-pos-unresolved-aggregate-layout","rules":["OP-9","ENT-1","FN-2","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A by-value aggregate containing an opaque type parameter has an unresolved schema layout, including after alpha-renaming through a generic relay. Concrete scalar and boxed payload instances independently establish their own allocation bound; an unresolved schema is not a known overflowing layout."}
{"id":"fn10-pos-self-transfer","rules":["FN-10","FN-6","STOR-3","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Guaranteed self transfers preserve generic scalar swaps, forwarded subranges, moved Box swaps, early release of an unreferenced Box, and an empty-release local with a live reference."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-not-return","rules":["FN-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A musttail call in a let initializer has a continuation and is refused."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-other-callee","rules":["FN-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A mutual-recursion edge is not a direct self transfer."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-local-reference","rules":["FN-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference actual rooted at a local of the replaced activation is refused."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-owned-parameter-reference","rules":["FN-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An owned parameter is current-activation storage and cannot root a tail reference actual."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-joined-reference","rules":["FN-10","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every possible path of a joined reference must have an incoming-reference root; one local alternative is enough to refuse the transfer."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-pending-release","rules":["FN-10","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A live valid reference prevents moving a Box owner release before the transfer."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-operation","rules":["FN-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The marker does not make a table operation a direct self call."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-requires","rules":["FN-10","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A tail-transfer marker cannot bypass the recursive callee requirement."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-position-named-arguments","rules":["FN-10","GRAM-11","DIAG-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"At one marked call outside return, the earlier named-argument rule takes precedence over the tail-position failure."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-callee-named-arguments","rules":["FN-10","GRAM-11","DIAG-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"At one marked nonself call, the earlier named-argument rule takes precedence over the direct-self failure."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-reference-requires","rules":["FN-10","FN-8","DIAG-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A failed ordinary call requirement takes precedence over an invalid local reference root at the same marked call."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-release-requires","rules":["FN-10","FN-8","DIAG-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A failed ordinary call requirement takes precedence over a referenced owner preventing early release at the same marked call."}
{"id":"fn10-neg-callee-effects","rules":["FN-10","EFF-5","DIAG-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A direct-self failure takes precedence over conflicting effects at the same marked call because FN-10 precedes EFF-5."}
{"id":"ref2-pos-selected-payload-after-match","rules":["REF-2","OWN-13","ENT-3.S15"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"An already selected payload remains usable after the selecting match exits, while the lexical refinement ends."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-wildcard-ancestor-write","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unknown-depth cursor is invalidated by a potentially destructive write through its ancestor."}
{"id":"op2-neg-wildcard-leaf-write-kills-facts","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","OP-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A primitive write preserves the cursor but kills possibly overlapping value facts."}
{"id":"op2-neg-wildcard-cover-is-not-identity","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","OP-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A common subtree cover is not equality of the selected values."}
{"id":"op11-neg-wildcard-ancestor-exchange","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","OP-11"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-11"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Exchange permits equality but cannot exchange a possible ancestor and descendant."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-wildcard-alias-content-write","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A straight-line alias shares the current target and may change its content without removing that target."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-iterative-owned-link-descent","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-13"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A loop-carried bare-pointer reference descends through Box-owned links, retaining already selected payloads across match exits."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-wildcard-primitive-write","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A primitive leaf store through the root preserves an unknown-depth selected place."}
{"id":"type2-neg-wildcard-readonly-path","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Widening retains the readonly restriction of every possible formation path outside the field's declaring module."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-wildcard-ancestor-call","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unused reference actual receives invalidation from another actuals ancestor write."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-wildcard-sibling-call-effects","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One captured cursor target retains disjoint finite sibling effects at a call."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-wildcard-independent-cursor","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An independent cursor write may destroy a previously saved target in the same subtree."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-wildcard-owner-move","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Moving an ultimate owner invalidates its descendant cursor instead of rerooting it."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-wildcard-three-holder-join","rules":["REF-1","REF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Three holders propagate a descending target and a second root to the fixed point."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-wildcard-three-holder-join","rules":["REF-1","REF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ancestor write invalidates a root reachable only after multiple holder transfers."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-wildcard-window-removal","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Removing a window slot invalidates a descendant cursor formed under that window's bound."}
{"id":"op11-pos-proved-separated-ancestors","rules":["OP-11","OWN-7","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Proved distinct array indices separate a node from a deeper target under another slot."}
{"id":"op12-neg-wildcard-ancestor-effect","rules":["REF-1","OP-12","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-12"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An atomic update row cannot write a possible ancestor of the captured descendant target."}
{"id":"ref1-pos-wildcard-range-descent","rules":["REF-1","REF-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A descendant summary retains the range kind, its captured length and typed element suffixes."}
{"id":"type2-neg-wildcard-range-readonly","rules":["REF-1","REF-4","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A readonly element field remains readonly below a widened range cursor outside its declaring module."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-drop-bounded-partial-box-consume","rules":["PROV-6","WIN-3","FN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"T: drop admits copy and affine actuals while excluding linear residuals. The generic take partially consumes Box<Inner<T>> and returns its selected Box; both concrete class substitutions produce the checked payloads."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-loop-rebound-index-disjoint","rules":["EFF-5","REF-1","OWN-7","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An explicit body rebind selects the current counted-loop index; its fresh capture and i < 2 prove separation from index 3 despite the loop-header summary."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-loop-rebound-index-may-overlap","rules":["EFF-5","REF-1","OWN-7","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fresh current-iteration reference may select index 1 in a loop over [0,2), so a write/read call against index 1 lacks separation."}
{"id":"fn10-pos-loop-summary-release","rules":["FN-10","REF-1","REF-2","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Tail-release eligibility uses the settled finite loop summary: a possible prefix write invalidates the local owner reference before the transfer, and refusals from an unfinished summary cannot reject the program."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-indexed-measure-stale-positive","rules":["ENT-5","MSR-2","ENT-4","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing a named offset kills the positive measure goal it supports. Repeating the same guard cannot make its reachable false branch contradictory to the obsolete goal; the zero-offset read of the newly selected row remains unproved and OP-4 rejects."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-indexed-measure-stale-negative","rules":["ENT-5","MSR-2","ENT-4","EFF-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A callee write projected onto a named offset kills the negative measure goal it supports. The repeated zero-length guard may then be true, and its empty-row read must reject under OP-4 instead of inheriting a contradiction from the obsolete negative goal."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-indexed-measure-stable-controls","rules":["ENT-5","MSR-2","ENT-4","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Both signs of indexed measure goals survive an unchanged named offset, a write to an unrelated binding, and a binding write when the measured offset is a literal. Opposite branches of repeated guards remain contradictory under ENT-4, preserving their ordinary proof behavior."}
{"id":"prov6-pos-discard-copy-affine-results","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An expression statement may discard a copy or affine call result: both have drop capability, including a nocopy fieldless struct whose derived release is empty."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-discard-linear-result","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Discarding a nodrop call result abandons a linear value. Its empty runtime representation does not grant drop capability, so the expression statement must reject under PROV-6."}
{"id":"prov6-neg-runtime-array-fill-noncopy-nested-element","rules":["PROV-6","OWN-1","OP-13"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PROV-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"box_array_filled requires a copy element type even at count zero; Array<Box<u64>, 2> inherits its Box elements' noncopy capability and must fail the constructor's bound."}
{"id":"ref2-pos-bystander-preservation","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","EFF-5","ENT-5","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference outside a call's actuals survives a symbolically disjoint ancestor write under a requirement or dominating guard. Joined references preserve every possible target; captured indices survive reassignment; loop-header alias uses require backedge preservation. An unused reference needs no preservation. Direct set writes use the same separation, including a target index captured before its RHS helper changes the scalar binding. The entry calls every helper whose row reads the whole array and writes one element: one argument supplies both entries, whose overlap holds at every index, so EFF-5 does not compare them."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-joined-target","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One incoming target is disjoint from the helper write, but the other names a field of the replaced element. Every member of the joined target set must remain valid; preserving only the first cannot admit the later use."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-stale-capture","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The selected index captures j before its source binding is assigned zero. The helper replaces element j, invalidating the selected field; the new source value cannot retarget the reference or establish separation."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-escaped-guard","rules":["REF-2","OWN-7","ENT-5","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The i < j fact exists only inside a completed branch. A helper write after the join cannot use that fact to preserve a possibly overlapping field reference."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-late-guard","rules":["REF-2","OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The helper's element replacement has no entering separation proof. A later i < j guard cannot restore the invalidated field reference; validity requires a new formation."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-loop-backedge","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The alias read precedes the helper write in source order but is reached again after that write on the next iteration. Without index separation the backedge invalidates the loop-header use."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-late-reformation","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Reforming a reference after its invalid use creates a new validity fact but cannot cancel the already reached use obligation following the helper's potentially overlapping ancestor write."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-direct-write","rules":["REF-2","OWN-7","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A direct set replaces element j while a field reference selects element i. Individual bounds do not prove separation, so the later field use is invalid just as it is after the corresponding helper call."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-bystander-rhs-capture","rules":["REF-1","REF-2","OWN-7","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The direct set captures element zero before its RHS helper changes the target-index binding to one. The commit still replaces element zero and invalidates its selected field; rereading the changed index must not manufacture separation."}
{"id":"inv1-pos-operation-and-mode-proof-names","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-6","INV-1","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Operation and mode spellings are admitted in the proof-only invariant domain: a header named cvt and a body invariant named checked each resolve in a nonunit certificate. A cvt call still selects the operation family independently."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-operation-is-not-a-proof-name","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-6","INV-1","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The dotless cvt operation family does not provide an invariant declaration for a named certificate premise. Exempting invariant declarations from operation-name reservation does not merge their lookup domains."}
{"id":"inv1-neg-expired-operation-proof-name","rules":["TYPE-6","INV-1","PRF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"INV-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An admitted header invariant named cvt expires on loop exit. Its published conclusion may survive exhaustion, but a later named premise cannot use the expired declaration or resolve to the operation family."}
{"id":"form3-neg-counted-operation-name","rules":["OP-1","FORM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A counted-loop value binder is included in OP-1's closed reservation list. The dotless operation name cvt is rejected with FORM-3 in an unlabeled loop, just as in an ordinary let binding."}
{"id":"form3-neg-counted-mode-name","rules":["OP-1","FORM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FORM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The mode word checked is rejected with FORM-3 as a counted-loop value binder. A loop label does not change the binder's reservation domain."}
{"id":"form3-pos-counted-name-boundaries","rules":["OP-1","FORM-3","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Counted-loop reservation checks complete identifier spellings: cvt_index and checked_index remain ordinary value names. The label @cvt is outside OP-1's closed declaration-role list and resolves as a break target."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-result-value-transport","rules":["FN-9","CALL-4","ENT-5","ERR-3","GIVE-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Conditional integer Result evidence survives naming, copy, move, replacement, both value initializers, propagation and verified return forwarding. Runtime checks exercise success and error, both value-if branches, an unchanged loop-carried outcome, and a saved copy after its source is replaced."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-replacement","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing the whole Result through an alias removes the old success association; a later payload cannot borrow the replaced value's equality."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-support-write","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Changing a mutable operand after capture cannot re-instantiate the old call relation against the new value."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-guard-isolation","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Selecting one outcome cannot use the conditional success facts of a different outcome."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-join-bound","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A join of payload bounds less than eight and less than ten proves only their common weaker bound; the stronger requirement is rejected."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-loop-replacement","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A Result changed on a continuing backedge cannot lend the first iteration's payload equality to later iterations."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-forward-strengthening","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Forwarding a verified Result cannot strengthen the returned payload equality into a strict inequality."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-result-conditional-joins","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","MSR-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Different success bounds retain their weakest common consequence, Err alternatives are neutral under the success guard, and forwarded returns select the correct result ordinal. An element write preserves an outcome relation to the unchanged descriptor length."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-empty-forward","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Copying and joining only Err values does not manufacture a selected success exit for a routed contract."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-recursive-forward","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two forwarded Result summaries in the same recursive component cannot establish each other; forwarding supplies no body-private or cyclic summary authority."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-result-descriptor-write","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5","MSR-2","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A descriptor mutation invalidates the old outcome relation to its live length; the former size cannot be equated to the new length."}
{"id":"const2-pos-generic-struct-const","rules":["CONST-2","CONST-1","FN-2","TYPE-5","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Concrete generic struct constants prepare their exact type and integer arguments without a prior instance seed. Nested generic fields and arrays retain their static values through scalar fields and read-only references."}
{"id":"const2-neg-generic-struct-instance","rules":["CONST-2","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The constant constructor must name the declared concrete struct instance; different phantom type arguments remain distinct even with identical empty layouts."}
{"id":"const2-neg-generic-struct-window","rules":["CONST-2","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Constant eligibility follows instantiated fields. A Slots field introduced through a generic type argument is still ineligible for static constant storage."}
{"doc":"A value parameter writes its type directly. Since v0.70 an IDENT may begin a module-qualified type path [GRAM-3], so the retired own annotation starts a type path whose required :: is missing, and the rejection cites GRAM-3 at the token after own.","expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-3"},"id":"gram2-neg-own-parameter-annotation","rules":["GRAM-2","GRAM-3"],"status":"runnable"}
{"doc":"A named result binding writes only its value type. Since v0.70 an IDENT may begin a module-qualified type path [GRAM-3], so the retired own annotation starts a type path whose required :: is missing, and DIAG-1 attributes the rejection to GRAM-3 at the token after own; result names remain mandatory.","expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"GRAM-3"},"id":"form3-neg-own-result-annotation","rules":["FORM-3","GRAM-3","DIAG-1","FN-1"],"status":"runnable"}
{"doc":"Removing own from the fixed grammar atoms makes it an ordinary IDENT. A function named own is declared, called through the ordinary named-argument boundary, and returns the expected value.","expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"id":"form3-pos-own-identifier","rules":["FORM-3","GRAM-2","GRAM-3","FN-1"],"status":"runnable"}
{"id":"op6-pos-exact-domain-proofs","rules":["OP-6","ENT-3","ENT-6","FN-8","CONST-2"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Whole-type, scalar-constant, integer-range, existing mask-image, caller-requirement, sufficient integer-to-float interval and guarded-float routes discharge the same conversion domain and execute exact values."}
{"id":"op6-pos-generic-conversions","rules":["OP-6","FN-2","FN-8","TYPE-5","FORM-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Int and Float endpoints retain one interface result type through symbolic validation, forwarded generics and concrete calls. Repeated symbolic endpoints prove identity totality inside an erased definition. Typed zero/one identities and concrete constants with uniform destination-domain answers prove their generic conversions. Checked total and partial pairs share Result shape."}
{"id":"op6-pos-domain-boundaries","rules":["OP-6","OP-8","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Exact bit-value boundaries distinguish 2^24+1, maximum integer round-trip collisions and f64 2^63 from valid neighboring values. Queries and checked conversions reject invalid values; NaN canonicalization and signed-zero results remain observable."}
{"id":"op6-pos-checked-integer-evidence","rules":["OP-6","ENT-3","ENT-5","FN-9","GIVE-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Checked integer conversion captures its input before mutation, retains L0 bounds through a Result copy, and joins weaker success bounds through give and forwarded return. A widened payload indexes the original four-element range."}
{"id":"op6-neg-unproved-domain","rules":["OP-6","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unconstrained u32 parameter does not prove the u8 conversion domain. Bare cvt is rejected at its domain obligation instead of returning a Result."}
{"id":"op6-neg-inexact-constant","rules":["OP-6","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The integer 2^24+1 is not exactly representable in f32. Exact constant normalization refutes its bare conversion; the neighboring even value is covered by the positive domain-proofs case."}
{"id":"op6-neg-float-range-without-integrality","rules":["OP-6","ENT-6","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A nonconstant float's range from zero through four does not prove integrality. The conversion needs its exact domain predicate; no automatic float arithmetic theorem is introduced."}
{"id":"op6-neg-defined-stale-operand","rules":["OP-6","ENT-3","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A saved conversion-domain Bool describes the old input. Replacing that input with 300 kills the origin support and cannot authorize the new u8 conversion."}
{"id":"op6-neg-defined-wrong-destination","rules":["OP-6","ENT-2","ENT-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A u8 conversion-domain goal has a different exact destination identity from an i8 goal. Its true edge cannot prove the signed conversion."}
{"id":"op6-neg-defined-false-edge","rules":["OP-6","ENT-3","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The false edge establishes the negative conversion-domain goal and refutes the identical bare conversion in the consistent branch."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-cvt-requirement-outside-domain","rules":["FN-8","OP-6","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The caller's constant 256 is outside u8. The same exact domain normalization that judges a bare cvt refutes the callee's cvt.defined requirement at the ordinary call boundary."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-partial-cvt-definition","rules":["FN-8","OP-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bare conversion in an erased definition must be whole-type total. A separate domain requirement cannot admit a partial u32-to-u8 conversion inside that contract."}
{"id":"op6-neg-checked-float-domain-evidence","rules":["OP-6","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A checked conversion with a float endpoint supplies no opaque conversion-domain fact through Result. Its Ok payload is usable, but the tag alone does not prove a second bare conversion of the original input."}
{"id":"op4-neg-cvt-result-is-not-new-input","rules":["OP-6","ENT-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The checked payload retains the old input's bounds; it cannot retarget those bounds to a replacement input of 1000. Indexing a four-element array with that replacement is rejected."}
{"id":"op6-neg-unused-generic-domain","rules":["OP-6","FN-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The source-canonical unused generic body must prove its conversion for every Int specialization. Independent T-to-u8 endpoints are not universally total and an absent requirement cannot be guessed from callers."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-readonly-field-below-subscript-endpoint","rules":["ENT-2","ENT-3","OP-4","TYPE-2","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A readonly field below a subscript is an ENT-2 clause (b) term, directly and through a range reference: a guard establishes its bound, S11 equates the upper capture with it, and the binder bound discharges every read of another storage without a preceding let [ENT-3, OP-4]. The program observes both sums."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-readonly-field-below-subscript-requirement","rules":["FN-8","ENT-2","ENT-3","OP-4","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A requirement may name a readonly field below a subscript of a formal, the offset being another formal. The caller discharges the instantiated goal from its own guard over the same place, and S4 lets the callee use the same term as a counted endpoint that discharges every child read [ENT-2, ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-readonly-field-let-copy","rules":["ENT-3","ENT-2","OP-4","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"S5's copy equality applies to a readonly field below a subscript because that place is an ENT-2 term: the guard on the element's field bounds the let-bound copy, which then discharges every cell read as a counted endpoint."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-offset-reassigned-in-loop","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","OP-4","INV-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The offset of a subscripted readonly-field term is part of its support. Reassigning it in the outer loop body is a continuing kill, so the pre-loop bound is absent at the outer head and the inner cell read owes an unproved c < cells.len. The identical program without the reassignment is accepted."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-element-replaced","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Replacing a whole element writes a place containing its readonly field, which is the one assignment such a field admits [TYPE-2]. The bound on the old element's field dies and the endpoint's cell read owes an unproved c < cells.len."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-element-swapped","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","OP-11","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An element exchange writes both element places, so the bound on the exchanged element's readonly field dies and the endpoint's cell read owes an unproved c < cells.len."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-window-shift","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","OP-10","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"remove_at writes the window's filled part, which overlaps every element, so a bound on an element's readonly field dies even though its offset is unchanged; the shifted element's width then leaves the cell read with an unproved c < cells.len."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-callee-writes-base","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","EFF-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A call whose declared row writes the array kills every fact supported by storage below it, including a subscripted readonly-field term; the endpoint's cell read after the call owes an unproved c < cells.len."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-readonly-field-sibling-and-distinct-writes","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","OWN-7","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The kill is the ordinary overlap judgment, not a blanket one: a write to a sibling field of the same element and a replacement at a distinct literal offset overlap no storage of the readonly field, so its bound survives and the cell reads discharge."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-writable-field-below-subscript-endpoint","rules":["ENT-2","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ENT-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A subscripted place is a term only when its final step selects a readonly field. A writable field below a subscript is no clause (b) term, so as a counted endpoint it is rejected with the let-binding restructuring."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-writable-field-below-subscript-requirement","rules":["FN-8","ENT-2","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A clause admits a subscript only inside an ENT-2 clause (b) place. A writable field below a subscript names an element value, which a requirement cannot, so the clause is refused under FN-8."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-readonly-field-below-subscript-relation","rules":["FN-9","FN-8","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-8 formation admits a readonly field below a subscript, but an FN-9 relation datum is a parameter datum with field and deref projections or a measure member: a subscripted readonly field of a formal is no relation datum in this version, and the clause is refused under FN-9."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-element-offset-readonly-field-endpoint","rules":["ENT-2","OP-4","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"ENT-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every offset of a clause (b) place is itself a clause (a) or clause (c) term, because only then does a change to it kill the term [ENT-5]. An element-read offset is neither, so a readonly field below it is no term and, as a counted endpoint, is rejected under ENT-2 with the let-binding restructuring."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-element-offset-in-clause-place","rules":["FN-8","ENT-2","MSR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A clause admits a subscript only inside an ENT-2 clause (b) place. A measure place whose offset is an element read is no such place, and its offset is itself a subscript outside one, so the requirement is refused under FN-8 as a source rejection, not reported as a compiler capability as v0.70 worded it."}
{"id":"mod1-pos-ordered-graph-and-entries","rules":["MOD-1","MOD-2","MOD-5","MOD-9","PROG-1","PROG-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A graph lists modules in dependency order with named entries; the entry runs through two modules, each naming only its listed dependencies."}
{"id":"mod1-neg-later-dependency","rules":["MOD-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A dependency must be an earlier row: listing a module that only a later row registers is refused at that path, which is what keeps the graph acyclic."}
{"id":"mod1-neg-self-dependency","rules":["MOD-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A row that lists its own module as a dependency is refused at that path."}
{"id":"mod2-pos-child-directory-is-its-own-module","rules":["MOD-2","MOD-3","MOD-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A child directory with its own module.wfm is a separate module: its records belong to it alone, so its function and the parent's function of the same name are distinct."}
{"id":"mod3-pos-forward-visibility-across-records","rules":["MOD-3","TYPE-6","CONST-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Module declarations are visible in every record of the module independently of record and item order, and consts are evaluated in dependency order."}
{"id":"mod3-neg-interface-names-implementation-type","rules":["MOD-3","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An interface sees only its module's interface declarations: a public struct's private field of a type that only an implementation record declares names nothing there, the same in the module's own verdict, its interface check and its clients' verdicts."}
{"id":"mod3-pos-interface-declares-private-support","rules":["MOD-3","MOD-6"],"expect":{"exit":7,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A private support type the interface declares closes a public struct's representation, and the implementation reuses that one definition."}
{"id":"mod3-neg-module-slot-collision","rules":["MOD-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A root-module function named util and the registered module pkg::util would occupy one qualified name."}
{"id":"mod3-neg-duplicate-across-records","rules":["MOD-3","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two records of one module share one inventory, so the same function declared in both is a redeclaration."}
{"id":"mod4-pos-aliases","rules":["MOD-4","MOD-5","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"File-local aliases bind a module, a struct, a variant of a nongeneric enum, a function and a const, and every use resolves to the target's identity."}
{"id":"mod4-neg-alias-after-item","rules":["MOD-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An alias is written only in its record's initial alias header, before any other item."}
{"id":"mod4-neg-alias-case-mismatch","rules":["MOD-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A lowercase alias binds a module, function or const; a type is bound by an uppercase alias."}
{"id":"mod4-neg-alias-to-private-interface-function","rules":["MOD-4","MOD-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Of another module's declarations an alias binds only public ones: a private function its interface declares is nothing an alias can bind, as one its implementation declares would be."}
{"id":"mod4-neg-interface-alias-to-implementation-declaration","rules":["MOD-4","MOD-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An alias of an interface record sees what that record sees, so a path to a type only an implementation record of the same module declares binds nothing."}
{"id":"mod4-neg-interface-alias-collides-with-implementation-declaration","rules":["MOD-4","MOD-3","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An alias collides with every declaration of its module's inventory, so an interface alias may not take the spelling of a type only an implementation record declares, although that declaration is not visible in the interface."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-missing-edge","rules":["MOD-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The root module lists only pkg::report, so naming pkg::data is refused even though pkg::report depends on it: a transitive path grants no name."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-private-declaration","rules":["MOD-5","MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A declaration no interface publishes is private to its module, so another module naming it is refused."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-public-in-implementation","rules":["MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"public is written only in a module interface; an implementation record has no publication switch."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-public-field-of-private-type","rules":["MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A field of a private type is reachable only inside its module, so public on it publishes nothing and is refused."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-public-payload-field-of-private-enum","rules":["MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A payload field of a private enum is reachable only inside its module, so public on it publishes nothing and is refused, as on a struct field."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-public-payload-field-in-bundle","rules":["MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A source bundle publishes nothing, so public on an enum payload field is refused there as on any other member."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-readonly-without-public","rules":["MOD-6","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"readonly withholds writes from other modules, so a source field carries it only together with public; a source bundle publishes nothing."}
{"id":"mod7-neg-interface-body","rules":["MOD-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A module interface declares a function without a body; its body belongs in an implementation record."}
{"id":"mod7-neg-interface-declaration-without-doc","rules":["MOD-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An interface declares each function with the doc entry that describes it to clients; a declaration ending in a bare semicolon is refused."}
{"id":"mod7-neg-definition-differs","rules":["MOD-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-7"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A definition repeats its interface declaration's contract exactly: an added requirement is a difference, not a refinement."}
{"id":"mod7-pos-spelling-and-define-sharing-may-differ","rules":["MOD-7","FN-8"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Correspondence compares resolved identities and expands erased define sharing, so a qualified or unqualified type spelling and an inlined define correspond."}
{"id":"mod8-pos-pending-declaration-checks","rules":["MOD-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A module whose interface declares a function no implementation record defines yet still checks, and its callers use the declared boundary."}
{"id":"mod8-neg-pending-declaration-blocks-composition","rules":["MOD-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An entry composes only when every declared function has its definition; the pending declaration blocks it."}
{"id":"mod9-neg-heap-declaration-in-module","rules":["MOD-9","GRAM-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A module program states the no-heap requirement on an entry; a record writing program no_heap is refused."}
{"id":"mod9-neg-private-entry","rules":["MOD-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named entry runs a public function, and this main is private to its module."}
{"id":"stor8-neg-entry-closure-allocates","rules":["STOR-8","MOD-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A no_heap entry withdraws the heap from its execution closure, so the reached function that allocates is refused."}
{"id":"stor8-pos-unused-allocating-helper","rules":["STOR-8","MOD-9"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An allocating helper the no_heap entry never reaches is still checked but imposes no heap requirement on that entry."}
{"id":"stor8-neg-entry-closure-holds-heap-layout","rules":["STOR-8","MOD-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A function of a no_heap entry's closure allocates nothing but binds a value whose type holds a Box. Releasing that value frees heap storage, so the function introduces the heap requirement through its layout."}
{"id":"stor8-neg-recursive-allocating-component","rules":["STOR-8","MOD-9","FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The allocation sits in a recursive component that the no_heap entry reaches. The component introduces the requirement, reported at its member that allocates, rather than the walk circling the cycle and admitting the entry."}
{"id":"stor8-pos-uncalled-supplied-actual","rules":["STOR-8","MOD-9","FN-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A no_heap entry calls an instance that is supplied an allocating function-kind actual it never calls. The closure follows calls, so the uncalled actual imposes no heap requirement on the entry."}
{"id":"stor8-neg-called-allocating-actual","rules":["STOR-8","MOD-9","FN-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A no_heap entry calls an instance whose body calls its allocating function-kind actual. The actual belongs to the entry's closure and is reported as the function that introduces the requirement, with the path through the instance."}
{"id":"type6-pos-variants-belong-to-their-enum","rules":["TYPE-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Variants belong to their enum: two enums may declare the same variant names, constructions name their enum and arm labels resolve against the scrutinee's enum."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-private-type-in-public-signature","rules":["MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A public function's signature names only declarations its clients can access; a private struct in its parameter type is refused at the use."}
{"id":"mod5-pos-private-fields-stay-inside-their-module","rules":["MOD-5","MOD-6"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A private field is an ordinary field inside its declaring module; another module reads the public field and reaches the private one through an operation."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-private-field-selection","rules":["MOD-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Another module selecting a field its declaring module does not publish is refused."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-construction-with-private-field","rules":["MOD-5","GRAM-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A construction names every field, so outside the declaring module a struct with a private field cannot be constructed."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-private-field-in-contract","rules":["MOD-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One accessibility rule serves code and annotations: a contract naming a private field of another module is refused like the same selection in a body."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-private-field-in-effect-row","rules":["MOD-5","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An effect row outside the declaring module names only published fields."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-private-payload-in-arm","rules":["MOD-5","GRAM-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An arm outside the enum's declaring module binds only published payload fields; this one binds the private checksum."}
{"id":"mod5-pos-arm-covers-private-payload","rules":["MOD-5","GRAM-10"],"expect":{"exit":1,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An arm outside the enum's declaring module binds the published payload field and covers the private one with a final `..`, which binds nothing it cannot access."}
{"id":"mod5-pos-qualified-interface-and-binding-groups","rules":["MOD-5","GRAM-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"exit":7,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A binding group and an interface gparam each name another module's public generic interface by its qualified path, whose targs follow the path; the gparam's targs declare the group binder, and the member is called through the qualified group."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-qualified-private-interface-group","rules":["MOD-5","MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A qualified group names only a public interface of another module; a binding over a private one is refused."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-public-field-without-edge","rules":["MOD-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A public field is accessible only in its declaring module and the modules whose graph rows list it: a value of pkg::a reaches pkg through pkg::b, and selecting its public field there is refused."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-public-variant-without-edge","rules":["MOD-5","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An arm label is a name written in the body: matching a public enum of pkg::a in pkg, whose row lists only pkg::b, is refused at the arm."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-private-field-in-public-contract","rules":["MOD-6","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A public function's contract names only fields its clients can access, even inside the declaring module: its requirement over the private limit field is refused."}
{"id":"mod6-pos-private-contract-names-private-field","rules":["MOD-6","MOD-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A private function's contract may name its module's private fields, and a public operation over them publishes a covering effect row."}
{"id":"mod6-neg-private-field-in-public-interface-group","rules":["MOD-6","EFF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-6"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A public interface group publishes its complete formal vector, so a member's effect row names only fields its clients can access, even inside the declaring module: the private right field is refused."}
{"id":"mod6-pos-private-interface-group-names-private-field","rules":["MOD-6","MOD-5"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A private interface group is module implementation: its member's effect row may name the module's private field, and a binding in the same module supplies it."}
{"id":"mod6-pos-grow-vector-boundary","rules":["MOD-6","MOD-5","TYPE-2","CALL-4","FN-4","FN-8","FN-9","PRF-1"],"expect":{"exit":17,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A growable vector keeps its proof requirements across a module boundary. Its module publishes the backing as a public readonly field beside a public tag, with contracts and effect rows over that field and a constructor whose result projection states the empty length; a second module repeats the append boundary in a wrapper and states it again as a function-kind formal, which the wrapper fills, while the filling loop keeps its counter's relation to the backing's length through a use certificate beyond the automatic family. The root module writes the tag, drains the vector until its length is zero and frees it. Six values are pushed and drained: 6 + 6 + the tag 5 = 17."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-grow-vector-free-after-push","rules":["FN-8","FN-9","MOD-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Across the same vector boundary, the wrapper's published postcondition leaves one value in the backing, so the free operation's requirement that the length be zero is unproved at its call."}
{"id":"call3-neg-inline-range-write-kills-element-measure","rules":["CALL-3","EFF-5","ENT-5","OWN-7","REF-4","MSR-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range formed at the call is the same path as a bound range reference. The guard bounds rows[1_u64].len by cells.len, the call writes through &rows[1_u64..3_u64], and EFF-5 substitutes that formation's path, rows extended by its range step, into writes(part). Element 1 lies inside that range, so OWN-7 does not separate its range step from the index step of rows[1_u64]; CALL-3 kills the guarded element measure, and the later cells[c] read is an OP-4 rejection. The callee replaces rows[1_u64] with an eight-element run, so the stale fact would admit reads past the three cells."}
{"id":"call3-neg-inline-reslice-write-kills-element-measure","rules":["CALL-3","EFF-5","ENT-5","OWN-7","REF-4","MSR-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A re-slice formed at the call names the path its range holder names extended by the re-slice's own range step. The guard bounds deref(view)[1_u64].len, the call writes through &deref(view)[1_u64..3_u64], the index 1_u64 lies inside the inner range, whose range step therefore overlaps the index step under OWN-7, and CALL-3 kills the guarded element measure, so the later cells[c] read is an OP-4 rejection."}
{"id":"call3-pos-inline-range-write-keeps-origin-length","rules":["CALL-3","EFF-5","ENT-5","REF-4","MSR-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The survival half of CALL-3 for a range formed at the call. A write through &rows[0_u64..2_u64] reaches that range's element storage and no measure of the origin place itself, so rows.len == 3_u64 survives the call and rows[2_u64] needs no new guard; the caller reads back the empty run the callee stored in rows[0_u64]."}
{"id":"mod10-pos-host-name-free","rules":["MOD-10","PRE-2","TYPE-6"],"expect":{"exit":5,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A program declares its own function named open_read. The host function of that name is std::fs::open_read, a standard library declaration this source never names, so it enters no inventory of the program and nothing collides."}
{"id":"mod4-pos-alias-header-after-heap-declaration","rules":["MOD-4","GRAM-2","STOR-8"],"expect":{"exit":4,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A no-heap source bundle's first record opens with its heap declaration and then its alias header, which names the standard library's exit status."}
{"id":"mod10-neg-unknown-standard-module","rules":["MOD-10","MOD-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A graph row lists a std dependency that names no standard library module."}
{"id":"mod10-neg-standard-row","rules":["MOD-10","MOD-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A program's graph cannot register a module under the standard library's qualifier; only the standard library's own graph registers its modules."}
{"id":"mod10-neg-standard-entry","rules":["MOD-10","MOD-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-10"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named entry selects a function of the program's own modules; an entry path that begins with std is refused."}
{"id":"mod5-neg-standard-module-without-edge","rules":["MOD-5","MOD-10"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"MOD-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A module names std::io through an alias while its graph row lists no standard library module; the one access rule refuses the alias as it refuses a pkg module the row does not list."}
{"id":"mod10-pos-interface-names-host-type","rules":["MOD-10","MOD-6","PRE-2"],"expect":{"exit":2,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A module interface names std::io's handle types, which its row lists; the root module calls it with the invocation's handles without naming std::io, and the build supplies write_once's definition."}
{"id":"mod10-pos-library-module-with-bodies","rules":["MOD-10","MOD-8"],"expect":{"exit":42,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A module program's row lists std::collections::vector, a standard library module written in Whitefoot. The program composes with that module's bodies, which the library's implementation records supply rather than the build, and exits with the sum the vector's drain delivers."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-inline-range-callee-write","rules":["ENT-5","CALL-3","EFF-2","REF-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range formed at a call argument names the storage it was formed over extended by its range step, so a callee writing that &[T] parameter kills a readonly-field term over an element it reaches [EFF-5, CALL-3, OWN-7]. Without that kill the program reads cells past its length; the endpoint's cell read owes an unproved c < cells.len."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-inline-reslice-callee-write","rules":["ENT-5","CALL-3","EFF-2","REF-4","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A re-slice formed at a call argument names the path its holder names extended by its own range step, which OWN-7 does not separate from an index step of the same run lying inside it, so the callee's projected write kills a readonly-field term over the holder's element [CALL-3]. Without that kill the program reads cells past its length; the cell read owes an unproved c < cells.len."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-requirement-names-append-slot-element","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","WIN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A requirement forms its places at body entry in the state holding the requirements before it. There the first requirement makes i the window's length, so the third requirement's subscript owes i < deref(rows).len and the state refutes it. Admitted, the requirement would bound the element place_back then creates wide, and the loop would read cells past its length."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-requirement-readonly-field-unbounded-subscript","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The subscript of a readonly-field place in a requirement is an OP-4 occurrence judged at body entry. No earlier requirement bounds i, so the clause owes an unproved i < deref(nodes).len."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-requirement-readonly-field-bounded-by-earlier-requirement","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","ENT-3","TYPE-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A definition's place is formed in the first requirement that expands it, here the one after the bound, so the earlier requirement discharges the subscript. S4 then bounds the element's readonly count and the counted endpoint discharges every child read; the program observes the sum."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-requirement-measure-unbounded-subscript","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A measure below a subscript is a clause (b) place like a readonly field, so its subscript in a requirement owes OP-4 at body entry. With i unbounded the clause owes an unproved i < deref(rows).len."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-requirement-measure-bounded-by-earlier-requirement","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","MSR-1","ENT-3"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The second requirement forms deref(rows)[i].len after the first discharges i < deref(rows).len, and S4 gives both bounds the nested read owes; the program reads back the element."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-definition-expanded-before-bound","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A definition's place is formed in the first requirement that expands it. That requirement precedes the bound, so the subscript owes an unproved i < deref(nodes).len there; a later requirement does not reach back."}
{"id":"win2-pos-live-index-survives-appends","rules":["WIN-2","ENT-5","OWN-7","OP-10","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A window's append slot is separate from r[i] only while i < r.len is proved at the event. Each of two place_back calls proves it in its entry state, so the bound on the element's readonly count survives both and the counted endpoint discharges every child read; the program observes the sum."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-row-index-live-beside-append","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","OWN-7","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One window reaches two reference arguments, so writes(b[j]) meets writes(a.next) on it. The row position takes the argument 0, which the call's entry state proves below the window's length, so the element is live there and separate from the append slot; the program observes the append and the overwrite."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-requirement-place-offset-through-definition","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A clause place's offset spelled through a definition is the offset the definition expands to, so deref(rows)[row] after define row = i is formed as deref(rows)[i] and the earlier requirement discharges its subscript; the program observes the element."}
{"id":"ent2-pos-definition-place-offset-through-definition-chain","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A definition's place whose offset reads a chain of definitions is formed in the first requirement whose expansion reaches it, after the bound, with the offset expanded to the parameter; the program observes the element."}
{"id":"ent2-neg-requirement-place-offset-through-definition-unbounded","rules":["ENT-2","FN-8","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Expanding the definition does not hide the subscript: the place is deref(rows)[i] and no earlier requirement bounds i, so the clause owes an unproved i < deref(rows).len."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-formal-requirement-place-unbounded-subscript","rules":["FN-8","ENT-2","OP-4","FN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A formal's block has the ordinary formation rules, so an interface member's requirement forms its places in the state holding its earlier requirements. None bounds i, so the member's clause owes an unproved i < deref(rows).len with no binding or call present."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-formal-requirement-place-bounded-by-earlier-requirement","rules":["FN-8","ENT-2","OP-4","FN-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An interface member's requirement place is bounded by the member's earlier requirement, the actual's requirements follow from the formal's, and a generic caller proves both at the member call; the program observes the element."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-assigned-in-declaring-module","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","TYPE-2","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Inside its declaring module a readonly field is an ordinary field, and an assignment to it writes the requirement's own place, so the fact bounding the element's count dies there; the counted endpoint reads the new count and the child read owes an unproved c < deref(kids).len."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-readonly-field-row-writes-field","rules":["ENT-5","ENT-2","EFF-2","EFF-5","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The declaring module's function writes the readonly field itself under its row, writes(rows[i].count), so a client's call kills the requirement's fact over that field; the counted endpoint reads the new count and the child read owes an unproved c < deref(kids).len."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-later-range-lends-no-captured-endpoints","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two ranges formed from one spelling, &deref(v)[b.lo..deref(v).len], capture the same values and overlap whenever either is nonempty, so a call writing both needs an OWN-7 separation of their own captured endpoints, and none exists. An unrelated empty range formed after them captures only its own endpoints, so it separates neither and the call is an EFF-5 rejection."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-earlier-range-lends-no-captured-endpoints","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The pair of eff5-neg-later-range-lends-no-captured-endpoints with the unrelated empty range formed before them. Each formation captures its own endpoints whatever order the formations run in, so the call writing the two overlapping ranges is an EFF-5 rejection."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-field-and-measure-range-starts-overlap","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One range starts at a field read, b.lo, and the other at a measure, deref(w).len, and both end at deref(v).len, so they overlap whenever either is nonempty. Endpoints of either form are captured by their own formation, so an unrelated empty range formed after them separates neither, and the call writing both is an EFF-5 rejection."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-one-range-formation-for-two-writes","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One range reference with a field-read start passed to both writable parameters names one storage twice: its range step is the same step with the same captured endpoints, so the two substituted writes overlap. An unrelated empty range formed beside it does not separate them, and the call is an EFF-5 rejection."}
{"id":"own7-pos-ranges-meeting-at-a-measure-separate","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","REF-4","MSR-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two ranges over one parameter have only measure endpoints: [deref(u).len, deref(w).len) and [deref(w).len, deref(v).len). The first's captured end is the second's captured start, so OWN-7 proves them disjoint and the call writing both is admitted; the caller reads each write back at its own element and finds the elements between them untouched."}
{"id":"own7-pos-index-outside-range-separate","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One argument supplies reads(values[start..end]) and writes(values[slot]), which EFF-5 compares at each call. OWN-7 separates the index step from the range step when the index is proved before the range's start or at or after its end, or the range empty: the first call's written values put slot 3 at the end of 0..2, and the others are proved by their callers' contracts, hi <= at, at < lo, and hi <= lo. The program observes each run length in its slot."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-index-inside-written-range","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One argument supplies reads(values[start..end]) and writes(values[slot]); the call passes slot 1 inside the run 0..2, so no OWN-7 ordering separates the two substituted paths."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-index-beside-range-unproved","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The caller's contract bounds the slot and the run but orders neither against the other, so its call's entry state proves none of OWN-7's three index-and-range orderings and the slot may lie inside the run, although the values main passes would place it after."}
{"id":"win2-pos-range-within-length-beside-append","rules":["WIN-2","EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One argument supplies reads(window[lo..hi]) and writes(window.next), and the callee's contract does not bound hi by the window's length. A run that ends at or below window.len never overlaps window.next; the call passes hi 2 and its entry state proves window.len equal to 2, so the pair is separate. The program observes the appended run length."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-range-reaching-append-slot","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The callee of win2-pos-range-within-length-beside-append called with hi 3 while its entry state proves window.len equal to 2: the run 0..3 reaches the append slot at index 2, so reads(window[0_u64..3_u64]) is not separate from writes(window.next)."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-range-beside-filled-not-compared","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","EFF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A run of a window overlaps window.filled whatever its endpoints, as every element does, so a call does not compare reads(window[lo..hi]) with writes(window.filled) and the callee's body is checked against both. The call still compares the run with writes(window.next), which its entry state separates. The program observes the inserted run length before the two elements."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-two-arguments-range-beside-append","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","OWN-7","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One window reaches two reference arguments, so reads(source[lo..hi]) meets writes(target.next) on it. The call passes hi 2 and its entry state proves window.len equal to 2, so the run ends at the append slot's index and is separate from it. The program observes the appended run length."}
{"id":"call3-pos-range-write-beside-element-keeps-its-measure","rules":["CALL-3","EFF-5","ENT-5","OWN-7","REF-4","MSR-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The counterpart of call3-neg-inline-range-write-kills-element-measure with the range &rows[2_u64..3_u64]. Element 1 lies before that range's start, so OWN-7 separates the index step of rows[1_u64] from the range step, CALL-3 leaves the guarded measure fact alive, and the loop's cells[c] reads are proved in bounds. The program observes the refilled element and the sum over rows[1_u64]'s two elements."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-range-element-measure-survives-append","rules":["ENT-5","WIN-2","OWN-7","REF-4","MSR-2","OP-4","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fact below a range survives an append that its entry state proves the range ends before. The guard bounds deref(run)[0_u64].len by cells.len, where run is rows[0_u64..2_u64]; the place_back after it writes rows.next with rows.len proved equal to 2, so WIN-2 separates the range from the append slot, ENT-5 leaves the guarded measure fact alive, and the loop's cells[c] reads are proved in bounds. The program observes the sum over the element's two cells."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-index-reference-after-user-pop","rules":["REF-2","OP-10","WIN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference into a window dies when its bound ends. e = &r[1_u64] is formed under 1 < r.len; a call to a program function whose row writes r.last may take elements back as take_back does, so it ends that bound, and reading e afterwards is refused."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-range-reference-after-user-pop","rules":["REF-2","OP-10","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The range form of ref2-neg-index-reference-after-user-pop: part = &r[0_u64..2_u64] is formed under 2 <= r.len, and a call to a program function whose row writes r.last ends that bound, so using part afterwards is refused."}
{"id":"ref2-pos-reference-survives-user-push","rules":["REF-2","OP-10","WIN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for ref2-neg-index-reference-after-user-pop: a program function whose row writes only r.next and r.len never lowers r.len, so e = &r[0_u64] stays valid across a call to it and still reads the first element."}
{"id":"own7-pos-two-row-ranges-separate","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One argument supplies reads(values[lo..hi]) and writes(values[a..b]), two ranges the row takes from the call's arguments. The call's entry state proves the read run 0..2 ends where the written run 2..4 starts, so OWN-7 separates them; the program observes the run length in the written run's first element."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-two-row-ranges-overlap","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The callee of own7-pos-two-row-ranges-separate called with the read run 0..2 and the written run 1..4, which share element 1, so no OWN-7 ordering separates the two substituted ranges."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-index-beside-last-not-compared","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","EFF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One argument supplies reads(window[i]) and writes(window.last). An index overlaps window.last whatever its value, because one writes(window.last) covers every take_back a body makes, so the pair overlaps at every position and no call compares it; the body is checked against both. The call passes the last slot itself, and the program observes the element read and then taken."}
{"id":"eff5-pos-range-beside-last-not-compared","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","EFF-2","OP-10"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The range form of eff5-pos-index-beside-last-not-compared: reads(window[lo..hi]) beside writes(window.last) overlaps at every position, so no call compares it. The call passes a run that holds the last slot, and the program observes the run length plus the taken element."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-two-arguments-index-beside-last","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"One window reaches two arguments, reads(source[i]) and writes(target.last). The callee takes two elements back, both covered by the one writes(target.last), so target.last reaches slot 1 although 1 is below the last slot at the call's entry; an index overlaps window.last whatever its value, and the call is refused."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-two-arguments-range-beside-last","rules":["EFF-5","WIN-2","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The range form of eff5-neg-two-arguments-index-beside-last: reads(source[1_u64..2_u64]) beside writes(target.last), whose callee takes two elements back, is refused, because a range overlaps window.last whatever its endpoints."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-element-reference-after-range-frame-pop","rules":["REF-2","OP-10","REF-4","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A call on a window reached through a range frame ends the bound of a reference into that window. part = &outer[0_u64..2_u64], so deref(part)[1_u64] is outer[1_u64]; a call to a program function whose row writes its last slot ends the bound held = &outer[1_u64][0_u64] was formed under, as a call on &outer[1_u64] does, and reading held afterwards is refused."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-element-reference-after-range-row-pop","rules":["REF-2","OP-10","REF-4","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The row form of ref2-neg-element-reference-after-range-frame-pop: a callee declared writes(part[i].last), called with part: &outer[0_u64..2_u64] and i: 1_u64, writes the last slot of outer[1_u64], so held = &outer[1_u64][0_u64] is refused afterwards."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-element-reference-after-range-frame-write","rules":["REF-2","REF-4","OWN-7","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write through a range frame is a proper-prefix write of what it replaces. set deref(part)[1_u64] = move empty, with part = &outer[0_u64..2_u64], replaces outer[1_u64] whole; the written path is one step longer than outer[1_u64] but selects the same storage, so held = &outer[1_u64][0_u64] is refused afterwards."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-reference-after-user-remove","rules":["REF-2","OP-10","WIN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The filled form of ref2-neg-index-reference-after-user-pop: a program function whose row writes w.filled may remove elements as remove_at does, so a call to it ends the bound e = &w[0_u64] was formed under, and reading e afterwards is refused."}
{"id":"op10-pos-slot-reference-survives-insert-at","rules":["OP-10","REF-2","WIN-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The prelude control for ref2-neg-reference-after-user-remove: insert_at's ensures carries held = &w[1_u64]'s bound across the call and its write of w.filled is a content write, so held stays valid and names its slot, whose occupant has changed."}
{"id":"ref2-neg-element-reference-after-run-write","rules":["REF-2","REF-4","OWN-7"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"REF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write of a run is a proper-prefix write of every element it holds. A callee declared writes(part), called with part: &v[0_u64..2_u64], writes a place above v[1_u64], so e = &v[1_u64] is refused afterwards. The v0.73 checker compared path lengths and accepted this program, while it refused the same reference formed as &deref(part)[1_u64]."}
{"id":"own7-pos-ranges-below-unproved-row-indices-separate","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two ranges formed at a call, rows[i][0..2] and rows[j][2..4], have containing paths that differ only in an index step. An index is an absolute coordinate, so the paths select one row, where the ranges are ordered, or two different rows, and OWN-7 separates the ranges though nothing relates i and j."}
{"id":"own7-pos-index-and-range-below-unproved-row-indices-separate","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The index-and-range form of own7-pos-ranges-below-unproved-row-indices-separate: a row reads(rows[i][lo..hi]), writes(rows[j][k]) called with lo 0, hi 2 and k 3 separates the slot from the run though nothing relates i and j. The v0.73 checker refused the call."}
{"id":"own7-pos-row-ranges-below-unproved-row-indices-separate","rules":["OWN-7","EFF-5","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The row form of own7-pos-ranges-below-unproved-row-indices-separate: a row reads(rows[i][lo..hi]), writes(rows[j][a..b]) called with 0..2 and 2..4 separates the two runs though nothing relates i and j. The v0.73 checker refused the call."}
{"id":"eff5-neg-ranges-below-different-range-frames-overlap","rules":["EFF-5","OWN-7","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for own7-pos-ranges-below-unproved-row-indices-separate: containing paths values[a..b] and values[c..d] that differ in a range step are frames every later step is relative to, so 0..2 of one and 2..4 of the other may overlap, and the call is refused while nothing proves the frames apart."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-offset-write","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference records the index value its formation read. wr = &rows[k] is formed while k is 0, then set k = 1_u64; a fact about rows[k] for the new k says nothing of the row wr names, so the requirement 2 < deref(wr).len of a call passing wr is refused."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-reference-requirement-from-its-referent","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-offset-write: k is not written after wr = &rows[k] is formed, so the fact 3 <= rows[k].len proves the requirement 2 < deref(wr).len of a call passing wr."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-loop-offset-write","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The loop form of fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-offset-write: the body writes set k = 1_u64 after the call, and the backedge carries that write to the call's next iteration, so the requirement 2 < deref(wr).len of a call passing wr = &rows[k] is refused there as it is after a straight-line write."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-counted-offset-write","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The counted form of fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-loop-offset-write: a write of k after the call reaches the call's next iteration of a for loop as well."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-loop-exit","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The loop leaves through break after an iteration wrote set k = 1_u64, so after it a fact about rows[k] says nothing of the row wr = &rows[k] names, and the requirement 2 < deref(wr).len of a call passing wr is refused."}
{"id":"op4-neg-postcondition-through-reference-after-offset-write","rules":["OP-4","CALL-6","REF-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-4"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A postcondition over a written reference parameter is established at the actual's resolved exit place. After wr = &rows[k] and set k = 1_u64, fill(x: wr) establishes 3 <= len for the row wr names, not for rows[k], so the read rows[k][2_u64] is refused."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-conditional-offset-write","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The conditional form of fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-offset-write: set k = 1_u64 runs in one branch, so after the join a fact about rows[k] says nothing of the row wr = &rows[k] names, and the requirement 2 < deref(wr).len of a call passing wr is refused."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-reference-guard-after-conditional-offset-write","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write of k in one branch leaves wr = &rows[k] one reference to the row its formation selected, not a joined reference, so a call passing wr is instantiated at that resolved referent and the guard 3 <= deref(wr).len, a term over wr, does not prove its requirement."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-reference-requirement-offset-written-before-break","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for fn8-neg-reference-requirement-after-loop-offset-write: the loop writes k only on the path that leaves it through break, so no iteration reaches the call after the write, and 3 <= rows[k].len proves the requirement of a call passing wr = &rows[k]."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-reference-requirement-offset-written-before-outer-break","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The nested form of fn8-pos-reference-requirement-offset-written-before-break: an inner loop writes k only before break @outer, so the outer backedge never carries the write and every call through wr = &rows[k] is proved by 3 <= rows[k].len."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-reference-requirement-beside-index-superseded-before-loop","rules":["FN-8","REF-1","ENT-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A write of k before a loop supersedes the index of r2 = &rows[k] formed before it, not that of r1 = &rows[k] formed after it. The loop writes no k, so r1's index stays named by k at the header and 3 <= rows[k].len proves the requirement of a call passing r1 in every iteration."}
{"id":"set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write","rules":["SET-1","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SET-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A row evaluates its index parameter once, at the call. After set index = 0_u64 a body write deref(window)[index] is no longer the row's window[index] but a place whose nearest nameable path is window, which the row writes(window[index]) does not declare, so the set is refused. The v0.74 checker attributed it to window[index] by spelling, and the caller's reference into element 0 read the changed value."}
{"id":"set1-neg-row-index-write-after-conditional-index-parameter-write","rules":["SET-1","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SET-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The conditional form of set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write: after a join where one edge wrote index, index may not hold its call value, so deref(window)[index] is not the row's window[index]."}
{"id":"set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write-in-loop","rules":["SET-1","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SET-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The loop form of set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write: a write of index after the access reaches the access in the next iteration through the backedge."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-row-index-argument-after-index-parameter-write","rules":["EFF-2","EFF-1","EFF-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The call form of set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write: a callee's writes(window[index]) substituted with an argument index that no longer holds the caller's call value reaches window, so the caller's declared row writes(window[index]) is refused with the repair writes(window)."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-row-range-after-endpoint-parameter-write","rules":["EFF-2","EFF-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A range endpoint is an index parameter too: after set lo = 0_u64 the range deref(window)[lo..hi] starts at 0, not at the row's lo, so a call writing it reaches window and the declared row writes(window[lo..hi]) is refused with the repair writes(window)."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-reference-index-read-before-index-parameter-write","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference formed while index holds its call value names the row's window[index] after index is written, since the write does not change the element it names, so a write through it matches writes(window[index])."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-reference-index-read-before-loop-writing-index-parameter","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The loop form of eff1-pos-reference-index-read-before-index-parameter-write: a reference captured the call value of index before a loop that writes index, so every iteration's write through it is the row's window[index]."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-index-parameter-written-after-last-access","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write: index is written only after its last access, so deref(window)[index] is the row's window[index]."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-index-parameter-written-before-break","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for set1-neg-row-index-write-after-index-parameter-write-in-loop: the loop writes index only on the path that leaves it through break, so no iteration reaches the access after the write."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-range-formed-before-endpoint-parameter-write","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The range form of eff1-pos-reference-index-read-before-index-parameter-write: a range formed while lo and hi hold their call values names the row's window[lo..hi] after lo is written, so a call writing through it matches writes(window[lo..hi])."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-range-formed-before-loop-writing-endpoint-parameter","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The loop form of eff1-pos-range-formed-before-endpoint-parameter-write: a range formed before a loop that writes lo names the row's window[lo..hi] in every iteration."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-reference-index-read-before-write-on-one-edge","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","REF-1"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A reference each arm of a value if forms from the call value of index, one arm before writing index, names the row's window[index] after the join, though index may no longer hold its call value there."}
{"id":"eff1-pos-range-formed-before-endpoint-write-on-one-edge","rules":["EFF-1","EFF-2","REF-4"],"expect":{"exit":0,"kind":"run"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The range form of eff1-pos-reference-index-read-before-write-on-one-edge: both branches form part from the call values of lo and hi, one before writing lo, so a call writing through part after the join matches writes(window[lo..hi])."}
{"id":"eff2-neg-row-range-formed-after-endpoint-write-on-one-edge","rules":["EFF-2","EFF-1","REF-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"EFF-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The control for eff1-pos-range-formed-before-endpoint-write-on-one-edge: one branch rebinds part to a range formed after writing lo, so after the join part may start at 0 and a call writing through it reaches window, which writes(window[lo..hi]) does not declare."}
